* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   IDE {DICT} {file {record | CREATING.SEQKEY}}
*   IDE {DICT} file record record ...   a tab for each record named (a record that is not there is a new one)
*   IDE {DICT} file *                   the records of the file listed as a select list is, the first ten in tabs
*   IDE path:/full/name                 an operating system file (see the history: !ide_osfile)
*
*   The account is the project: the tree lists the files of the VOC (type F); Return on a file lists its
*   records (up to 2000) and its dictionary, Return on the dictionary lists its items, and Return on a record
*   opens it in the editor.  Everything the editor does with a record is the SCREEN MEMO's own; this program
*   adds what a development environment needs around it.
*
*   A record named on the command line that is not there opens as a new record (nothing is written until it
*   is saved).  With a file and no record, an active select list opens its first records in tabs.  A record
*   open in a tab is locked; a record another session has locked opens read-only.
*
*   Save writes the record, and asks first if somebody else changed it since it was read.  Compile saves,
*   runs BASIC and shows the compiler's lines in the table at the bottom; Return on one goes to its line.
*   Run, Catalogue and Debug compile first and do nothing if the compile failed.  Run and Debug give the
*   terminal back to the program and return to the IDE afterwards.  Find searches every record of a file
*   for a text and lists the lines in the same table.
*
*   Nothing here is privileged: the IDE reads and writes records and runs commands exactly as the account's
*   user could from the command prompt, so the per-account gates apply to it as they do there.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $ide
$catalog $ide

$include keyin.h
$include syscom keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include sl.h
$include tk.h
$include dictdict.h
$include idebrk.h

   deffun edit_msg(kind) calling '!edit_msg'      ;* 18 Oct 26 dm, S.66

   prompt ''
   if system(25) then stop                 ;* a phantom has no terminal

   MAXREC = 2000                           ;* records listed for a file (and read by Find in the records, and from a select list)
   MAXHIT = 500                            ;* lines a search lists
   FINDMAX = 256                           ;* the longest text Find, Replace, Query replace and List take: INDEX cuts a longer one and COUNT stops the program (29 Oct 26, review R6)
   MAXSEL = 10                             ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): records of an active select list opened in tabs at the start
   MAXLOCK = 100                           ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): tabs that can hold a record lock at one time (file variables kept in tfv)
   MAXBRK = 20                             ;* 1 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): breakpoints a program can have (the debugger's MAX.BREAKPOINTS and the kernel's list)
   IH.MAX = 20                             ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): files and records kept in /idehist/ for Open's form, each

   * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3, SED's history stacks): the files and the records opened this session, newest first, as fields (hist.push).  A named common, so that they
   *   last from one IDE to the next in the session; $ide_name reads them for Up and Down in Open's form.  A common starts as 0, so ih.on says whether it was set up.
   common /idehist/ ih.on, ih.files, ih.recs, ih.cmds
   if ih.on + 0 # 1 then
      ih.files = ''
      ih.recs = ''
      ih.cmds = ''                         ;* the commands given to Run a command, newest first (do.xeq, do.xeqterm; Up and Down in the prompt)
      ih.on = 1
   end
   LAYW = 140                              ;* what the layout is made for (the owner's 140 by 43 for every full-screen app): a bigger window is used whole, a smaller one
   LAYH = 43                               ;* is refused at the start and, if it becomes smaller while the IDE runs, the keyboard is held until it is big enough again (layout lock)

   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): IDE {DICT} {file {record | CREATING.SEQKEY}}.  A record id in quotes is taken as it is (so "creating.seqkey" is that record);
   *   the file name is lower case, as everywhere SD writes one (the name form makes it so too, and the tabs and the tree compare with it)
   * 31 Oct 26 (S.90 stage 2): a last word /form opens the dictionary item named as a FORM (do.dform) instead of as text; it is taken off the sentence here, so that no id is read from it
   * 31 Oct 26 (S.90): a dictionary item opens as the form whatever the last word (it is the default face); a last word /text asks for the text, and is taken off the same way
   sent.in = @sentence
   alone = (upcase(field(trim(sent.in), ' ', 1)) = 'SED')      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED: the editor by itself, with no files pane
   form0 = @false
   text0 = @false
   * 2 Nov 26 (S.96 M4): a last word /in=account,file comes from the master files page (tool.entries), which opens a program of ANOTHER account through a temporary pointer in
   *   SDSYS's VOC.  The file named on the sentence is that pointer; Compile and Catalog of a tab of it go through run.in into the account (its VOC, its bp.out, its catalogue,
   *   NO.CATALOG on the compile: the owner's mechanism C of 2 Nov 26), never BASIC here in SDSYS; Run and Debug of such a tab are refused (run.in has no run; debugging is M5).
   *   Taken off the sentence like /form.  The account and file names are plain words (the page checks them; they are checked again here).
   in.ptr = '' ; in.acct = '' ; in.file = ''
   in.last = field(trim(sent.in), ' ', dcount(trim(sent.in), ' '))
   if downcase(in.last[1, 4]) = '/in=' then
      sent.in = trimb(trim(sent.in)[1, len(trim(sent.in)) - len(in.last)])
      in.w = downcase(in.last[5, 999])
      in.acct = field(in.w, ',', 1)
      in.file = field(in.w, ',', 2)
      in.good = (in.acct # '' and in.file # '' and dcount(in.w, ',') = 2)
      if in.good and convert('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789._-', '', in.acct : in.file) # '' then in.good = @false
      if in.good then
         in.ptr = downcase(field(sent.in, ' ', 2))         ;* the pointer: the file the sentence names
      end else
         in.acct = '' ; in.file = ''
      end
   end
   if upcase(field(sent.in, ' ', 2)[1, 5]) # 'PATH:' and dcount(sent.in, ' ') > 2 then
      sw.last = upcase(field(sent.in, ' ', dcount(sent.in, ' ')))
      if sw.last = '/FORM' or sw.last = '/TEXT' then
         if sw.last = '/FORM' then form0 = @true else text0 = @true
         sent.in = trimb(sent.in[1, len(trimb(sent.in)) - 5])
      end
   end
   dflag0 = ''
   fn0 = field(sent.in, ' ', 2)
   id0 = field(sent.in, ' ', 3)
   if upcase(fn0) = 'DICT' then
      dflag0 = 'DICT'
      fn0 = field(sent.in, ' ', 3)
      id0 = field(sent.in, ' ', 4)
   end
   gosub sent.tokens                         ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): the words of the sentence, a text in quotes being one word, as SED takes them: a record id may hold a blank
   id0 = st.toks<(if dflag0 # '' then 4 else 3)>
   if alone then text0 = @true               ;* started as SED a dictionary item is text, as it was: the form is the IDE's
   fn0 = downcase(fn0)
   seq0 = (upcase(id0) = 'CREATING.SEQKEY')
   q0 = id0[1, 1]
   if len(id0) > 1 and (q0 = '"' or q0 = "'") and id0[len(id0), 1] = q0 then id0 = id0[2, len(id0) - 2]

   * 28 Oct 26 (stage E): IDE path:/full/name opens an operating system file.  The path is the rest of the sentence after the first word (it may hold blanks), as it was typed: it is
   *   never folded to lower case or split, and the word path: is not a file of the VOC (the file and record above are cleared, so the start is the bare one and the path opens over it)
   path0 = ''
   rest0 = field(sent.in, ' ', 2, 9999)
   if upcase(rest0[1, 5]) = 'PATH:' then
      path0 = trimf(trimb(rest0[6, 9999]))     ;* the ends only: TRIM would squeeze two blanks inside a name into one and open another file (review of 28 Oct 26, finding 5)
      pq0 = path0[1, 1]
      if len(path0) > 1 and (pq0 = '"' or pq0 = "'") and path0[len(path0), 1] = pq0 then path0 = path0[2, len(path0) - 2]
      fn0 = ''
      id0 = ''
      dflag0 = ''
      seq0 = 0
   end

   * 31 Oct 26 (S.86 stage 4, G4 of the old CED): IDE {DICT} file id1 id2 ... opens a tab for each record named (one that is not there is a new record, as for one id), and IDE {DICT} file *
   *   lists the records of the file as a select list does (the first MAXSEL in tabs).  The ids are the words of the sentence, so an id with a blank in it cannot be named here (Ctrl-O does it).
   ids0 = ''
   star0 = @false
   sel0.new = @false
   if fn0 # '' and id0 # '' and not(seq0) then
      id.base = (if dflag0 # '' then 4 else 3)
      n.tok = dcount(st.toks, @fm)
      begin case
         case id0 = '*' and n.tok <= id.base
            star0 = @true
            id0 = ''
            lr.ids = ''                                  ;* load.file leaves these alone when the file will not open
            lr.more = @false
         case n.tok > id.base
            for g4.i = id.base to n.tok
               g4.t = st.toks<g4.i>
               g4.q = g4.t[1, 1]
               if len(g4.t) > 1 and (g4.q = '"' or g4.q = "'") and g4.t[len(g4.t), 1] = g4.q then g4.t = g4.t[2, len(g4.t) - 2]
               if g4.t # '' then ids0<-1> = g4.t
            next g4.i
            id0 = ''
      end case
   end

   * The file variables through which the tabs hold their record locks: a lock belongs to the file variable it was taken through, and goes when that
   *   variable is closed, so each tab that holds one keeps its own (bx l<uid> is the slot, tused the slots in use)
   dim tfv(MAXLOCK)
   mat tfv = 0
   tused = ''

   * Ctrl-C and the terminal's break are keys of the editor; both are given back on the way out.  The setting is also kept by $tk_brk, so that an abort
   * inside the IDE (which skips every way out below) does not leave the person at the prompt with Ctrl-C dead: $sl_cleanup gives it back (28 Oct 26)
   saved.trap = pterm(PT$BREAK, -1)
   call $tk_brk('save', saved.trap)
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, @false)
   break off

   * Line-drawing characters (the frame, the drop-downs, the scroll bars) when the terminal says it is UTF-8 -
   * LC_ALL, then LC_CTYPE, then LANG as a shell resolves them - and + - | otherwise.  Only the borders change.
   loc = env('LC_ALL')
   if loc = '' then loc = env('LC_CTYPE')
   if loc = '' then loc = env('LANG')
   loc = upcase(loc)
   utf8 = (index(loc, 'UTF-8', 1) > 0 or index(loc, 'UTF8', 1) > 0)
   if loc = '' then utf8 = @true     ;* 10 Nov 26 Windows port (RELEASE_1.1 211): no locale set means UTF-8 here, as in $tk_open

   * A window smaller than the layout is refused before the screen is taken (the library's one check and message)
   call $sl_need('IDE', LAYW, LAYH, need.msg, '')
   if need.msg # '' then
      call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)        ;* the setting back and BREAK ON; nothing is held any more
      display need.msg
      stop
   end

   mouse.off = ''                    ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b): MOUSE (0) or NO.MOUSE (1) in &ide.options&, '' when it says neither; the switch itself is the session's ($tk_std)
   gosub open.screen
   if not(opened) then
      call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)
      stop
   end

   * 23 Oct 26: whether the AI is usable for this account (the gate, a provider, a model and a key): without it the IDE has no AI entry, no AI menu and the same layout as ever
   call $tk_ai('usable', '', ai.on, ai.pm)

   * 28 Oct 26 (S.88 plan 2.9, D2): whether this session keeps recovery copies (the account's switch, delivered off; kept in the person's own Linux home)
   call !edit_recovery('on', '', '', '', rc.on)
   rc.warned = 0
   RC.LOOK = 10                            ;* seconds between two looks for a copy a tab does not own (rc.tick)

   * 28 Oct 26: the bytes that make a record read-only here (128 to 247: text in another character set, or UTF-8; 248 to 255 are SD's marks, which the
   *   token form shows), and the AUTOLOGOUT countdown (idle.text reads the kernel's own inactivity clock)
   NA.CHARS = ''
   for na.i = 128 to 247
      NA.CHARS := char(na.i)
   next na.i
   IDL.WARN = 60                           ;* seconds before the sign-out that the status line says so

   * The function-key row (foot.draw): the keys the IDE acts on in do.key, most used first, so that a narrow row loses the last.  $tk_fkeys cuts it to the width.
   ide.keys = ''
   bk.first = @false
   if ibk.mode + 0 # IBK.K.LIVE then ibk.mode = IBK.K.IDLE    ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): nothing is handed to a debugger before F7 says so (an armed run left by a session that died is forgotten; the run of a debugger that has taken its hand-over, which an IDE started by the debugged program must not disturb, is not)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'esc', 'menu', 'menu', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f1', 'help', 'help', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-s', 'save', 'save', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-o', 'open', 'open', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-w', 'close', 'close', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-q', 'quit', 'quit', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f4', 'problem', 'nexterr', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f5', 'refresh', 'refresh', '', 1)        ;* 8 Nov 26 (S.179): F5 refreshes on every page; run is F9, build Shift-F9
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f6', 'pane', 'nextpane', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f7', 'debug', 'debug', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f8', 'compile', 'compile', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f9', 'run', 'run', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'shift-f9', 'build', 'catalog', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'alt-g', 'go to', 'goto', '', 1)          ;* S.179: Alt-G, as in SED (Ctrl-L redraws on every page)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'alt-r', 'macro', 'macrec', '', 1)        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): the editor's own key, listed by Ctrl-K
   ide.keys<dcount(ide.keys, @fm), TK.K.ROW> = 0
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'alt-p', 'play macro', 'macrun', '', 1)
   ide.keys<dcount(ide.keys, @fm), TK.K.ROW> = 0
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'alt-=', 'char code', 'charcode', '', 1)  ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): SED's expand char, Alt-= as in SED; not on the key row
   ide.keys<dcount(ide.keys, @fm), TK.K.ROW> = 0
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-t', 'theme', 'theme', '', 1)        ;* S.179: the theme and the keys, as on every page
   ide.keys<dcount(ide.keys, @fm), TK.K.ROW> = 0
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-k', 'keys', 'keys', '', 1)
   ide.keys<dcount(ide.keys, @fm), TK.K.ROW> = 0
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f12', 'fields', 'fields', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-n', 'new', 'new', '', 1)
   if not(alone) then call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-e', 'zoom', 'zoom', '', 1)     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): the editor fills the screen, and back; SED is on the Go menu
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-r', 'replace', 'replace', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'shift-f7', 'breakpoint', 'bptoggle', '', 1)
   * 31 Oct 26 (S.86 stage 4): the keys of the fields pane (F12), which fields.key hands to the engine ($ced_core 'key'); the pane's name is not one of the focus ring's.  Not on the
   *   function-key row (that names the pane '', so a row that names panes is left off it) and no key of the IDE's own is repeated here: F1, Ctrl-S, Ctrl-Q, F12 and the rest win over the pane
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'enter', 'open', 'dive', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'f3', 'rename', 'rename', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ins', 'insert', 'insert', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'del', 'delete', 'delete', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-c', 'copy', 'copy', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-x', 'cut', 'cut', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'ctrl-v', 'paste', 'paste', 'fields', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ide.keys, 'alt-t', 'type', 'type', 'fields', 1)       ;* S.179: Alt-T (Ctrl-T is the theme on every page; F2 is reserved for the bar's finder)
   last.foot = ''

   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   w = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   h = scr<SL.S.H> + 0

   * 2 Nov 26 (S.88 p3.7b): the site's theme (SCREENTHEME in sd.conf), as the toolkit pages, SED and the debugger have it; paper when the site names none
   *   (8 Nov 26, S.172: the delivered theme).  apply.theme falls back to paper for a name that is no theme, and to mono on a terminal with no colour.  The
   *   Theme command takes the next theme from the library ($sl_theme 'next'), in the one order every page shows them; th.want is the theme asked for, put on
   *   again when the screen is taken back.
   call $sl_theme('site', '', '', '', '', th.want)     ;* 8 Nov 26 (S.179): the person's own theme first, then SCREENTHEME
   if th.want = '' then th.want = 'paper'
   gosub apply.theme

   * The menus: one message each, entries separated by | and the parts of an entry by ~
   menus = ''
   for j = 12230 to 12235
      if j = 12231 then
         m = change(sysmsg(15019), '|', @vm)             ;* 8 Nov 26 (S.180): the Edit menu, after File - every editing key of the editor and the fields pane is on a menu
         m = change(m, '~', @sm)
         menus<-1> = m
      end
      if j = 12235 and not(alone) then
         m = change(sysmsg(15004), '|', @vm)             ;* 3 Nov 26 (S.75 DV10): the Tools menu, the development pages, before AI and Help
         m = change(m, '~', @sm)
         menus<-1> = m
      end
      if j = 12235 and ai.on then
         m = change(sysmsg(12659), '|', @vm)             ;* the AI menu, before Help
         m = change(m, '~', @sm)
         menus<-1> = m
      end
      m = change(sysmsg(j), '|', @vm)
      m = change(m, '~', @sm)
      if alone and j = 12231 then gosub alone.go.menu
      if alone and j = 12230 then gosub alone.file.menu
      menus<-1> = m
   next j

   * State
   nbuf = 0                          ;* the records open: bn (file), bi (record), bu (unique number) in step
   cb = 0                            ;* the one in the editor
   uid = 0
   bn = ''
   bi = ''
   bu = ''
   bx = collection()                 ;* s<uid> the text as it is on disk, t<uid> the text as it was left, c<uid> the cursor
   *                                   28 Oct 26 (stage B): k<uid> the kind (source, dict, voc, data), d<uid> 'DICT' or '', l<uid> the lock slot (0: no lock
   *                                   held), o<uid> the user who holds the record's lock when the tab is read-only ('' when it is not)
   fvs = collection()                ;* 30 Oct 26 (CED in the IDE, stage 2b): e<uid> the fields view's engine state ($ced_core) of a tab that shows its fields (bx v<uid> is 1)
   fv.on = @false                    ;* the current tab shows its fields (fields.is.on)
   fv.shown = @false                 ;* the form has the fields table showing and the text area hidden
   fv.pt = ''                        ;* the path line as it was last put on the form
   fl = ''                           ;* the files of the VOC
   lfiles = ''                       ;* the files whose records are listed in the tree
   lrecs = ''                        ;* their record ids, as values, in step
   lmore = ''                        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): in step: 1 when the list stopped at MAXREC
   lr.key = ''                       ;*   list.ids's list key: 'F:file' or 'D:file' for the tree, '' for a caller that wants every record
   lt.keys = ''                      ;*   the lists that have a filter or a page: the key, as fields (do.filter)
   lt.text = ''                      ;*   in step: the filter, a query clause
   lt.page = ''                      ;*   in step: the page, from 0
   ldict = ''                        ;* in step: 1 when the file has a dictionary (the tree shows a node for it)
   dfiles = ''                       ;* the files whose DICTIONARY items are listed in the tree (opened by Return on the dictionary node)
   drecs = ''                        ;* their item ids, as values, in step
   ofiles = ''                       ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): the directories of the operating system whose entries are listed in the tree ('/' is the node itself)
   orecs = ''                        ;* their entries, in step: as values, the letter D or F and then the name
   os.on = kernel(K$PATH.OK, '/') + 0   ;* the operating system node is in the tree only when this account may open pathnames outside its own directory (OS access, or SDSYS)
   or.dict = ''                      ;* open.record's dictionary flag and "a missing record is a new one": each call takes them and puts them back to ''
   or.mknew = 0
   rt.dict = ''
   out.dict = ''                     ;* the dictionary flag of the records a search or a select list put in the table
   cur.code = ''
   err.rows = ''                     ;* the table at the bottom
   err.lines = ''
   sid = ''
   sln = ''
   out.kind = ''
   out.file = ''
   al.err = @false                   ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED: 1 while the results table is shown under the editor
   zm = alone                        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): 1 while Ctrl-E has the editor filling the screen
   ra.args = ''                      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): Run with arguments: the words put after the program's name (' ' and the words), and the last ones typed
   ra.last = ''
   lst.ids = ''                      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): the select list the IDE started with (open.select): its ids as fields, its file and dictionary flag, the next
   lst.file = ''                     ;*   id Go > Next record from the list looks at, whether ids not on the disk are new records (ids typed on the command line) and
   lst.dict = ''                     ;*   whether the list was cut at MAXREC.  Never read again from list 0: the IDE used it up at the start
   lst.next = 1
   lst.mk = 0
   lst.more = @false
   syn.off = 0                      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): View > Syntax colour and View > Line numbers switched off (SED's SYNTAX OFF and LNUM OFF)
   lnum.off = 0
   focus.id = (if alone then 'ed' else 'tree')
   msg = ''
   ai.sel = ''                       ;* the AI: the selected text and a sentence about it, for the request being made
   ai.note = ''
   ai.req = ''
   sv.ok = @true
   have.form = @false
   done = @false
   untitled = 0                      ;* the number of the last untitled buffer made
   srcc.name = 'source.control'      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 4): the subroutine a save calls when it is catalogued, as in SED and ED
   srcc.active = -1                  ;* not yet looked for
   ed.macro = ''                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): the keyboard macro, kept when the form is made again
   ed.over = 0                       ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): the editor's overwrite mode, auto-indent and tab width; the form is made again on a resize, so they live here and are given to it
   ed.auto = 0
   ed.tab = 3
   pa.allow = ''                     ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): the kind path.not.for lets through for one command (do.fields asks 'coll')
   * 29 Oct 26 (stage D): the find options of Search / Find options - match case (on as delivered: how Replace has always compared) and whole words (off) - and the characters a word is made of
   fo.case = @true
   fo.word = @false
   WORDCH = '._$%'
   for wc.i = 48 to 57
      WORDCH := char(wc.i)
   next wc.i
   for wc.i = 65 to 90
      WORDCH := char(wc.i) : char(wc.i + 32)
   next wc.i
   ex.nocase = @false                ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b): the tree's records in case-blind order (NO.CASE.EXPLORE)
   gosub read.options                ;* the account's &ide.options& (or &sed.options&): before any form is made, so the editor's modes are in force from the first one
   if mouse.off # '' then call $tk_std('set', '', '', mouse.off, ms.r)     ;* MOUSE / NO.MOUSE: the session's switch, which every page and the IDE share

   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a file named with no record and an active select list (SELECT, then IDE file, as SED takes it): its ids are read here, before anything
   *   could use list 0, at most MAXREC of them and without building the rest of a list that is still being made; the list is used up, as SED's is
   sel0 = ''
   sel0.more = @false
   if fn0 # '' and id0 = '' and not(seq0) and ids0 = '' and not(star0) then
      if selectinfo(0, SL$ACTIVE) then
         sel0.n = 0
         loop
            readnext sel.id from 0 else exit
            if sel0.n >= MAXREC then
               sel0.more = @true
               exit
            end
            sel0.n += 1
            sel0<-1> = sel.id
         repeat
         clearselect 0
         * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): SED asks "use the list?" before it takes it, and so does the IDE (the screen is open and in its theme; no form yet).  No (or Esc) leaves
         *   the list unused: it is cleared as SED clears it, so that nothing run from the IDE (a compile, a command) takes it, and the file's tree opens as with no list
         if sel0 # '' then
            lq.n = sel0.n : (if sel0.more then '+' else '')
            screen msgbox sysmsg(15047, lq.n, sel0<1>) buttons 'Yes,No' default 1 returning lq.b else lq.b = 2
            if lq.b # 1 then
               sel0 = ''
               sel0.more = @false
            end
         end
      end
   end

   gosub build.files
   if fn0 # '' then
      lf.name = fn0
      gosub load.file
      cur.code = 'F:' : fn0
      if dflag0 # '' then
         ld.name = fn0
         gosub load.dict
         cur.code = 'D:' : fn0
      end
   end
   * 31 Oct 26 (stage 4, G4): the ids typed after the file name, or all the records of the file (the list load.file or load.dict made last, at most MAXREC), are opened as a select list is
   if ids0 # '' then
      sel0 = ids0
      sel0.new = @true
   end
   if star0 then
      sel0 = convert(@vm, @fm, lr.ids)
      sel0.more = lr.more
   end

   * 28 Oct 26 (S.88 Phase 4): with no record named the IDE opens an editable "Untitled 1", the focus in the editor; with only a file named the tree keeps the focus
   if id0 = '' and not(seq0) and sel0 = '' then
      gosub add.untitled
      if fn0 = '' then focus.id = 'ed'
   end

   gosub new.form
   begin case
      case fn0 # '' and seq0
         * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): CREATING.SEQKEY in the place of the record: the file's next sequential id, as SED takes it, and a new record of that id
         sq.dict = dflag0
         sq.file = fn0
         gosub seq.take
         if sq.id # '' then
            or.dict = dflag0
            or.file = fn0
            or.id = sq.id
            or.mknew = 1
            gosub open.record
         end
      case fn0 # '' and id0 # ''
         or.dict = dflag0
         or.file = fn0
         or.id = id0
         or.mknew = 1                          ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a record that is not there is a new one (it said "Cannot read record" and opened nothing)
         gosub open.record
      case sel0 # ''
         gosub open.select
   end case
   if alone and id0 = '' and not(seq0) and sel0 = '' and path0 = '' and ids0 = '' and not(star0) then gosub alone.ask
   if fn0 # '' and (id0 # '' or seq0 or sel0 # '') then
      if nbuf = 0 then
         gosub add.untitled                    ;* nothing could be opened: the editor is never left without a buffer
         gosub load.current
      end
      screen focus f, 'ed'
      focus.id = 'ed'
   end
   if path0 # '' then
      op.path = path0                          ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): the bare start's untitled buffer is taken away by the file that opens, and stays when it does not
      gosub open.path
      screen focus f, 'ed'
      focus.id = 'ed'
   end
   if id0 = '' and not(seq0) and sel0 = '' and path0 = '' then gosub rc.untitled          ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): the text of untitled buffers kept when an editor ended

   if form0 and nbuf > 0 then
      gosub redraw                             ;* 31 Oct 26 (S.90 stage 2): IDE DICT file item /form: the form is the first thing the person sees, over the IDE's own screen
      gosub do.dform
   end else
      if dflag0 = 'DICT' and id0 # '' and not(text0) then gosub dform.default      ;* 31 Oct 26 (S.90): the same without the word, unless /text asked for the text
   end

   menu.click = ''                          ;* the column of a click on the menu bar, while the menu it opens is being started
   loop
      gosub fields.sync                           ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): a tab that shows its fields agrees with its text, whoever changed the text (Reload, an AI edit)
      gosub status.line
      * A second of quiet is an event (timeout), so that the status line follows what is typed: the editor
      * consumes its own keys and nothing else returns to this loop for them
      screen wait f timeout 1 returning ev then
         et = ev<1>
         eid = ev<2>
         edata = ev<3>
         begin case
            case et = 'activate'
               msg = ''
               focus.id = eid
               gosub do.act
            case et = 'focus'
               focus.id = eid
            case et = 'change'
               if eid = 'tabs' then
                  new.cb = edata + 0
                  if new.cb >= 1 and new.cb <= nbuf and new.cb # cb then
                     gosub switch.to
                  end
               end
            case et = 'key'
               gosub do.key
            case et = 'resize'
               gosub relayout
         end case
      end else
         begin case
            case ev<1> = 'end'
               gosub rc.tick                    ;* the input ended or the terminal hung up: the changed buffers' copies are brought up to date (D2)
               done = @true
            case ev<1> = 'timeout'
               gosub rc.tick                    ;* a second of quiet: the copies follow the buffers (an abort cannot be caught)
            case 1
               gosub fields.esc         ;* Esc: up a level in the fields view when it is not at the top, else the menu
         end case
      end
   until done
   repeat

   gosub release.all                           ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): every record lock the tabs hold is given back (quit, the end of the input, a hang-up, the cursor lock)
   screen close
   call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)           ;* the setting back (or the one an earlier, aborted app left held) and BREAK ON
   display @(-1) :
   stop

* ======================================================================
* Themes

apply.theme:
   screen theme th.want returning th else
      * refused: no theme of that name (a site's sd.conf may name one that is not), or this terminal cannot show colour.  The delivered paper next, then
      * whatever the library could keep (mono on a terminal with no colour); th.want is what is in force, so that the Theme command goes on from it
      if th.want # 'paper' then
         screen theme 'paper' returning th else null
      end
      call $sl_theme('name', '', '', '', '', th.want)
   end
   return

* Out: opened.  The screen is taken, with line-drawing borders if the terminal is UTF-8
open.screen:
   opened = @false
   if utf8 then
      screen open clear lines then
         opened = @true
      end
   end else
      screen open clear then
         opened = @true
      end
   end
   if opened then call $tk_std('apply', '', '', '', ms.r)  ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b, S.186): View > Mouse off - here or on any page - holds over every screen the IDE takes back
   * The window lock (the layout mode of $sl_guardmode, 28 Oct 26): while the WINDOW is smaller than LAYW by LAYH the keys are not the IDE's, wherever its cursor is - the
   *   keyboard is held, the warning names both sizes, and Ctrl-Q then y leaves ('guardquit'); the lock lifts the moment the window is big enough
   if opened then call $sl_guardmode('IDE', LAYW, LAYH, 'layout', LAYW, LAYH)
   return

* ======================================================================
* The files of the account: the VOC entries of type F

build.files:
   fl = ''
   open 'VOC' to vocf then
      sselect vocf to 9
      readlist ids from 9 else ids = ''
      n = dcount(ids, @fm)
      if ids = '' then n = 0
      for i = 1 to n
         read r from vocf, ids<i> then
            * the first field is F, or a description that starts "File" (a file's description is kept there)
            t1 = upcase(r<1>)
            * the $ entries ($acc, $hold, $map, $release) are SD's own, not files of records (28 Oct 26, Opus's review): the tree does not list them
            if (t1 = 'F' or t1[1, 5] = 'FILE ') and ids<i>[1, 1] # '$' then fl<-1> = ids<i>
         end
      next i
   end
   return

* In: lf.name.  The records of the file go into lfiles / lrecs (or are replaced there), and ldict says whether it has a dictionary
load.file:
   open lf.name to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, lf.name)
      return
   end
   lr.key = 'F:' : lf.name             ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): this list has a filter and pages of its own (do.filter)
   lr.sent = lf.name
   gosub list.ids
   lr.key = ''
   if lr.more and lr.skip = 0 then msg = sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, lf.name)
   if lr.tmpl # '' and lr.ok then msg = sysmsg(15064, lf.name, lr.tmpl)
   ids = lr.ids
   lf.dict = 0
   open 'DICT', lf.name to lf.dv then lf.dict = 1
   lp = 0
   nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
   if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
   for q = 1 to nl
      if lfiles<q> = lf.name then
         lp = q
         exit
      end
   next q
   if lp = 0 then
      lfiles<-1> = lf.name
      lrecs<-1> = ids
      ldict<dcount(lfiles, @fm)> = lf.dict
      lmore<dcount(lfiles, @fm)> = lr.more
   end else
      lrecs<lp> = ids
      ldict<lp> = lf.dict
      lmore<lp> = lr.more
   end
   return

* In: ld.name.  The items of the file's DICTIONARY go into dfiles / drecs (or are replaced there).  28 Oct 26 (stage B): read when the dictionary node is opened, not before
load.dict:
   open 'DICT', ld.name to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, 'dict ' : ld.name)
      return
   end
   lr.key = 'D:' : ld.name
   lr.sent = 'dict ' : ld.name
   gosub list.ids
   lr.key = ''
   if lr.more then msg = sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, 'dict ' : ld.name)
   if lr.tmpl # '' and lr.ok then msg = sysmsg(15064, 'dict ' : ld.name, lr.tmpl)
   lp = 0
   nl = dcount(dfiles, @fm)
   if dfiles = '' then nl = 0
   for q = 1 to nl
      if dfiles<q> = ld.name then
         lp = q
         exit
      end
   next q
   if lp = 0 then
      dfiles<-1> = ld.name
      drecs<-1> = lr.ids
   end else
      drecs<lp> = lr.ids
   end
   return

* In: fv, an open file.  Out: lr.ids, its record ids as values, in order; lr.more, true when the file has more than MAXREC records and the list stops there.
* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): SELECT and READNEXT, stopped at MAXREC, and the list cleared - a hashed file's SELECT is made a group at a time as it is read, so a big
*   file is not read to the end (the SSELECT that was here sorted the whole file first, which hangs the IDE on a big data file).  The ids read are put in order.
list.ids:
   lr.ids = ''
   lr.keys = ''                        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b): the ids in capitals, the order when ex.nocase (NO.CASE.EXPLORE) asks for case-blind order
   lr.n = 0
   lr.more = @false
   lr.ok = @true
   lr.skip = 0
   lr.tmpl = ''
   if lr.key # '' then
      locate lr.key in lt.keys<1> setting lr.p then
         lr.tmpl = lt.text<lr.p>
         lr.skip = (lt.page<lr.p> + 0) * MAXREC
      end
   end
   if lr.tmpl # '' then
      * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): the filter, through the query processor as SED's explore list; what it prints is not for the screen.  It is sorted, so a filter reads the whole file
      execute 'sselect ' : lr.sent : ' ' : lr.tmpl : ' to 9' trapping aborts capturing lr.cap
      if @system.return.code < 0 then
         clearselect 9
         lr.ok = @false
         msg = sysmsg(15063, lr.tmpl)
         return
      end
   end else
      select fv to 9 on error
         clearselect 9
         return
      end
   end
   for lr.s = 1 to lr.skip                ;* an earlier page's records
      readnext lr.id from 9 else exit
   next lr.s
   loop
      readnext lr.id from 9 else exit
      if lr.n >= MAXREC then
         lr.more = @true
         exit
      end
      lr.n += 1
      * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b): <1,1>, the values of field 1 - a LOCATE in <1> walks the FIELDS, so the ids were never in order ('d b e a c' gave a c b e d)
      if ex.nocase then
         locate upcase(lr.id) in lr.keys<1,1> by 'AL' setting lr.p else null
         ins upcase(lr.id) before lr.keys<1, lr.p>
      end else
         locate lr.id in lr.ids<1,1> by 'AL' setting lr.p else null
      end
      ins lr.id before lr.ids<1, lr.p>
   repeat
   clearselect 9                       ;* a select left part read keeps the file from splitting and merging: it is ended here
   return

* ======================================================================
* The layout from w and h

size.layout:
   lw = w
   lh = h
   if lw < LAYW then lw = LAYW                 ;* the window is smaller only after the start: the layout keeps its size, the library cuts what falls outside
   if lh < LAYH then lh = LAYH                 ;* (the upper left corner shows) and the window lock holds the keyboard until the window is big enough
   * the rows, as the toolkit's pages have them (tk.h): the menu bar on row 0, the form from row 1, then the status line, the border and the function keys on the last three
   ystat = lh - 3
   ybord = lh - 2
   ykeys = lh - 1
   fw = lw
   fh = ystat - 1
   iw = fw - 2
   ih = fh - 2
   tw = 28
   if iw < 90 then tw = int(iw / 3)
   if tw < 14 then tw = 14
   eh = 7
   if ih < 20 then eh = 4
   ex = tw + 1
   ew = iw - tw
   mh = ih - 1 - eh
   err.y = 2 + mh
   ai.rows = (if ai.on then 1 else 0)          ;* the AI entry takes the last row of the form under the editor and the table: the table is one row shorter
   ai.y = ih
   if zm then
      * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED, al.err keeps the results table under the editor, at the place it has unzoomed
      * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): zoomed, the editor (and the tabs, the fields view and the AI line, which share its columns) take the whole inside of the form.  The
      *   files and the messages keep the places they have unzoomed, hidden (zoom.show), so that every widget is always inside the form
      ex = 1
      ew = iw
      if not(al.err) then mh = ih - 1 - ai.rows
   end
   return

* The form and everything on it, from the state
new.form:
   last.status = char(1)                ;* the screen is made again: the status line has to be written (status.line writes only a changed text), and the foot (it forgets last.foot)
   lc.nodraw = @false                   ;* see load.current
   did.focus = @false
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   w = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   h = scr<SL.S.H> + 0
   * 19 Oct 26 dm - the layout is made for the WINDOW as well as the display, like SED, CED and the debugger (a display of 200 by 60 typed with TERM on a
   *   140 by 42 window made the IDE lay out 200 by 60, which scrolled and garbled the screen): the smaller of the two.
   call $sl_window(ww0, wh0)
   if ww0 < w then w = ww0
   if wh0 < h then h = wh0
   gosub size.layout
   t = sysmsg(12204, @who)
   screen form title t at 0,1 size fw,fh returning f
   have.form = @true

   gosub tree.nodes
   screen tree f, 'tree', nodes at 1,1 size tw - 1,ih value cur.k

   * A vertical line between the tree and the editor, from the top of the form to its bottom, in the colours of the
   * form's own frame (a line-drawing character on a UTF-8 terminal, | otherwise)
   call $sl_line('v', '', vch)
   for r = 1 to ih
      screen label f, 'sep' : r, vch at tw,r width 1 attr 'window'
   next r

   * The strip of tabs on a bar of its own, as wide as the editor
   screen label f, 'tabbar', ' ' at ex,1 width ew attr 'tab'
   gosub tab.labels
   screen tabs f, 'tabs', labels at ex,1 value tab.n

   ed.text = ''
   ed.syn = ''
   ed.ro = 1
   if nbuf > 0 then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      tb.u = u
      tb.raw = bx{k}
      gosub tab.display
      ed.text = tb.disp
      k = 'o' : u
      ed.ro = (bx{k} # '')                ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): read-only while another session holds the record's lock
      k = 'z' : u
      if bx{k} then ed.ro = 1              ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E, review finding 7): or an operating system file this session may not change - load.current had it, the form made again after a resize did not
      sy.file = bn<cb>
      sy.id = bi<cb>
      if bn<cb> = 'path:' then
         sy.file = ''                      ;* (stage E) named by its last component, as load.current does
         sy.id = field(bi<cb>, '/', dcount(bi<cb>, '/'))
      end
      sy.first = field(tb.raw, @fm, 1)
      gosub syntax.for
      ed.syn = syn
   end
   if ed.ro then
      screen memo f, 'ed', ed.text at ex,2 size ew,mh syntax ed.syn lnum readonly
   end else
      screen memo f, 'ed', ed.text at ex,2 size ew,mh syntax ed.syn lnum
   end
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - a horizontal scroll bar in the editor's last row (the owner: "there is no horizontal scroll bar"); the text has one row less
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   mw{'hbar'} = 1
   mw{'passkeys'} = (if alone then '' else 'ctrl-e' : @vm) : 'ctrl-t' : @vm : 'ctrl-k'    ;* Ctrl-E zooms the editor (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 1; it handed the record to SED) (the area's own meaning of it, end of row, is End); F3 / Shift-F3 are the area's find next / previous (the owner's D31).  8 Nov 26 (S.179): Ctrl-T (the theme) and Ctrl-K (the keys) are the IDE's, as on every page; the area cuts a line with Alt-K
   mw{'syn.want'} = mw{'synlang'} + 0            ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): the tab's language, kept so that View > Syntax colour can put it back
   if syn.off then mw{'synlang'} = 0              ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): View > Syntax colour off
   if lnum.off then mw{'lnum'} = 0               ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): View > Line numbers off
   mw{'stay'} = 1                                ;* 28 Oct 26 (I3): Up on the first line and Down on the last stay in the editor and do not move the focus to the tabs or the messages
   mw{'edkeys'} = 1                              ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): Ctrl-Home / Ctrl-End (start and end of the text), Ctrl-Left / Ctrl-Right (a word) and Ctrl-Del (delete a word)
   mw{'over'} = ed.over
   mw{'autoindent'} = ed.auto
   mw{'macro'} = ed.macro                ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): the keyboard macro outlives a form made again (a resize, a program run)
   mw{'tabindent'} = ed.tab                         ;* 28 Oct 26 (approved by the owner): Tab and Shift-Tab indent and outdent here, by the codebase's 3; F6 / Shift-F6 move between the panes
   mw{'softcur'} = 1                             ;* 19 Oct 26: the character under the cursor is highlighted while the editor has the focus
   gosub bk.form                                 ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the mark column and the tab's marks

   cols = sysmsg(12226) : @sm : '6R' : @vm : sysmsg(12227) : @sm : (ew - 11) : 'L'
   * A bar of its own above the table: the table is drawn like the editor (the text style), and with nothing
   * between them the messages read as the end of the editor
   screen label f, 'errbar', sysmsg(12236) at ex,err.y width ew attr 'window'
   screen table f, 'err', cols, err.rows at ex,err.y + 1 size ew,eh - 1 - ai.rows
   if ai.on then
      * 23 Oct 26: the AI line, the last stop of the Tab ring: a label and an entry in the form's last row, Return in it is an event of its own (aw{'activate'})
      screen label f, 'ailab', sysmsg(12641) at ex,ai.y width 4 attr 'window'
      screen entry f, 'ai' at ex + 4,ai.y width ew - 4 maxlen 300
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ai', an)
      aw = f{'widgets', an}
      aw{'activate'} = 1
   end
   * 30 Oct 26 dm (CED in the IDE, stage 2a): the fields view's two widgets, in the editor's rectangle and HIDDEN until F12 (fields.run) shows them: the table of the record's fields and the
   *   line that names the container.  They are made here, with the rest of the form, so that a form made again (a resize, a program run) has them; added before the pane indicators, which stay on top.
   screen table f, 'fld', sysmsg(12138) : @sm : '10L' : @vm : sysmsg(12139) : @sm : '10L' : @vm : sysmsg(12140) : @sm : (ew - 26) : 'L', '' at ex,2 size ew,mh - 1
   screen label f, 'fpath', ' ' at ex,mh + 1 width ew attr 'window'
   screen set f, 'fld' visible 0
   screen set f, 'fpath' visible 0
   * Return in the tree and in the table is an event of its own (activate), not the press of a default button
   f{'activate'} = 1
   f{'bgclick'} = 1                     ;* a left click on no widget (the menu bar) comes back as the key mouse: the bar is the IDE's, not a widget
   f{'tabkeys'} = 1                     ;* S.176: Tab and Shift-Tab do not move between the panes (F6 / Shift-F6 do, as on every page); the editor's Tab indents

   * 19 Oct 26 dm - the owner: "a small visual indicator in the top right corner of each pane to indicate that it is active, [-] for off, [*] for on".
   *   One three-cell label to a pane that can have the focus, added last so that it is drawn over the pane's own top edge: the files on the form's top
   *   border above the tree, the editor on the border above it, the tabs at the right end of their strip, the messages at the right end of their bar.
   ind.ids = 'ixtree' : @fm : 'ixtabs' : @fm : 'ixed' : @fm : 'ixerr'
   ind.panes = 'tree' : @fm : 'tabs' : @fm : 'ed' : @fm : 'err'
   gosub indicator.texts
   screen label f, 'ixtree', ind.want<1> at tw - 4,0 width 3 attr 'window'
   screen label f, 'ixtabs', ind.want<2> at ex + ew - 3,1 width 3 attr 'tab'
   screen label f, 'ixed', ind.want<3> at ex + ew - 4,0 width 3 attr 'window'
   screen label f, 'ixerr', ind.want<4> at ex + ew - 3,err.y width 3 attr 'window'
   ind.cur = ind.want

   gosub zoom.show                      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): the files and the messages hidden while zoomed
   gosub fields.form                   ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the form was made with the text area showing; a tab that shows its fields has the view put back
   gosub tree.apply
   if nbuf > 0 then gosub memo.cursor
   screen focus f, focus.id
   gosub draw.bar
   return

* The menu bar on row 0, and the IDE's name and the person at its right end in the bar's style, as $tk_frame writes them on the toolkit pages (S.99, 1 Nov 26).  The bar is
* written whole each time, so the name is written after it, every time; the cursor is put back where the widget with the focus left it.
draw.bar:
   screen menubar menus row 0 draw else null
   db.tag = 'ide  ' : @logname : '@' : @who : ' '
   keep.cur = ''
   call $sl_curat('get', keep.cur)
   screen text db.tag at lw - len(db.tag),0 attr 'bar'
   call $sl_curat('back', keep.cur)
   return

* The nodes of the tree (a file, and under it the records of a file that is loaded), the numbers of the open ones
* and the number of the node whose code is cur.code
tree.nodes:
   nodes = ''
   open.list = ''
   cur.k = 1
   nn = 0
   nf = dcount(fl, @fm)
   if fl = '' then nf = 0
   for i = 1 to nf
      nm = fl<i>
      nn += 1
      nodes<-1> = nm : @sm : 1 : @sm : 'F:' : nm
      if 'F:' : nm = cur.code then cur.k = nn
      lp = 0
      nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
      if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
      for q = 1 to nl
         if lfiles<q> = nm then
            lp = q
            exit
         end
      next q
      if lp then
         open.list<1, -1> = nn
         * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the file's dictionary, first under the file, a node of its own; its items are under it once it has been opened
         if ldict<lp> then
            nn += 1
            nodes<-1> = sysmsg(12698) : @sm : 2 : @sm : 'D:' : nm
            if 'D:' : nm = cur.code then cur.k = nn
            dp = 0
            ndl = dcount(dfiles, @fm)
            if dfiles = '' then ndl = 0
            for q = 1 to ndl
               if dfiles<q> = nm then
                  dp = q
                  exit
               end
            next q
            if dp then
               open.list<1, -1> = nn
               dids = drecs<dp>
               ndi = dcount(dids, @vm)
               if dids = '' then ndi = 0
               for j = 1 to ndi
                  nn += 1
                  c2 = 'Q:' : nm : ':' : dids<1, j>
                  nodes<-1> = dids<1, j> : @sm : 3 : @sm : c2
                  if c2 = cur.code then cur.k = nn
               next j
            end
         end
         lt.k = 'F:' : nm
         gosub filter.find
         if lt.p then
            if lt.page<lt.p> + 0 then          ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): a list longer than MAXREC goes by pages
               nn += 1
               c2 = 'B:' : nm
               nodes<-1> = sysmsg(15062, MAXREC) : @sm : 2 : @sm : c2
               if c2 = cur.code then cur.k = nn
            end
         end
         ids = lrecs<lp>
         ni = dcount(ids, @vm)
         if ids = '' then ni = 0
         for j = 1 to ni
            nn += 1
            c2 = 'R:' : nm : ':' : ids<1, j>
            nodes<-1> = ids<1, j> : @sm : 2 : @sm : c2
            if c2 = cur.code then cur.k = nn
         next j
         if lmore<lp> then
            nn += 1
            c2 = 'N:' : nm
            nodes<-1> = sysmsg(15061, MAXREC) : @sm : 2 : @sm : c2
            if c2 = cur.code then cur.k = nn
         end
      end
   next i
   if os.on then gosub tree.os
   return

* The operating system node (30 Oct 26, stage E2), after the files of the account: a root, then the entries of every directory that is listed (os.find), a directory a node of its own
*   that opens and closes with Return (code O:path) and a file a node that opens in a tab (P:path).  The listings are depth first, each under the directory it belongs to.
tree.os:
   nn += 1
   os.rootn = nn
   nodes<-1> = sysmsg(12751) : @sm : 1 : @sm : 'O:/'
   if cur.code = 'O:/' then cur.k = nn
   os.dir = '/'
   gosub os.find
   if not(os.q) then return
   open.list<1, -1> = os.rootn
   os.sp = 1
   os.stk.dir = '/'
   os.stk.ix = 0
   os.stk.lv = 2
   loop
   while os.sp > 0
      os.dir = os.stk.dir<os.sp>
      gosub os.find
      os.ents = orecs<os.q>
      os.ne = dcount(os.ents, @vm)
      if os.ents = '' then os.ne = 0
      os.ix = os.stk.ix<os.sp> + 1
      if os.ix > os.ne then
         os.sp -= 1
      end else
         os.stk.ix<os.sp> = os.ix
         os.lv = os.stk.lv<os.sp>
         os.e = os.ents<1, os.ix>
         os.nm = os.e[2, 9999]
         if os.dir = '/' then
            os.pth = '/' : os.nm
         end else
            os.pth = os.dir : '/' : os.nm
         end
         nn += 1
         if os.e[1, 1] = 'D' then
            os.c = 'O:' : os.pth
            nodes<-1> = os.nm : '/' : @sm : os.lv : @sm : os.c
            if os.c = cur.code then cur.k = nn
            os.dir = os.pth
            gosub os.find
            if os.q then
               open.list<1, -1> = nn
               os.sp += 1
               os.stk.dir<os.sp> = os.pth
               os.stk.ix<os.sp> = 0
               os.stk.lv<os.sp> = os.lv + 1
            end
         end else
            os.c = 'P:' : os.pth
            nodes<-1> = os.nm : @sm : os.lv : @sm : os.c
            if os.c = cur.code then cur.k = nn
         end
      end
   repeat
   return

* In: os.dir.  Out: os.q, the place of that directory in ofiles (0: its entries are not listed)
os.find:
   os.q = 0
   os.nf = dcount(ofiles, @fm)
   if ofiles = '' then os.nf = 0
   for os.j = 1 to os.nf
      if ofiles<os.j> = os.dir then
         os.q = os.j
         exit
      end
   next os.j
   return

* In: os.dir.  The entries of the directory are asked of !ide_osfile 'list' (the only DIR() of the IDE; the kernel asks the account's OS gate) and put in ofiles / orecs, or replaced there.
*   Out: os.ok, and msg when the directory could not be listed or entries were left out.
os.load:
   os.ok = @false
   call !ide_osfile('list', os.dir, os.lf, os.lt, os.li, os.lw)
   if os.lw # '' then
      begin case
         case os.lw = 'name'
            msg = sysmsg(12733, os.dir)
         case os.lw = 'gate'
            if kernel(K$PATH.OK, os.dir) then
               msg = sysmsg(12604, os.dir, os.li<7>)    ;* 02 Nov 26 (S.88 A5): the account may reach it, so the operating system would not open it; not "no OS access"
            end else
               msg = sysmsg(12185, @who)
            end
         case os.lw = 'none'
            msg = sysmsg(12734, os.dir)
         case 1
            msg = sysmsg(12752, os.dir, os.li<6>)
      end case
      return
   end
   os.ents = ''
   os.nl = dcount(os.lt, @fm)
   if os.lt = '' then os.nl = 0
   for os.j = 1 to os.nl
      os.ents<1, -1> = os.lt<os.j, 2> : os.lt<os.j, 1>
   next os.j
   gosub os.find
   if os.q = 0 then
      ofiles<-1> = os.dir
      os.q = dcount(ofiles, @fm)
   end
   orecs<os.q> = os.ents
   if os.li<5> + 0 then msg = sysmsg(12753, os.dir, os.li<5>)
   os.ok = @true
   return

* In: os.dir.  The directory and every directory listed under it are taken out of the tree's lists ('/' takes them all)
os.collapse:
   os.nf = dcount(ofiles, @fm)
   if ofiles = '' then os.nf = 0
   for os.j = os.nf to 1 step -1
      os.pp = ofiles<os.j>
      if os.pp = os.dir or os.dir = '/' or os.pp[1, len(os.dir) + 1] = os.dir : '/' then
         del ofiles<os.j>
         del orecs<os.j>
      end
   next os.j
   return

* In: os.fp, the path of a file that has just been made. If the directory it is in is listed in the tree, it is listed again and the tree is drawn
os.refresh:
   if not(os.on) then return
   os.keep.i = i                                ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 A4): tree.nodes uses i, and a caller may be in a loop on it (refresh.tree.for keeps it the same way)
   os.dir = '/'
   os.ln = len(os.fp)
   for os.j = os.ln to 1 step -1
      if os.fp[os.j, 1] = '/' then
         if os.j > 1 then os.dir = os.fp[1, os.j - 1]
         exit
      end
   next os.j
   gosub os.find
   if os.q then
      gosub os.load
      gosub tree.apply
      gosub redraw.tree
   end
   i = os.keep.i
   return

* The tree widget takes the nodes again, keeping the current one
tree.apply:
   gosub tree.nodes
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'tree', tn)
   if tn = 0 then return
   tr = f{'widgets', tn}
   tr{'items'} = convert(@fm, @vm, nodes)
   tr{'cur'} = cur.k
   tr{'open'} = open.list
   tr{'opened'} = 1
   call $sl_widget_init(tr)
   return

tab.labels:
   labels = ''
   for i = 1 to nbuf
      labels<1, i> = bi<i>
      k = 'd' : bu<i>
      if bx{k} # '' then labels<1, i> = 'dict ' : bi<i>         ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a dictionary item says so on its tab
      if bn<i> = 'path:' then labels<1, i> = 'os ' : field(bi<i>, '/', dcount(bi<i>, '/'))     ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file: its last name, the whole path is on the status line
      if bi<i> = '' then
         k = 'u' : bu<i>
         labels<1, i> = sysmsg(12968, bx{k})              ;* an untitled buffer
      end
   next i
   if nbuf = 0 then labels = sysmsg(12225)
   tab.n = cb
   if tab.n < 1 then tab.n = 1
   return

* In: sy.file, sy.id, sy.first (the first line of the record).  Out: syn, the language the editor colours the record as ('' for none).  $sl_syntax decides, as it does for SED:
* the file name first, then the record id, then the first line (a script that begins #!/bin/sh), then the languages a site added.
syntax.for:
   syn = ''
   if sy.file = '' and sy.id = '' then sy.file = 'bp'          ;* an untitled buffer is coloured as a program until it is named
   call $sl_syntax('detect', sy.file, sy.id, sy.first, '', syn)
   return

* ======================================================================
* The token form of a record (26 Oct 26).  The tab's record is always the real one; only the editor's text is converted.

* In: tb.u (the tab's uid), tb.raw (its record).  Out: tb.disp, the text the editor shows.  A tab in token mode (bx m) shows the record with @vm / @sm / @tm; if the record
* cannot be shown so any more (a replace made a field that holds the text of a token) the tab goes back to showing it as it is, and says so.
tab.display:
   tb.disp = tb.raw
   k = 'm' : tb.u
   if bx{k} then
      call $recarr('totext', tb.raw, tb.disp, tb.ok)
      if not(tb.ok) then
         bx{k} = 0
         msg = sysmsg(12997, tb.disp)
         tb.disp = tb.raw
      end
   end
   return

* In: tm.u (the tab's uid), tm.raw (its record, as it is on disk).  Sets bx m: 1 when the tab shows the token form, else 0.
* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the KIND decides, not what the record held when it was read: a dictionary item, a VOC record and a data record are shown in the token form
*   (one with no marks too, so that :@vm: typed into it is a mark when it is kept), a program never is, whatever it holds (bx k 'source', and an untitled buffer).
*   The form is used only when 'totext' accepts the record (it reads every line back); one it refuses is shown as it is and says why.
set.token.mode:
   k = 'm' : tm.u
   bx{k} = 0
   tm.k = 'k' : tm.u
   tm.kind = bx{tm.k}
   if tm.kind # 'dict' and tm.kind # 'voc' and tm.kind # 'data' then return
   tm.marks = (index(tm.raw, @vm, 1) or index(tm.raw, @sm, 1) or index(tm.raw, @tm, 1))
   call $recarr('totext', tm.raw, tm.disp, tm.ok)
   if tm.ok then
      bx{k} = 1
      if tm.marks then msg = sysmsg(12996)
   end else
      msg = sysmsg(12997, tm.disp)
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* The editor and the buffers

* Keep what the editor holds (text and cursor) with the record that is open
stash.current:
   if nbuf > 0 and have.form then
      k = 'v' : bu<cb>
      if bx{k} then return              ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the tab shows its fields: its text is the one the view wrote (fields.writeback), and the hidden text area holds an older one
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      screen get f, 'ed' returning txt
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 'm' : u
      if bx{k} then
         call $recarr('fromtext', txt, tb.raw, tb.ok)         ;* the editor shows tokens: the tab keeps the record
         txt = tb.raw
      end
      k = 't' : u
      bx{k} = txt
      k = 'c' : u
      bx{k} = mw{'cx'} : @vm : mw{'cy'} : @vm : mw{'top'} : @vm : mw{'left'}
      k = 'k' : u
      if bx{k} = 'source' then
         k = 'b' : u                            ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the breakpoint marks, and the text they were kept for (bk.load moves them if the text is changed while the tab is not shown)
         bx{k} = mw{'bp'}
         k = 'j' : u
         bx{k} = mw{'value'}
      end
   end
   return

* The editor shows the record cb: its text, its language, its cursor; the tab strip says which
load.current:
   gosub tab.labels
   f{'quiet'} = 1                        ;* SCREEN SET draws the whole form after each set: the strip and the editor are drawn below, alone
   screen set f, 'tabs' items labels
   screen set f, 'tabs' value tab.n
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   if nbuf > 0 then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      tb.u = u
      tb.raw = bx{k}
      gosub tab.display
      mw{'value'} = tb.disp
      sy.file = bn<cb>
      sy.id = bi<cb>
      if bn<cb> = 'path:' then
         sy.file = ''                    ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file is named by its last component (the language follows its suffix, or a #! first line)
         sy.id = field(bi<cb>, '/', dcount(bi<cb>, '/'))
      end
      sy.first = field(tb.raw, @fm, 1)
      gosub syntax.for
      k = 'o' : u
      ro = (bx{k} # '')                  ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): another session holds the record's lock: the tab is read-only
      k = 'z' : u
      if bx{k} then ro = 1               ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): and an operating system file this session may not change
   end else
      mw{'value'} = ''
      syn = ''
      ro = 1
   end
   call $sl_widget_init(mw)
   gosub bk.load                         ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): after the init, which would otherwise take the marks from a text they were not made for
   nl = 0
   if syn # '' then call $sl_syntax('index', syn, '', '', '', nl)
   mw{'synlang'} = nl + 0
   if syn.off then mw{'synlang'} = 0              ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): View > Syntax colour off holds for every tab
   mw{'syn.want'} = nl + 0                        ;* the tab's language, so that View > Syntax colour can put it back
   mw{'readonly'} = ro
   if nbuf > 0 then gosub memo.cursor
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - only the tab strip and the editor have changed: drawing the whole form again wrote every cell of the screen, which the
   *   owner saw as a flash each time a record was chosen ("flashes the whole screen").  A caller that is about to focus the editor (which
   *   draws it) sets lc.nodraw and draws the tab strip itself.
   if not(lc.nodraw) then gosub redraw.editor
   gosub fields.apply                    ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the tab now shown shows its text or its fields; the form says the same
   return

* The cursor of the record cb goes back where it was
memo.cursor:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 'c' : u
   cc = bx{k}
   if cc # '' then
      k = 't' : u
      nl = dcount(bx{k}, @fm)
      if cc<1, 2> + 0 >= 1 and cc<1, 2> + 0 <= nl then
         mw{'cx'} = cc<1, 1> + 0
         mw{'cy'} = cc<1, 2> + 0
         mw{'top'} = cc<1, 3> + 0
         mw{'left'} = cc<1, 4> + 0
         mw{'want'} = cc<1, 1> + 0
      end
   end
   return

redraw:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('redraw', f, scr, '', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* The tab strip and the editor only (a record chosen, a tab closed or switched); the rest of the screen is as it was
redraw.editor:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabbar', rr)       ;* the blank bar under the strip: a strip that got shorter leaves no label behind
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabs', rr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'ed', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* A file name and a record id for a command line: qp.file and qp.id in, qp.f and qp.i out, each in quotes when it holds a blank (the command
* parser reads one word to a name otherwise, and the verifier's Ctrl-E on the record "my rec" opened a new record "my" in SED).  A name that
* holds both kinds of quote is left as it is.
quote.pair:
   qp.f = qp.file
   qp.i = qp.id
   for qp.k = 1 to 2
      qp.v = if qp.k = 1 then qp.file else qp.id
      if index(qp.v, ' ', 1) or index(qp.v, '"', 1) or index(qp.v, "'", 1) then
         if not(index(qp.v, '"', 1)) then
            qp.v = '"' : qp.v : '"'
         end else
            if not(index(qp.v, "'", 1)) then qp.v = "'" : qp.v : "'"
         end
      end
      if qp.k = 1 then qp.f = qp.v else qp.i = qp.v
   next qp.k
   return

* The focus goes to widget ft.id and is shown there: the widget that had it and the one that has it are drawn, and the cursor goes to the new one
focus.to:
   gosub fields.is.on
   if ft.id = 'ed' and fv.on then ft.id = 'fld'        ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the editor pane is the fields table while the tab shows its fields
   if ft.id = 'fld' and not(fv.on) then ft.id = 'ed'
   focus.id = ft.id
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('focus', f, scr, ft.id, rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

redraw.tabs:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabbar', rr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tabs', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* The tree only (a file opened or closed in it)
redraw.tree:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'tree', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* In: or.file, or.id; or.dict ('DICT': an item of the file's dictionary) and or.mknew (1: a record that is not there opens as a NEW record, after the account's
*   edit gate), which are taken here and put back to '' and 0 at once, so that every other caller gets the data part and "not found".  Opens the record in a tab
*   of its own, or goes to the tab it is already in.  28 Oct 26 (stage B): the record is read under its lock, which the tab keeps (lock.take); a record another
*   session holds opens read-only; a collection file's record is refused (stage F hands it to CED).
open.record:
   od = or.dict
   omk = or.mknew
   or.dict = ''
   or.mknew = 0
   tf.dict = od
   tf.file = or.file
   tf.id = or.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      new.cb = tf.p
      if new.cb # cb then gosub switch.to
      if od = '' then gosub hist.push        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): opened again, so it is the newest
      return
   end
   lk.dict = od
   lk.file = or.file
   lk.id = or.id
   gosub lock.take
   if lk.err # '' then
      msg = lk.err
      return
   end
   or.name = lk.name
   rec = lk.rec
   if lk.kind = 'dict' then
      dt.rec = rec
      gosub dict.trim
      rec = dt.rec
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (D2): a recovery copy of the record is offered before the tab is made - also for a record that is not on the disk (a new record that was never
   *   saved), which then opens as a new record holding the copy.  Cancel opens nothing and keeps the copy.
   or.new = 0
   rc.file = or.name                  ;* 'dict file' for a dictionary item, whose copy is not the data record's
   rc.id = or.id
   if not(lk.exists) then
      if lk.owner # '' then
         msg = sysmsg(12691, or.name, or.id, lk.owner)     ;* %1 %2 is being made by another session (user %3); it was not opened
         return
      end
      rc.has.disk = @false
      rc.disk = ''
      gosub rc.offer
      begin case
         case rc.choice = 'use'
            null
         case rc.choice = 'cancel'
            gosub lock.free
            return
         case omk
            * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a record named on the command line that is not there is a new one - after the account's edit gate, as the name form asks it
            gosub edit.check
            if not(edit.ok) then
               gosub lock.free
               rf.text = msg
               gosub refuse.box
               return
            end
            if rc.res = '' then msg = sysmsg(12690, or.id, or.name)   ;* New record %1 in %2: nothing is written until you save it
         case 1
            gosub lock.free
            if rc.res = '' then msg = sysmsg(12206, or.id, or.name)
            return
      end case
      rec = ''
      or.new = 1
   end else
      rc.has.disk = @true
      rc.disk = rec
      gosub rc.offer
      if rc.choice = 'cancel' then
         gosub lock.free
         return
      end
   end
   gosub stash.current
   gosub drop.clean.untitled
   nbuf += 1
   uid += 1
   bn<nbuf> = or.file
   bi<nbuf> = or.id
   bu<nbuf> = uid
   k = 'n' : uid
   bx{k} = or.new
   k = 's' : uid
   bx{k} = rec
   k = 'c' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   tb.shown = rec
   if rc.choice = 'use' then tb.shown = rc.text
   if lk.kind = 'coll' and or.new and rc.choice # 'use' then tb.shown = '{}'      ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a new record of a collection file starts as an empty collection
   k = 't' : uid
   bx{k} = tb.shown
   gosub rc.init.tab
   ts.kind = lk.kind
   ts.dict = od
   ts.slot = lk.slot
   ts.owner = lk.owner
   gosub tab.state
   tm.u = uid
   tm.raw = tb.shown
   gosub set.token.mode
   if rc.choice = 'use' then gosub rc.took
   if lk.owner # '' then msg = sysmsg(12694, or.name, or.id, lk.owner)    ;* %1 %2 is open read-only: user %3 holds its lock
   cb = nbuf
   if od = '' then gosub hist.push          ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): the file and the record for Up and Down in Open's form (a dictionary item is not: the form names no dictionary)
   gosub load.current
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3, SED's file and record history; sed:624-632) - in: or.file, or.id, a record opened in a tab.  Each goes to the top of its list in the named common
*   /idehist/ (a name already there moves up, it is not kept twice), and a list keeps the IH.MAX newest.  Only names are kept: nothing is read or opened from them here.
hist.push:
   hp.item = or.file
   hp.list = ih.files
   gosub hist.add
   ih.files = hp.list
   hp.item = or.id
   hp.list = ih.recs
   gosub hist.add
   ih.recs = hp.list
   return

* In: hp.item, a command given to Run a command or Run a command at the terminal: the two prompts share one list.
hist.command:
   hp.list = ih.cmds
   gosub hist.add
   ih.cmds = hp.list
   return

* In: hp.item, hp.list (fields, newest first).  Out: hp.list with hp.item first
hist.add:
   if hp.item = '' then return
   locate hp.item in hp.list<1> setting hp.at then del hp.list<hp.at>      ;* <1> walks the FIELDS (the list's entries)
   if hp.list = '' then hp.list = hp.item else hp.list = hp.item : @fm : hp.list
   if dcount(hp.list, @fm) > IH.MAX then hp.list = field(hp.list, @fm, 1, IH.MAX)
   return

* ======================================================================
* Operating system files (28 Oct 26, the editor merge's stage E)

* In: op.path, the full name as typed (the command line's, or the name form's: never folded).  Opens the file in a tab of its own, or goes to the tab it is already in.
*   EVERYTHING THAT TOUCHES THE FILE IS !ide_osfile's, which is not $internal, so the kernel asks the account's OS gate and the edit gates for it; K$PATH.OK is asked here of the
*   same string first, for its own message.  The tab: bn 'path:', bi the path, kind 'path', no dictionary flag; it keeps the file variable (tfv, bx l) and with it the file's SD
*   lock until it is closed; bx p holds the line ends and bx z is 1 when the file is read-only.  A recovery copy is never kept for it (rc.tick), and Save writes it in place.
open.path:
   op.path = trimf(trimb(op.path))
   call !ide_osfile('check', op.path, op.f, op.text, op.info, op.why)
   if op.why # '' then
      gosub path.refusal
      return
   end
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = 'path:'
   tf.id = op.path
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      new.cb = tf.p
      if new.cb # cb then gosub switch.to
      return
   end
   if not(kernel(K$PATH.OK, op.path)) then
      msg = sysmsg(12185, @who)                ;* %1 has no OS access, so a file or directory outside its own account cannot be reached by pathname
      return
   end
   for op.s = 1 to MAXLOCK
      if tused<op.s> = '' then exit
   next op.s
   if op.s > MAXLOCK then
      msg = sysmsg(12695)                      ;* Too many records are open: close a tab first
      return
   end
   call !ide_osfile('open', op.path, op.f, op.text, op.info, op.why)
   if op.why # '' then
      gosub path.refusal
      return
   end
   tfv(op.s) = op.f
   close op.f                                  ;* the tab's variable is the one that holds the file, and its lock, from here
   tused<op.s> = 1
   gosub path.tab.make
   return

* In: op.s (the slot of tfv that holds the file's variable, and with it the file's SD lock, tused set), op.path, op.text and op.info (as !ide_osfile 'open' or 'create' gave them).
*   The file is opened in a tab of its own, which becomes the one in the editor (30 Oct 26, stage E2: the tail of open.path, which New with an operating system name uses too)
path.tab.make:
   gosub stash.current
   gosub drop.clean.untitled
   nbuf += 1
   uid += 1
   bn<nbuf> = 'path:'
   bi<nbuf> = op.path
   bu<nbuf> = uid
   k = 'n' : uid
   bx{k} = 0
   k = 's' : uid
   bx{k} = op.text
   k = 'c' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 't' : uid
   bx{k} = op.text
   gosub rc.init.tab
   ts.kind = 'path'
   ts.dict = ''
   ts.slot = op.s
   ts.owner = ''
   gosub tab.state
   k = 'm' : uid
   bx{k} = 0                                   ;* never the token form: the lines of a file are text
   k = 'p' : uid
   bx{k} = (op.info<1> + 0) : @fm : (op.info<2> + 0)
   k = 'z' : uid
   bx{k} = op.info<3> + 0
   cb = nbuf
   begin case
      case op.info<3> + 0
         msg = sysmsg(12740, op.path)
      case op.info<1> + 0
         msg = sysmsg(12745, 'path:', op.path)
   end case
   gosub load.current
   return

* In: op.why, op.path, op.info.  Out: msg, why an operating system file was not opened
path.refusal:
   begin case
      case op.why = 'name'
         msg = sysmsg(12733, op.path)
      case op.why = 'none'
         msg = sysmsg(12734, op.path)
      case op.why = 'gate'
         msg = sysmsg(12185, @who)
      case op.why = 'dir' or op.why = 'special'
         msg = sysmsg(12735, op.path)
      case op.why = 'locked'
         msg = sysmsg(12736, op.path)
      case op.why = 'big'
         msg = sysmsg(12737, op.path, op.info<4>)
      case op.why = 'bytes'
         msg = sysmsg(12738, op.path)
      case 1
         msg = sysmsg(12739, op.path, op.info<6>)
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* Making operating system files (30 Oct 26, stage E2).  EVERY TOUCH OF A FILE IS STILL !ide_osfile's: the kernel creates it exclusively (OPENSEQ ... CREATING is O_EXCL since 30 Oct 26), asks the
*   account's OS gate and edit gates for it, and never makes the directory it is in.  The name form has asked K$PATH.OK and K$EDIT.OS.OK first, for its own words.  A file is made BEFORE anything
*   else changes: when it is not made, the tab, the record and the lock the tab had are as they were.  An existing EMPTY file is filled; a file with content is never replaced.

* In: cr.path (the name as the form returned it), cr.text (the lines, field marks between them), cr.info (the line ends: field 1 CR LF, field 2 the last line's end; 0 FM 1 for text typed).
*   Out: cr.why ('' when made), cr.f (open, with the SD lock, then; the caller keeps it in a tab or closes it), cr.text and cr.info as the file reads back; msg (and the box when the file may be
*   there and not whole) when it was not made
os.create:
   call !ide_osfile('create', cr.path, cr.f, cr.text, cr.info, cr.why)
   begin case
      case cr.why = ''
         null
      case cr.why = 'name'
         msg = sysmsg(12733, cr.path)
      case cr.why = 'gate'
         if kernel(K$EDIT.OS.OK, cr.path) then
            msg = sysmsg(12185, @who)
         end else
            msg = sysmsg(12768, cr.path, 3035)       ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 A5): the refusal is an edit gate that came into force since the name form asked, not "no OS access"
         end
      case cr.why = 'exists' or cr.why = 'ro'
         rf.text = sysmsg(12767, cr.path)                       ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 9g): in a box, the status line cut it
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
      case cr.why = 'dir' or cr.why = 'special'
         msg = sysmsg(12754, cr.path)
      case cr.why = 'locked'
         rf.text = sysmsg(15997, cr.path)                       ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 A3); since 02 Nov 26 the kernel makes nothing when the name is locked
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
      case cr.why = 'cantmade'
         rf.text = sysmsg(15996, cr.path, cr.info<6>)           ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 A3): an ON ERROR ended the create; the file may have been made
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
      case cr.why = 'bytes' or cr.why = 'big'
         msg = sysmsg(12770, cr.path)
      case cr.why = 'cant'
         msg = sysmsg(12768, cr.path, cr.info<6>)             ;* also an edit-blocked race (S.88 A5)
      case 1
         rf.text = sysmsg(12769, cr.path, cr.info<6>)
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
   end case
   return

* In: cr.path.  Out: cr.slot (a free slot of tfv, 0 and msg when there is none or the file is open in a tab already).  Not touching any file.
os.slot:
   cr.slot = 0
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = 'path:'
   tf.id = cr.path
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, 'path:', cr.path)                  ;* open in another tab: save it there
      return
   end
   for cr.s = 1 to MAXLOCK
      if tused<cr.s> = '' then exit
   next cr.s
   if cr.s > MAXLOCK then
      msg = sysmsg(12695)                                   ;* Too many records are open: close a tab first
      return
   end
   cr.slot = cr.s
   return

* New record (Ctrl-N) with a name of the operating system: an EMPTY file is made and opened in a tab of its own (a tab needs the file, and its lock).  In: nm.id (the path).
os.new:
   cr.path = nm.id
   cr.why = 'slot'                                           ;* not made (os.slot refused) until os.create says otherwise
   gosub os.slot
   if not(cr.slot) then return
   cr.text = ''
   cr.info = 0 : @fm : 0
   gosub os.create
   if cr.why # '' then return
   op.s = cr.slot
   op.f = cr.f
   op.path = cr.path
   op.text = cr.text
   op.info = cr.info
   tfv(op.s) = op.f
   close op.f
   tused<op.s> = 1
   gosub path.tab.make
   msg = sysmsg(12207, 'path:', op.path)
   os.fp = op.path
   gosub os.refresh                             ;* the directory it is in, when the tree lists it
   return

* Save as (and the first save of an untitled buffer) with a name of the operating system: the file is made with the tab's text, and ONLY THEN does the tab let go of the record it had (its
*   lock, its recovery copy) and become the file's tab, which keeps the file variable and the SD lock.  In: oc.i (the tab), oc.path.  Out: oc.ok.
os.convert:
   oc.ok = @false
   cr.path = oc.path
   gosub os.slot
   if not(cr.slot) then return
   oc.u = bu<oc.i>
   k = 't' : oc.u
   cr.text = bx{k}
   cr.info = 0 : @fm : (cr.text # '')
   oc.sent = cr.text
   gosub os.create
   if cr.why # '' then return
   oc.s = cr.slot
   tfv(oc.s) = cr.f
   close cr.f
   tused<oc.s> = 1
   ri = oc.i
   gosub rc.drop.tab                            ;* the text is in the file now; a recovery copy of the buffer or record goes
   tu.i = oc.i
   gosub tab.unlock                             ;* the record's lock, if the tab held one
   bn<oc.i> = 'path:'
   bi<oc.i> = oc.path
   oc.uid = uid                                 ;* tab.state works on uid (the last one made): point it at this tab for a moment
   uid = oc.u
   ts.kind = 'path'
   ts.dict = ''
   ts.slot = oc.s
   ts.owner = ''
   gosub tab.state
   uid = oc.uid
   k = 'm' : oc.u
   bx{k} = 0
   k = 'p' : oc.u
   bx{k} = (cr.info<1> + 0) : @fm : (cr.info<2> + 0)
   k = 'n' : oc.u
   bx{k} = 0
   k = 's' : oc.u
   bx{k} = cr.text                              ;* the baseline is what the disk gives back
   if (cr.text : '|') # (oc.sent : '|') then
      k = 't' : oc.u
      bx{k} = cr.text
   end
   oc.ok = @true
   msg = sysmsg(12207, 'path:', oc.path)
   if oc.i = cb then gosub load.current
   os.fp = oc.path
   gosub os.refresh
   return

* Write a copy and Export with a name of the operating system: the text is written to a file that is made for it; no tab is made and no lock is kept.  In: wa.id (the path), wa.text.
copy.to.os:
   cr.path = wa.id
   cr.text = wa.text
   cr.info = 0 : @fm : (cr.text # '')
   gosub os.create
   if cr.why = '' then
      close cr.f
      msg = sysmsg(12207, 'path:', wa.id)
      os.fp = wa.id
      gosub os.refresh
   end
   return

* Out: pa.no, true (and msg says why) when the tab in the editor is an operating system file: the commands for records and programs - compile, run, debug, catalogue, SED, fields,
*   Save as, Write a copy, Open the include - do not apply to it
path.not.for:
   pa.no = @false
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   pa.k = 'k' : bu<cb>
   if bx{pa.k} = 'coll' and pa.allow # 'coll' then
      pa.no = @true                    ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a record of a collection file is not a program either (Edit as fields is the one command that asks with pa.allow = 'coll')
      msg = sysmsg(12749, bn<cb>, bi<cb>)
      return
   end
   if bn<cb> # 'path:' then return
   pa.no = @true
   rf.text = sysmsg(12744, bn<cb>, bi<cb>)                      ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 9g): in a box, the status line cut it
   msg = rf.text
   gosub refuse.box
   return

* ======================================================================
* Kinds and locks (28 Oct 26, the editor merge's stage B)

* In: uid, ts.kind, ts.dict, ts.slot, ts.owner.  The tab uid's kind, dictionary flag, lock slot and read-only owner
tab.state:
   k = 'k' : uid
   bx{k} = ts.kind
   k = 'd' : uid
   bx{k} = ts.dict
   k = 'l' : uid
   bx{k} = ts.slot
   k = 'o' : uid
   bx{k} = ts.owner
   k = 'z' : uid
   bx{k} = 0                         ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): not an operating system file that this session may not change (open.path sets it)
   k = 'p' : uid
   bx{k} = ''                        ;* an operating system file's line ends: 1 when every line ends CR LF, FM, 1 when the last line had its line end (the helper's INFO<1> and <2>, as they are passed to 'save')
   return

* In: tf.dict, tf.file, tf.id.  Out: tf.p, the tab that holds that record (0: none).  A dictionary item and the data record of the same id are two records
tab.find:
   tf.p = 0
   for tf.i = 1 to nbuf
      tf.k = 'd' : bu<tf.i>
      if bn<tf.i> = tf.file and bi<tf.i> = tf.id and bx{tf.k} = tf.dict then
         tf.p = tf.i
         exit
      end
   next tf.i
   return

* In: ko.dict, fv (the file, open).  Out: ko.kind - 'dict' for the dictionary part (or a file the kernel knows as one), 'voc' for a VOC, 'source' for a program
*   file (the kernel's own test of the name: bp, bp.out, *.bp) and for any directory file (scripts, C, text), 'data' for every other file.  A collection file is
*   refused before this is asked.  The kind decides the token form (never for source) and that a dictionary item is not compiled.
kind.of:
   ko.kind = 'data'
   if ko.dict # '' then
      ko.kind = 'dict'
      return
   end
   ko.ek = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, fileinfo(fv, FL$PATH))
   begin case
      case ko.ek = 1
         ko.kind = 'voc'
      case ko.ek = 2
         ko.kind = 'dict'
      case ko.ek = 3
         ko.kind = 'source'
      case fileinfo(fv, FL$TYPE) = FL$TYPE.DIR
         ko.kind = 'source'
   end case
   return

* In: dt.rec, a dictionary item.  An A, C, I or S item loses its compiled code (the fields from DICT.SYS.INFO on), as SED shows it: the editor holds what a
*   person writes, and the item is compiled again when it is next used.  The same is done to the disk record before the compare at a save.
dict.trim:
   dt.t = upcase(dt.rec[1, 1])
   if dt.t = 'A' or dt.t = 'C' or dt.t = 'I' or dt.t = 'S' then
      dt.rec = trim(field(dt.rec, @fm, 1, DICT.SYS.INFO - 1), @fm, 'T')
   end
   return

* In: lk.dict, lk.file, lk.id.  Out: lk.err ('' or why nothing can be opened), lk.name (the file as the person reads it: 'dict file' for a dictionary), lk.kind,
*   lk.exists and lk.rec (the record, '' when it is not there), lk.slot (the slot of tfv the lock is held through; 0 when none is held) and lk.owner (the user
*   who holds the lock, when it is not this session).  fv is the file, open.  A record that is there and locked by another session is read without a lock (the
*   tab is read-only); one that is not there is locked for its creation, as SED locks it.
lock.take:
   lk.err = ''
   lk.kind = ''
   lk.slot = 0
   lk.owner = ''
   lk.exists = @false
   lk.rec = ''
   lk.name = lk.file
   if lk.dict # '' then lk.name = 'dict ' : lk.file
   open lk.dict, lk.file to fv else
      lk.err = sysmsg(12205, lk.name)
      return
   end
   * 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a collection file's record is a collection, not text, and a plain READ of it into the editor would stop the IDE and every tab with it: the tab
   *   shows its JSON (coll.text) and the save parses it back (coll.value, in save.one); kind 'coll'.  A collection file has no dictionary part to ask about.
   lk.coll = (fileinfo(fv, FL$COLLECTION) + 0 # 0)
   if lk.coll then
      lk.kind = 'coll'
   end else
      ko.dict = lk.dict
      gosub kind.of
      lk.kind = ko.kind
   end
   for lk.s = 1 to MAXLOCK
      if tused<lk.s> = '' then exit
   next lk.s
   if lk.s > MAXLOCK then
      lk.err = sysmsg(12695)               ;* Too many records are open: close a tab first
      return
   end
   tfv(lk.s) = fv                        ;* the tab's own file variable: the lock is taken through it and lives as long as it does
   lk.got = @false
   readu lk.rec from tfv(lk.s), lk.id on error
      lk.err = sysmsg(12206, lk.id, lk.name) : ' (' : status() : ')'
      lk.rec = ''
   end locked
      lk.owner = status()
      read lk.rec from tfv(lk.s), lk.id then
         lk.exists = @true
      end else
         lk.rec = ''
      end
   end then
      lk.exists = @true
      lk.got = @true
   end else
      lk.rec = ''
      lk.got = @true
   end
   if lk.coll and lk.err = '' and lk.exists then gosub coll.text
   if lk.err # '' then lk.got = @false
   if lk.got then
      lk.slot = lk.s
      tused<lk.s> = 1
   end else
      tfv(lk.s) = 0
   end
   return

* 29 Oct 26 (stage F).  In: lk.rec, a record of a collection file as READ gave it (a collection or an array).  Out: lk.rec as the text of its JSON, one element to a line
*   (JBUILD with JB$PRETTY, the layout CT shows), or lk.err (12689) when it is neither, or has no JSON form (JBUILD gives a null string and STATUS() 1019, never an abort).
*   29 Oct 26 (review R3): the lines are joined by FIELD MARKS, which is how the text area keeps them (the memo turns a line feed into one); JBUILD's own line feeds would make the tab "changed" for
*   ever, the text area's text never being equal to the text it was given.  Lossless: JBUILD writes a line feed inside a string as \n and every byte under 32 or over 127 as an escape, so no mark can
*   stand in the text it makes; coll.value turns the field marks back into line feeds before JPARSE (a mark typed inside a string would be read as data).
coll.text:
   lk.ct = jtype(lk.rec)
   lk.cj = ''
   if lk.ct = 'collection' or lk.ct = 'array' then lk.cj = convert(char(10), @fm, jbuild(lk.rec, JB$PRETTY))
   if lk.cj = '' then
      lk.err = sysmsg(12689, lk.name, lk.id)
      lk.rec = ''
      if lk.got then release tfv(lk.s), lk.id          ;* the READU took the lock: it goes with the refusal (lock.take then closes the tab's variable)
   end else
      lk.rec = lk.cj
   end
   return

* In: lk.slot, lk.id.  The lock lock.take took is given back (no tab was made for it)
lock.free:
   if lk.slot then
      release tfv(lk.slot), lk.id
      tfv(lk.slot) = 0
      tused<lk.slot> = ''
      lk.slot = 0
   end
   return

* In: tu.i.  The lock tab tu.i holds is given back (the tab is being closed, or becomes another record)
tab.unlock:
   tu.k = 'l' : bu<tu.i>
   tu.s = bx{tu.k} + 0
   if tu.s then
      tu.k2 = 'k' : bu<tu.i>
      if bx{tu.k2} = 'path' then
         close tfv(tu.s)                 ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file's lock goes with the file variable (bi is a path, not a record id to release)
      end else
         release tfv(tu.s), bi<tu.i>
      end
      tfv(tu.s) = 0
      tused<tu.s> = ''
      bx{tu.k} = 0
   end
   return

* Every lock the tabs hold is given back (every way the IDE ends)
release.all:
   for tu.i = 1 to nbuf
      gosub tab.unlock
   next tu.i
   return

* In: tu.i.  The tab is given its record's lock (a tab that has just become a record: an untitled buffer named, Save as, New record).  Out: tl.ok, false when
*   another session holds it (the tab then holds none; its save will say so) or the file cannot be opened (msg)
tab.lock:
   tl.ok = @false
   tl.u = bu<tu.i>
   tl.k = 'd' : tl.u
   lk.dict = bx{tl.k}
   lk.file = bn<tu.i>
   lk.id = bi<tu.i>
   gosub lock.take
   if lk.err # '' then
      msg = lk.err
      return
   end
   tl.k = 'k' : tl.u
   bx{tl.k} = lk.kind
   tl.k = 'l' : tl.u
   bx{tl.k} = lk.slot
   tl.ok = (lk.slot > 0)
   return

* After the terminal was handed to a program (Run, Debug, SED, CED): a lock the program let go - a RELEASE in it, or SED's own READU of the record, which moves the
*   lock to SED's file variable and gives it back when SED closes the file - is taken again through the tab's own file variable (a lock this session already holds
*   is only moved back to it).  One that another session took meanwhile makes the tab read-only, and says so.
relock.all:
   for rl.i = 1 to nbuf
      rl.u = bu<rl.i>
      rl.k = 'l' : rl.u
      rl.s = bx{rl.k} + 0
      rl.k2 = 'k' : rl.u
      if bx{rl.k2} = 'path' then rl.s = 0     ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file's lock is the file variable's own and is not taken again here. A BARE RELEASE in a program run from the IDE lets it go (review, finding 3): the save then still reads the file again (a changed or replaced file is caught), and only another session's write between that read and ours is not
      if rl.s then
         rl.lost = @false
         recordlocku tfv(rl.s), bi<rl.i> locked
            rl.lost = @true
            rl.owner = status()
         end
         if rl.lost then
            tfv(rl.s) = 0
            tused<rl.s> = ''
            bx{rl.k} = 0
            k = 'o' : rl.u
            bx{k} = rl.owner
            i = rl.i
            gosub buf.label
            msg = sysmsg(12697, bf.name, bf.id, rl.owner)   ;* %1 %2: another session (user %3) took its lock while the IDE was away; the tab is read-only
         end
      end
   next rl.i
   return

* In: ro.i.  Out: ro.ok, false (and msg says why) when tab ro.i is read-only: another session holds its record's lock, so nothing may change its text;
*   ro.why 'lock' for that
ro.check:
   ro.ok = @true
   ro.why = ''
   ro.k = 'z' : bu<ro.i>
   if bx{ro.k} then
      ro.ok = @false                                              ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file this session may not change
      ro.why = 'osro'
      i = ro.i
      gosub buf.label
      msg = sysmsg(12742, bf.name, bf.id)                         ;* %1 %2 is read-only here: the operating system, or an edit gate of this account, does not let this session change it
      return
   end
   ro.k = 'o' : bu<ro.i>
   if bx{ro.k} = '' then return
   ro.ok = @false
   ro.why = 'lock'
   i = ro.i
   gosub buf.label
   msg = sysmsg(12693, bf.name, bf.id, bx{ro.k})                 ;* %1 %2 is read-only here: user %3 holds its lock ...
   return

* After ro.check refused: an operating system file's refusal is shown in a box, whole; the lock's stays on the status line
ro.box:
   if ro.why # 'osro' then return         ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 9g): 12742 (an operating system file this session may not change) is as long as the ASCII message and was cut by the status line
   rf.text = msg
   gosub refuse.box
   return

* In: sq.dict, sq.file.  Out: sq.id, the file's next sequential record id (CREATING.SEQKEY, as SED takes it: $seqkey), or '' with the reason in msg.  The account's
*   edit gate is asked first: an id taken is not given back
seq.take:
   sq.id = ''
   sq.name = sq.file
   if sq.dict # '' then sq.name = 'dict ' : sq.file
   open sq.dict, sq.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, sq.name)
      return
   end
   gosub edit.check
   if not(edit.ok) then
      rf.text = msg
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   sq.why = ''
   call $seqkey(fv, @true, sq.id, sq.why)
   if sq.why # '' then
      msg = sq.why
      sq.id = ''
   end
   return

* The records of the select list that was active at the start (sel0, read before anything else could use list 0): all of them in the table at the bottom, where
*   Return opens one, and the first MAXSEL of them in tabs
open.select:
   os.n = dcount(sel0, @fm)
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   sid = ''
   sln = ''
   for os.i = 1 to os.n
      err.rows<-1> = 1 : @vm : sel0<os.i>
      err.lines<-1> = 1
      sid<-1> = sel0<os.i>
      sln<-1> = 1
   next os.i
   out.kind = 'search'
   gosub zoom.leave                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): what fills the messages is shown
   out.file = fn0
   out.dict = dflag0
   gosub err.load
   os.open = os.n
   if os.open > MAXSEL then os.open = MAXSEL
   for os.i = 1 to os.open
      or.dict = dflag0
      or.file = fn0
      or.id = sel0<os.i>
      or.mknew = (if sel0.new then 1 else 0)   ;* 31 Oct 26 (stage 4): an id typed on the command line that is not there is a new record, as for one id; the ids of a list are there
      gosub open.record
   next os.i
   if nbuf > 1 and cb # 1 then
      new.cb = 1
      gosub switch.to
   end
   if ids0 # '' or star0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12924, os.n, os.open)         ;* %1 records are listed below; the first %2 are open in tabs
      if sel0.more then msg := '  ' : sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, fn0)
   end else
      msg = sysmsg(12699, os.n, os.open)          ;* %1 records of the select list are listed below; the first %2 are open in tabs
      if sel0.more then msg := '  ' : sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, 'the select list')
   end
   * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): the list is kept for Go > Next record from the list (do.nextsel), which goes on after the records opened here
   lst.ids = sel0
   lst.file = fn0
   lst.dict = dflag0
   lst.next = os.open + 1
   lst.mk = (if sel0.new then 1 else 0)
   lst.more = sel0.more
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3) - Go > Next record from the list: SED goes from one record of an active select list to the next; the IDE opens the first MAXSEL in tabs at the
*   start (open.select) and this opens the next one that is not open in a tab yet, in a tab of its own, from the ids kept in lst.ids.  A record already in a tab is passed
*   over; one that cannot be opened (another session's lock, a record gone) says why and is passed too, so the next choice goes on after it.  When every id has been
*   looked at the list is used up and the status line says so.  Nothing is selected again: the list was used up when the IDE started, as SED's is.
do.nextsel:
   if lst.ids = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(15048)                         ;* There is no select list: ...
      return
   end
   ns.n = dcount(lst.ids, @fm)
   ns.done = @false
   loop
   while lst.next <= ns.n and not(ns.done)
      ns.id = lst.ids<lst.next>
      lst.next += 1
      tf.dict = lst.dict
      tf.file = lst.file
      tf.id = ns.id
      gosub tab.find
      if tf.p = 0 then
         ns.done = @true
         msg = ''
         or.dict = lst.dict
         or.file = lst.file
         or.id = ns.id
         or.mknew = lst.mk
         gosub open.record
         if nbuf > 0 then
            ft.id = 'ed'
            gosub focus.to
         end
         if msg = '' then msg = sysmsg(15049, lst.next - 1, ns.n)       ;* Record %1 of %2 of the select list
      end
   repeat
   if not(ns.done) then
      msg = sysmsg(15050, ns.n)                   ;* The select list is used up: ...
      if lst.more then msg := '  ' : sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, 'the select list')
   end
   return

* Go / Open the include: the line at the editor's cursor is $include {file} record (the compiler's form: a comment after ; is not part of it).  The record opens in a
*   tab.  With no file the compiler's own order is looked in: the file of the record in the editor, then syscom
do.include:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub path.not.for                   ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): a $include names a record of the account's files
   if pa.no then return
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      msg = sysmsg(15998)               ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): it reads the line at the cursor of the text area
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   in.line = mw{'value'}<mw{'cy'} + 0>
   in.line = trim(field(in.line, ';', 1))
   if downcase(field(in.line, ' ', 1)) # '$include' then
      msg = sysmsg(12700)                              ;* The line at the cursor is not a $include line
      return
   end
   in.n = dcount(in.line, ' ')
   begin case
      case in.n = 2
         in.rec = field(in.line, ' ', 2)
         in.files = bn<cb>
         if in.files = '' then in.files = 'bp'
         k = 'd' : bu<cb>
         if bx{k} # '' then in.files = 'bp'               ;* a dictionary item has no program file of its own
         if in.files # 'syscom' then in.files<-1> = 'syscom'
      case in.n = 3
         in.rec = field(in.line, ' ', 3)
         in.files = field(in.line, ' ', 2)
      case 1
         msg = sysmsg(12700)
         return
   end case
   in.found = @false
   in.nf = dcount(in.files, @fm)
   for in.j = 1 to in.nf
      in.file = in.files<in.j>
      open in.file to fv else
         open downcase(in.file) to fv then in.file = downcase(in.file) else continue
      end
      if fileinfo(fv, FL$COLLECTION) then continue
      read in.x from fv, in.rec on error
         continue
      end then
         in.found = @true
      end else
         read in.x from fv, downcase(in.rec) then
            in.rec = downcase(in.rec)
            in.found = @true
         end
      end
      if in.found then exit
   next in.j
   if not(in.found) then
      msg = sysmsg(12701, in.rec, change(in.files, @fm, ', '))   ;* Include record %1 was not found (looked in %2)
      return
   end
   or.file = in.file
   or.id = in.rec
   cur.code = 'R:' : or.file : ':' : or.id
   gosub open.record
   if nbuf > 0 then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
      gosub redraw.tabs
   end
   return

* In: new.cb
switch.to:
   gosub stash.current
   cb = new.cb
   gosub load.current
   return

* Whether record i has changes: dirty
is.dirty:
   u = bu<i>
   k = 't' : u
   d1 = bx{k}
   k = 's' : u
   d2 = bx{k}
   dirty = ((d1 : '|') # (d2 : '|'))     ;* as strings: # compares two numbers as numbers, so a one-line "1.0" changed to "1" was not a change (review of 28 Oct 26, finding 6)
   k = 'n' : u
   if bx{k} and d1 # '' then dirty = @true            ;* a record that is not on the disk yet and holds text
   return

* ======================================================================
* The recovery copy (28 Oct 26, S.88 plan 2.9, D2).  Only where the administrator has turned it on for the account (MODIFY.ACCOUNT RECOVERY-ON; delivered off), and
* only in a store in the person's own Linux home (28 Oct 26: it was the account's $recovery): !edit_recovery decides that, makes and checks the owner-only
* directories, and keeps the copies.  The IDE says WHICH text and
* WHEN.  For each tab: bx qk<uid> is the file and record (as two values) of the copy THIS TAB owns ('' none), bx r<uid> the text that copy holds, bx w<uid> the
* token an untitled buffer's copy is kept under.  A tab owns a copy it wrote or one the person took into it (Use the copy); a copy it does not own is never written
* over, and the person is asked about it (rc.offer) before any tab for that record is made.  NOTHING HERE WRITES THE RECORD: the person's save does, as ever.

* A new tab owns no copy.  In: uid
rc.init.tab:
   k = 'qk' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'r' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'w' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'x' : uid
   bx{k} = ''                        ;* file VM record VM time: a copy the tab does not own was found under that key, look again at that time (rc.tick)
   return

* In: uid, rc.file, rc.id, rc.text.  The tab uid has just taken the copy: it owns it, and the copy stays until the record is saved or the tab closed
rc.took:
   k = 'qk' : uid
   bx{k} = rc.file : @vm : rc.id
   k = 'r' : uid
   bx{k} = rc.text
   if rc.file = '' then
      k = 'w' : uid
      bx{k} = rc.id
   end
   msg = sysmsg(12778)               ;* The recovery copy is in the editor; the record on disk is unchanged until you save it
   return

* In: ri.  Out: rk.file, rk.id, the key of buffer ri's copy: its file and record, or for an untitled buffer no file and a token of its own (made the first time)
rc.key:
   rk.u = bu<ri>
   rk.file = bn<ri>
   k = 'd' : rk.u
   if bx{k} # '' then rk.file = 'dict ' : bn<ri>     ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a dictionary item's copy is not the data record's (open.record offers it so)
   rk.id = bi<ri>
   if rk.id = '' then
      rk.file = ''
      k = 'w' : rk.u
      rk.id = bx{k}
      if rk.id = '' then
         rk.id = 'untitled.' : date() : '.' : int(time()) : '.' : rk.u
         bx{k} = rk.id
      end
   end
   return

* In: ri.  The copy buffer ri owns goes (its record was saved, or the person closed the buffer, saved or discarded, or it has no changes any more)
rc.drop.tab:
   k = 'qk' : bu<ri>
   rq = bx{k}
   if rq # '' then
      call !edit_recovery('drop', rq<1, 1>, rq<1, 2>, '', rr)
      bx{k} = ''
      k = 'r' : bu<ri>
      bx{k} = ''
   end
   return

* After a second of quiet, before the screen is handed to a program, and when the IDE ends without the person's word: every buffer with changes has a copy holding
* its text, and a buffer without changes has none.  Nothing at all when the switch is off.  A failure is said once on the status line (12782) and the text stays in
* the editor; 'off' (RECOVERY-OFF since the IDE started) stops the tries.
rc.tick:
   if not(rc.on) then return
   gosub stash.current
   for ri = 1 to nbuf
      i = ri
      gosub is.dirty
      rk.pk = 'k' : bu<ri>
      if bx{rk.pk} = 'path' then continue                  ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): no recovery copy is kept of an operating system file: the store is in the person's home, the file may be anywhere
      if not(dirty) then
         gosub rc.drop.tab
      end else
         k = 't' : bu<ri>
         rt.text = bx{k}
         gosub rc.key
         rk.now = rk.file : @vm : rk.id
         k = 'qk' : bu<ri>
         rq = bx{k}
         rt.write = @true
         if rq = rk.now then
            k = 'r' : bu<ri>
            if bx{k} = rt.text then rt.write = @false     ;* the copy already holds this text
         end else
*           The tab is another record now (save as; an untitled buffer that was named), or owns no copy yet: the old one goes, and a copy somebody else
*           left under the new name (another session of the same person) is not written over.
            if rq # '' then
               call !edit_recovery('drop', rq<1, 1>, rq<1, 2>, '', rr)
               bx{k} = ''
            end
*           28 Oct 26 (TUI phase 2 review): a copy the tab does not own was found under its key once, and then the tab never wrote a copy again, even after that copy
*           was gone, and the person was not told.  Now the person is told (12783) the first time, and the copy is looked for again every RC.LOOK seconds (not
*           every second: it is a file read and a digest); once it is gone the tab writes its own.  Such a copy is another IDE session's of the same person in the
*           same account with the same record open and changed, or one left when an editor ended, under a name this tab took without the question (Save as, or
*           the first save of an untitled buffer, to that record): either holds text the person may want, so it is never written over.
            k = 'x' : bu<ri>
            rx = bx{k}
            rt.now = date() * 86400 + int(time())
            rt.seen = @false
            if rx # '' then rt.seen = (rx<1, 1> = rk.file and rx<1, 2> = rk.id)
            if rt.seen and rt.now < rx<1, 3> + 0 then
               rt.write = @false                                  ;* looked for less than RC.LOOK seconds ago
            end else
               call !edit_recovery('find', rk.file, rk.id, rt.other, rr)
               if rr # '' then
                  rt.write = @false
                  bx{k} = rk.now : @vm : rt.now + RC.LOOK
                  if not(rt.seen) then
                     i = ri
                     gosub buf.label
                     msg = sysmsg(12783, bf.name, bf.id)   ;* %1 %2 gets no recovery copy: another one is there (another session's, or older) and is kept
                  end
               end else
                  bx{k} = ''                                      ;* none there (or no longer): the tab writes its own below
               end
            end
         end
         if rt.write then
            call !edit_recovery('write', rk.file, rk.id, rt.text, rr)
            if rr = '' then
               k = 'qk' : bu<ri>
               bx{k} = rk.now
               k = 'r' : bu<ri>
               bx{k} = rt.text
               rc.warned = 0
            end else
               k = 'qk' : bu<ri>
               bx{k} = ''                                   ;* a failed write leaves no copy behind (!edit_recovery removes it)
               if rr = 'off' then
                  rc.on = 0
               end else
                  if not(rc.warned) then msg = sysmsg(12782, rr)   ;* A recovery copy could not be written (%1); the text is still in the editor
                  rc.warned = 1
               end
            end
         end
      end
   next ri
   return

* In: rc.file, rc.id (a record a tab is about to be made for), rc.has.disk and rc.disk (the record as it is on the disk).  Out: rc.res ('' when there is no whole,
* private copy), rc.choice and rc.text: 'none' (no copy; or a copy that says nothing the record does not, which is removed; or the person deleted it), 'use'
* (rc.text is the copy, for the tab to take) or 'cancel' (no tab: the copy stays).  The question is the person's; Enter is Use the copy, which writes nothing.
rc.offer:
   rc.choice = 'none'
   rc.text = ''
   call !edit_recovery('find', rc.file, rc.id, rc.text, rc.res)
   if rc.res = '' then return
   if rc.has.disk and rc.text = rc.disk then
      call !edit_recovery('drop', rc.file, rc.id, '', rr)
      rc.text = ''
      return
   end
   rc.when = oconv(rc.res<2>, 'D') : ' ' : oconv(rc.res<3>, 'MT')
   rf.w = lw - 16
   if rf.w > 100 then rf.w = 100
   call $tk_wrap(sysmsg(12777, rc.file, rc.id, rc.when), rf.w, rc.pieces)   ;* A recovery copy of %1 %2, kept on %3, holds text that was not saved...
   screen msgbox change(rc.pieces, @fm, @vm) buttons 'Use the copy,Delete the copy,Cancel' default 1 returning rc.b else rc.b = 3
   last.status = char(1)
   begin case
      case rc.b = 1
         rc.choice = 'use'
      case rc.b = 2
         call !edit_recovery('drop', rc.file, rc.id, '', rr)
         rc.text = ''
         msg = sysmsg(12779)                  ;* The recovery copy was deleted
      case 1
         rc.choice = 'cancel'
         rc.text = ''
         msg = sysmsg(12780, rc.file, rc.id)  ;* The recovery copy of %1 %2 is kept; the record was not opened
   end case
   return

* A bare IDE: the copies of untitled buffers that were kept when an editor ended are offered, each in a tab of its own (Open), deleted, or left for another time
rc.untitled:
   rc.list = ''
   call !edit_recovery('list', '', '', rc.list, rc.n)
   rc.got = @false
   for ru = 1 to rc.n
      rc.file = ''
      rc.id = rc.list<ru, 1>
      call !edit_recovery('find', rc.file, rc.id, rc.text, rc.res)
      if rc.res # '' then
         rc.when = oconv(rc.res<2>, 'D') : ' ' : oconv(rc.res<3>, 'MT')
         rf.w = lw - 16
         if rf.w > 100 then rf.w = 100
         call $tk_wrap(sysmsg(12781, rc.when), rf.w, rc.pieces)   ;* Text typed in an untitled buffer was kept on %1 when the editor ended.  Open it in a new tab?
         screen msgbox change(rc.pieces, @fm, @vm) buttons 'Open,Delete the copy,Later' default 1 returning rc.b else rc.b = 3
         last.status = char(1)
         begin case
            case rc.b = 1
               gosub drop.clean.untitled
               gosub add.untitled
               k = 't' : uid
               bx{k} = rc.text
               tm.u = uid
               tm.raw = rc.text
               gosub set.token.mode
               gosub rc.took
*              28 Oct 26 (TUI phase 2 review): the editor is made to hold this tab at once.  It still held the empty startup buffer, so the next Open's add.untitled
*              (stash.current: the editor's text into the tab in the editor) wrote that empty text over THIS copy's tab, and the next tick deleted the copy from the
*              disk as a buffer without changes.  The copy stays on the disk; it goes only by the rules of any tab's copy, now that its text is in a tab.
               gosub load.current
               rc.got = @true
            case rc.b = 2
               call !edit_recovery('drop', rc.file, rc.id, '', rr)
               msg = sysmsg(12779)
         end case
      end
   next ru
   if rc.got then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* The pane indicators: [*] on the pane that has the focus (focus.id), [-] on the others

* ind.want = the four texts, in the order of ind.panes
indicator.texts:
   ind.want = ''
   ind.at = focus.id
   if ind.at = 'fld' then ind.at = 'ed'                ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the fields view is the editor pane's other face
   for ix = 1 to 4
      ind.want<ix> = if ind.at = ind.panes<ix> then '[*]' else '[-]'
   next ix
   return

* The ones that changed are set and drawn again, alone: the form is not drawn again (f{'quiet'}), and a label that has not changed is not touched
set.indicators:
   gosub indicator.texts
   if ind.want = ind.cur then return
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   for ix = 1 to 4
      if ind.want<ix> # ind.cur<ix> then screen set f, ind.ids<ix> text ind.want<ix>
   next ix
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   for ix = 1 to 4
      if ind.want<ix> # ind.cur<ix> then call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, ind.ids<ix>, rr)
   next ix
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   ind.cur = ind.want
   return

* ======================================================================
* The AUTOLOGOUT countdown (28 Oct 26, approved by the owner).  The kernel ends a session whose AUTOLOGOUT period passes with nothing typed (keyin counts
* the time spent waiting, across the IDE's one-second waits, the waits of a dialog and of the AI line), and an IDE with unsaved changes then loses them.  The
* countdown is read from that same clock: kernel(K$AUTOLOGOUT, -2) is the time waited with nothing typed, as AUTOLOGOUT will judge it at the next wait, 0 after a
* key.  (The first version GUESSED it from what the IDE could see - a wait that took long, a change in the editor - and was late by an AI wait and by a stall.)

* Out: idl.txt, the countdown for the status line, or '' when the period is off (0) or more than IDL.WARN seconds away
idle.text:
   idl.txt = ''
   idl.al = kernel(K$AUTOLOGOUT, -1) + 0          ;* ms, as the autologout verb reads it; 0 is off
   if idl.al <= 0 then return
   idl.ms = kernel(K$AUTOLOGOUT, -2) + 0          ;* ms waited with nothing typed: the kernel's own clock
   idl.left = int((idl.al - idl.ms + 999) / 1000)
   if idl.left > IDL.WARN then return
   if idl.left < 1 then idl.left = 1
   idl.dirty = @false
   for idl.i = 1 to nbuf
      i = idl.i
      gosub is.dirty
      if dirty then idl.dirty = @true
   next idl.i
   begin case
      case not(idl.dirty)
         idl.txt = sysmsg(12757, idl.left)        ;* Idle: signing out in %1 s
      case rc.on
         idl.txt = sysmsg(12756, idl.left)        ;* ...; a recovery copy is kept
      case 1
         idl.txt = sysmsg(12755, idl.left)        ;* ...; unsaved changes will be lost
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* The status line, and the bar

status.line:
   if last.status = char(1) then last.foot = ''     ;* the screen was made again, or a dialog or a program wrote over it: the foot is written too
   gosub set.indicators
   t = ''
   if nbuf > 0 then
      gosub stash.current
      i = cb
      gosub is.dirty
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      gosub buf.label
      t = bf.name : ' ' : bf.id
      if bi<cb> = '' then t = bf.name
      if dirty then t := ' *'
      k = 'o' : bu<cb>
      if bx{k} # '' then t := '  ' : sysmsg(12692, bx{k})        ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): [read-only: user %1 holds its lock]
      k = 'z' : bu<cb>
      if bx{k} then t := '  ' : sysmsg(12741)                    ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): [read-only: this session may not change it]
      gosub fields.is.on
      if fv.on then
         if msg = '' then t := '   ' : sysmsg(15995)                   ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the keys of the fields view, where the cursor's line and column would be
      end else
         t := '   ' : sysmsg(12218, mw{'cy'}, mw{'cx'})
         if mw{'over'} + 0 then t := '  ' : sysmsg(12771)                  ;* 29 Oct 26 (S.88 D2): [overwrite]
         if mw{'macrec'} + 0 then t := '  ' : sysmsg(15055)                ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): [recording a macro]
      end
   end
   if msg # '' then t := '   ' : msg
   gosub idle.text                                               ;* 28 Oct 26: the AUTOLOGOUT countdown goes first, where it is read
   if idl.txt # '' then t = idl.txt : (if t = '' then '' else '   ' : t)
   * 18 Oct 26 dm - SCREEN TEXT leaves the cursor after the text, so the cursor stayed at the end of the status row and the editor showed none
   *   (found by the owner: "no cursor when it is first started"; the loop writes this line once a second and on every key).  It is put back
   *   where the widget with the focus had left it.
   * The line is written only when its text has changed since it was last written: the loop comes here about ten times a second, and an idle IDE
   *   wrote 140 cells each time (new.form, which makes the screen again, forgets the text so that the line is written then)
   * 19 Oct 26 dm - which pane has the focus, first on the line (the owner, using the keyboard only: "no visible indication of which box is active"):
   *   Tab moves the focus from the files to the tabs, the editor and the messages, and the terminal's own cursor is the only other sign of it
   begin case
      case focus.id = 'tree'
         pn = sysmsg(12242)
      case focus.id = 'tabs'
         pn = sysmsg(12243)
      case focus.id = 'ed' or focus.id = 'fld'
         pn = sysmsg(12244)
      case focus.id = 'err'
         pn = sysmsg(12245)
      case focus.id = 'ai'
         pn = 'AI'
      case 1
         pn = ''
   end case
   if pn # '' and zm and not(alone) then pn := ', ' : sysmsg(15023)      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): the line says the editor fills the screen
   if pn # '' then t = '[' : pn : ']' : (if t = '' then '' else '  ' : t)
   t = fmt(t[1, lw], lw : 'L')
   if t # last.status then
      last.status = t
      keep.cur = ''
      call $sl_curat('get', keep.cur)
      screen text t at 0,ystat attr 'reverse'     ;* the status row: off the screen in a smaller window
      call $sl_curat('back', keep.cur)
   end
   gosub foot.draw
   return

* The border and the function-key row under the status line (the toolkit's frame: tk.h rows H-2 and H-1, plan section 5.1).  Written only when the layout's size
* changed or the screen was made again (status.line forgets last.foot then); the row is made by $tk_fkeys from the IDE's key table (ide.keys), cut to the layout's width.
foot.draw:
   foot.geo = lw : '/' : lh
   if foot.geo = last.foot then return
   last.foot = foot.geo
   fk.ctx = ''
   fk.ctx<TK.C.W> = lw
   call $tk_fkeys(fk.ctx, ide.keys, '', fk.text)
   call $sl_line('h', 0, fk.hc)
   keep.cur = ''
   call $sl_curat('get', keep.cur)
   screen text str(fk.hc, lw) at 0,ybord
   screen text fk.text at 0,ykeys attr 'bar'
   call $sl_curat('back', keep.cur)
   return

* ======================================================================
* Keys, the menu and the commands

do.key:
   msg = ''
   cmd = ''
   begin case
      case edata = 'f1'
         cmd = 'help'
      * 28 Oct 26 dm (S.88 Phase 4, the owner's keys of 20 Oct, D28 / D31 / D32 / D33): Ctrl-S is the only save key (F2 went; it is the debugger's abort), the build keys follow Geany
      *   (F5 run, F8 compile, F9 build = compile and catalogue, F7 debug), F6 is the next pane, the tabs are Ctrl-PageDown / Ctrl-PageUp, Ctrl-N new, Ctrl-O open, Ctrl-W close.
      *   F11 (find in the records: the terminal's full screen) is a menu entry; Save as and Write a copy are menu entries.
      case edata = 'ctrl-s'
         cmd = 'save'
      case edata = 'f4'
         cmd = 'nexterr'
      case edata = 'f5' or edata = 'ctrl-l'
         cmd = 'refresh'                 ;* 8 Nov 26 (S.179): F5 and Ctrl-L draw the screen again, as on every page (F5 ran, Ctrl-L went to a line)
      case edata = 'f9'
         cmd = 'run'                     ;* S.179: F9 (it was F5)
      case edata = 'shift-f9'
         cmd = 'catalog'                 ;* S.179: Shift-F9 (it was F9)
      case edata = 'alt-g'
         cmd = 'goto'                    ;* S.179: Alt-G, as in SED (it was Ctrl-L)
      case edata = 'alt-r'
         cmd = 'macrec'                  ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): reached only with the focus outside the editor, whose memo acts on these two itself
      case edata = 'alt-p'
         cmd = 'macrun'
      case edata = 'alt-='
         cmd = 'charcode'                ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): SED's expand char
      case edata = 'ctrl-t'
         cmd = 'theme'                   ;* S.179: the theme, kept for the person, as on every page
      case edata = 'ctrl-k'
         cmd = 'keys'                    ;* S.179: the keys of the IDE, as on every page
      case edata = 'f6'
         cmd = 'nextpane'
      case edata = 'shift-f6'
         cmd = 'prevpane'                ;* F3 is find next in the editor (the owner's D31), so the tree is reached by the panes' ring, the Go menu and the mouse
      case edata = 'f7'
         cmd = 'debug'
      case edata = 'shift-f7'
         cmd = 'bptoggle'                ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the breakpoint mark of the cursor's line
      case edata = 'f8'
         cmd = 'compile'
      case edata = 'ctrl-pgdn' or edata = 'alt-pgdn'
         cmd = 'nexttab'                 ;* GNOME Terminal keeps Ctrl-PageUp / PageDown for its own tabs: Alt-PageUp / PageDown are the same keys with another modifier (Opus, 28 Oct)
      case edata = 'ctrl-pgup' or edata = 'alt-pgup'
         cmd = 'prevtab'
      case edata = 'f10'
         cmd = 'menu'
      case edata = 'ctrl-q'
         cmd = 'quit'
      case edata = 'ctrl-w'
         cmd = 'close'
      case edata = 'ctrl-n'
         cmd = 'new'
      case edata = 'ctrl-o'
         cmd = 'open'
      case edata = 'ctrl-e'
         cmd = 'zoom'                    ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): the editor fills the screen, or the files and messages come back (the editor leaves this key to the IDE: passkeys)
      case edata = 'ctrl-r'
         cmd = 'replace'                 ;* SED's replace
      case edata = 'f12'
         cmd = 'fields'                  ;* the record as fields, values and subvalues, in CED
      case edata = 'guardquit'
         gosub rc.tick                   ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): the copies are kept, as for a hang-up: the person could not see the buffers to decide
         done = @true                    ;* the user left from the cursor lock ($sl_guardmode): nothing is saved
      case edata = 'mouse'
         * The bar is not a widget, so a click on it comes here as a key the form had no use for.  19 Oct 26 dm: the left button on the bar's
         * row (0) opens the menu whose title is under it; the menu was opened only by F10 or Esc before, and a click did nothing until then.
         call $sl_mouse('get', mev)
         if mev<1> = 'press' and mev<2> = 1 and mev<4> = 0 then
            menu.click = mev<3>
            cmd = 'menu'
         end
      case focus.id = 'fld'
         * 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the keys the IDE has no use of go to the fields view, last, so that every IDE key (F1, Ctrl-S, F4-F10, Ctrl-N / O / W / Q / E / L / R, F12) wins
         fz.key = edata
         gosub fields.key
   end case
   if cmd # '' then gosub run.cmd
   return

run.cmd:
   if in.ptr # '' and nbuf > 0 then
      if bn<cb> = in.ptr and (cmd = 'catalog' or cmd = 'run' or cmd = 'debug' or cmd = 'debug1') then
         gosub master.build
         return
      end
   end
   begin case
      case cmd = 'menu'
         mk = menu.click
         menu.click = ''
         if mk = '' then
            screen menubar menus returning mc then
               cmd = mc
               gosub run.cmd
            end
         end else
            screen menubar menus click mk returning mc then      ;* the menu under the click; the space beside the titles opens nothing
               cmd = mc
               gosub run.cmd
            end
         end
         gosub draw.bar                    ;* S.99: the menu closed and the library drew its bar again, without the name at the right
      case cmd = 'help'
         s = convert(char(13), '', sysmsg(12219) : sysmsg(12220) : sysmsg(12221))
         s = change(s, char(10), @vm)
         screen msgbox s returning b else b = 0
      case cmd = 'save'
         gosub save.current
      case cmd = 'tree'
         if zm then gosub zoom.toggle      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): the files are hidden while zoomed: Go > Files and records shows them again
         ft.id = 'tree'                    ;* SCREEN FOCUS moves the pointer and draws nothing: the form's own op shows the change
         gosub focus.to
      case cmd = 'nexterr'
         gosub next.error
      case cmd = 'nexttab'
         if nbuf > 1 then
            new.cb = cb + 1
            if new.cb > nbuf then new.cb = 1
            gosub switch.to
         end
      case cmd = 'prevtab'
         if nbuf > 1 then
            new.cb = cb - 1
            if new.cb < 1 then new.cb = nbuf
            gosub switch.to
         end
      case cmd = 'nextpane' or cmd = 'prevpane'
         * F6 and Shift-F6: the focus goes round the panes in the order the status line and the [*] marks name them (S.176: the only box keys; Tab does not move the focus)
         gosub fields.is.on
         pane.ring = 'tree' : @fm : 'tabs' : @fm : (if fv.on then 'fld' else 'ed') : (if err.rows # '' then @fm : 'err' else '')      ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the editor pane is the fields table while the tab shows its fields; 10 Nov 26 (S.200): an empty messages box is not a stop
         if zm then pane.ring = 'tabs' : @fm : (if fv.on then 'fld' else 'ed') : (if al.err then @fm : 'err' else '')                       ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): zoomed, the files and the messages are hidden
         if ai.on then pane.ring<-1> = 'ai'
         locate focus.id in pane.ring<1> setting pane.at else pane.at = 0
         if cmd = 'nextpane' then pane.at += 1 else pane.at -= 1
         if pane.at > dcount(pane.ring, @fm) then pane.at = 1
         if pane.at < 1 then pane.at = dcount(pane.ring, @fm)
         ft.id = pane.ring<pane.at>
         gosub focus.to
      case cmd = 'open'
         gosub do.open
      case cmd = 'compile'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
      case cmd = 'catalog'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'catalog ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i capturing co
            gosub catalog.verified
         end
      case cmd = 'run'
         c.opt = ''
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            gosub leave.screen
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'run ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i : ra.args trapping aborts      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): ra.args is Run with arguments' words, '' for Run
            gosub retake.screen
         end
      case cmd = 'runargs'
         * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): SED's RUN with arguments - the words are typed and put after the program's name, as RUN takes them (the program reads them with SENTENCE())
         screen ask sysmsg(15025) width 60 value ra.last returning ra.text else ra.text = char(0)
         if ra.text # char(0) then
            ra.text = trim(ra.text)
            if convert(@fm : @vm : @sm : @tm, '', ra.text) # ra.text then
               msg = sysmsg(15026)
            end else
               ra.last = ra.text
               if ra.text # '' then ra.args = ' ' : ra.text
               cmd = 'run'
               gosub run.cmd
               ra.args = ''
            end
         end
      case cmd = 'stamp'
         gosub do.stamp
      case cmd = 'expandtabs'
         gosub do.expandtabs
      case cmd = 'charcode'
         gosub do.charcode
      case cmd = 'synswitch' or cmd = 'lnumswitch' or cmd = 'mouseswitch'
         gosub do.viewswitch
      case cmd = 'debug1'
         * 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): "Debug from the first line" stops at the first line as F7 always did; F7 (debug) runs on to the first breakpoint when the tab has marks
         bk.first = @true
         cmd = 'debug'
         gosub run.cmd
         bk.first = @false                ;* (a compile that failed leaves it set)
      case cmd = 'debug'
         c.opt = ' debugging'
         gosub do.compile
         if c.ok then
            gosub bk.arm
            bk.first = @false
            gosub leave.screen
            qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
            execute 'debug ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i trapping aborts
            gosub bk.take
            gosub retake.screen
         end
      case cmd = 'bptoggle'
         gosub bk.toggle
      case cmd = 'bpclear'
         gosub bk.clear
      case cmd = 'close'
         gosub close.current
      case cmd = 'zoom'
         gosub zoom.toggle
      case cmd = 'fields'
         gosub do.fields
      case cmd = 'dform'
         gosub do.dform                  ;* 31 Oct 26 (S.90 stage 2): a dictionary item as a full-screen form
      case cmd = 'include'
         gosub do.include                ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): Go / Open the include (a menu entry; no key: a terminal sends Ctrl-Enter as Return)
      case cmd = 'quit'
         gosub do.quit
      case cmd = 'goto'
         gosub do.goto                      ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage D): a line, a line and column, or field, value, subvalue
      case cmd = 'find'
         gosub do.find
      case cmd = 'writeas'
         gosub do.writeas
      case cmd = 'spool'
         gosub do.spool
      case cmd = 'listrecs'
         gosub do.listrecs.menu
      case cmd = 'filter'
         gosub do.filter
      case cmd = 'saveas'
         gosub do.saveas
      case cmd = 'replace'
         gosub do.replace
      case cmd = 'view'
         gosub do.view                    ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage D): the editing commands of the Search, Build and View menus
      case cmd = 'qreplace'
         gosub do.qreplace
      case cmd = 'findopts'
         gosub do.findopts
      case cmd = 'upcase'
         dc.up = 1
         gosub do.case
      case cmd = 'downcase'
         dc.up = 0
         gosub do.case
      case cmd = 'import'
         gosub do.import
      case cmd = 'export'
         gosub do.export
      case cmd = 'xeq'
         gosub do.xeq
      case cmd = 'xeqterm'
         gosub do.xeqterm                 ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): Build > Run a command at the terminal (SED's command line)
      case cmd = 'nextsel'
         gosub do.nextsel                 ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): Go > Next record from the list
      case cmd = 'tablist'
         gosub do.tablist                 ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): View > List of tabs (SED's list of buffers)
      case cmd[1, 5] = 'tool.'
         gosub do.tool                    ;* 3 Nov 26 (S.75 DV10): Tools > a development page, through the terminal hand-over
      * 8 Nov 26 (S.180): the Edit menu.  Each entry hands the key it names to the widget that owns it - the editor, or the fields pane - so a choice
      *   does exactly what the key does there (the read-only test, the undo steps and the clipboard are the widget's own)
      case cmd = 'undo' or cmd = 'redo' or cmd = 'cut' or cmd = 'copy' or cmd = 'paste' or cmd = 'selall'
         ek.key = (if cmd = 'undo' then 'ctrl-z' else if cmd = 'redo' then 'ctrl-y' else if cmd = 'cut' then 'ctrl-x' else if cmd = 'copy' then 'ctrl-c' else if cmd = 'paste' then 'ctrl-v' else 'ctrl-a')
         gosub do.editkey
      case cmd = 'wordback' or cmd = 'killend' or cmd = 'transpose' or cmd = 'wordup' or cmd = 'worddown' or cmd = 'wordcap' or cmd = 'closesp' or cmd = 'align' or cmd = 'swapmark' or cmd = 'nudgeup' or cmd = 'nudgedown' or cmd = 'macrec' or cmd = 'macrun'
         * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 2): SED's editing commands, the editor's own keys (sl_memo sed.edit / sed.move); not the fields pane's, where Alt-T is the type
         *   (stage 3b: and View > Scroll the view up / down a line, SED's nudge, which the memo has on Ctrl-Up and Ctrl-Down)
         if focus.id = 'fld' then
            msg = sysmsg(15024)
         end else
            sk.l = 'wordback' : @vm : 'killend' : @vm : 'transpose' : @vm : 'wordup' : @vm : 'worddown' : @vm : 'wordcap' : @vm : 'closesp' : @vm : 'align' : @vm : 'swapmark' : @vm : 'nudgeup' : @vm : 'nudgedown' : @vm : 'macrec' : @vm : 'macrun'
            locate cmd in sk.l<1,1> setting sk.i else sk.i = 0     ;* stage 3b: <1,1>, the values (in <1> it found nothing and every entry sent Alt-Backspace)
            ek.key = field('alt-bs,alt-e,alt-t,alt-u,alt-l,alt-c,alt- ,alt-a,alt-m,ctrl-up,ctrl-down,alt-r,alt-p', ',', sk.i)
            gosub do.editkey
         end
      case cmd = 'finsert' or cmd = 'fdelete' or cmd = 'frename' or cmd = 'ftype'
         if focus.id = 'fld' then
            fz.key = (if cmd = 'finsert' then 'ins' else if cmd = 'fdelete' then 'del' else if cmd = 'frename' then 'f3' else 'alt-t')
            gosub fields.key
         end else
            msg = sysmsg(15020)                      ;* The field entries work in the fields pane (F12).
         end
      case cmd = 'overwrite'
         gosub do.overwrite
      case cmd = 'autoindent'
         gosub do.autoindent
      case cmd = 'tabwidth'
         gosub do.tabwidth
      case cmd = 'format'
         gosub do.format
      case cmd = 'refresh'
         gosub do.refresh
      case cmd = 'new'
         gosub do.new
      case cmd = 'ai.error'
         gosub ai.error
      case cmd = 'ai.sel'
         gosub ai.selection
      case cmd = 'ai.ask'
         if nbuf = 0 then
            msg = sysmsg(12667)
         end else
            ft.id = 'ai'                  ;* the entry has the focus: the person types the request there
            gosub focus.to
            msg = sysmsg(12660)
         end
      case cmd = 'theme'
         * 8 Nov 26 (S.179): the routine every page uses ($tk_theme): the library's list and order, only the themes this terminal can show, kept for the person
         call $tk_theme(1, th.chg, th.msg)
         call $sl_theme('name', '', '', '', '', th.want)          ;* what is in force now: retake.quiet puts it on again after SED or a run
         screen refresh
         last.status = char(1)          ;* everything was drawn again, the status line too
         msg = th.msg
      case cmd = 'keys'
         call $tk_std('keylist', '', ide.keys, '', th.kr)         ;* 8 Nov 26 (S.179): the IDE's keys, in a box, as on every page
   end case
   return

* Return in the tree, the table or the tab strip (the form's default button)
do.act:
   did.focus = @false
   begin case
      case focus.id = 'tree'
         screen get f, 'tree' returning code
         begin case
            case code[1, 2] = 'F:'
               lf.name = code[3, 99]
               lp = 0
               nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
               if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
               for q = 1 to nl
                  if lfiles<q> = lf.name then
                     lp = q
                     exit
                  end
               next q
               if lp then
                  del lfiles<lp>
                  del lrecs<lp>
                  del ldict<lp>
                  del lmore<lp>
               end else
                  gosub load.file
               end
               cur.code = code
               gosub tree.apply
               gosub redraw.tree
            case code[1, 2] = 'D:'
               * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the dictionary node: its items are read now (the first time), and Return again closes it
               ld.name = code[3, 99]
               lp = 0
               nl = dcount(dfiles, @fm)
               if dfiles = '' then nl = 0
               for q = 1 to nl
                  if dfiles<q> = ld.name then
                     lp = q
                     exit
                  end
               next q
               if lp then
                  del dfiles<lp>
                  del drecs<lp>
               end else
                  gosub load.dict
               end
               cur.code = code
               gosub tree.apply
               gosub redraw.tree
            case code[1, 2] = 'N:' or code[1, 2] = 'B:'
               * 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 5): the next or the previous MAXREC records of the file, in place of those shown
               lf.name = code[3, 99]
               lt.k = 'F:' : lf.name
               gosub filter.slot
               lt.page<lt.p> = (lt.page<lt.p> + 0) + (if code[1, 2] = 'N:' then 1 else -1)
               if lt.page<lt.p> < 0 then lt.page<lt.p> = 0
               gosub load.file
               cur.code = (if lt.page<lt.p> + 0 then 'B:' else 'F:') : lf.name      ;* the cursor on the new page, which the file's own row may be a screen away from
               gosub tree.apply
               gosub redraw.tree
            case code[1, 2] = 'O:'
               * 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): a directory of the operating system: its entries are listed (!ide_osfile 'list') the first time, and Return again closes it and everything listed under it
               os.dir = code[3, 9999]
               gosub os.find
               if os.q then
                  gosub os.collapse
               end else
                  gosub os.load
               end
               cur.code = code
               gosub tree.apply
               gosub redraw.tree
            case code[1, 2] = 'P:'
               op.path = code[3, 9999]                     ;* a file of the operating system: opened in a tab of its own, as Open with a path: name does
               cur.code = code
               gosub open.path
               if nbuf > 0 then
                  ft.id = 'ed'
                  gosub focus.to
                  did.focus = @true
                  gosub redraw.tabs
               end
            case code[1, 2] = 'R:' or code[1, 2] = 'Q:'
               or.file = field(code, ':', 2)
               or.id = code[len(or.file) + 4, 9999]
               if code[1, 2] = 'Q:' then or.dict = 'DICT'      ;* an item of the file's dictionary
               cur.code = code
               * 18 Oct 26 dm - the editor is drawn once: by the focus that follows, not also by open.record (which drew the whole form, and then
               *   again at the end of this routine).  SCREEN FOCUS only moves the form's pointer and draws nothing, so the focus goes through the
               *   form's own op, which draws the widget that had it (the tree) and the one that has it (the editor, with the cursor in its text).
               lc.nodraw = @true
               dq.u0 = uid
               gosub open.record
               lc.nodraw = @false
               if nbuf > 0 then
                  ft.id = 'ed'
                  gosub focus.to
                  did.focus = @true
                  gosub redraw.tabs
               end
               if code[1, 2] = 'Q:' and uid > dq.u0 then gosub dform.default      ;* 31 Oct 26 (S.90): an item that was not open yet is shown as the form; one already in a tab is only gone to
         end case
      case focus.id = 'err'
         screen get f, 'err' returning rn
         if rn # '' then
            k2 = rn + 0
            gosub act.error
         end
      case focus.id = 'ai'
         * Return in the AI entry: the request is what was typed; the whole record is the text, the page says where the cursor is
         ai.req = edata
         ai.kind = 'ask'
         gosub ai.go
      case focus.id = 'fld'
         fz.key = 'enter'                  ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): Return in the fields table opens the element (the engine's dive / edit)
         gosub fields.key
      case 1
         null
   end case
   if not(did.focus) then screen focus f, focus.id
   return

* The row k2 of the table: go to what it names
act.error:
   if k2 < 1 or k2 > dcount(err.rows, @fm) then return
   if out.kind = 'search' then
      or.dict = out.dict                ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a select list of dictionary items lists items
      or.file = out.file
      or.id = sid<k2>
      gosub open.record
      gl.n = sln<k2> + 0
   end else
      gl.n = err.lines<k2> + 0
   end
   if gl.n >= 1 and nbuf > 0 then
      gosub goto.line
   end
   return

* In: gl.n.  The cursor of the editor goes to that line, which is brought to the middle of the window
goto.line:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   nl = dcount(mw{'value'}, @fm)
   if gl.n > nl then gl.n = nl
   if gl.n < 1 then gl.n = 1
   mw{'cy'} = gl.n
   mw{'cx'} = 1
   mw{'want'} = 1
   mw{'left'} = 1
   mw{'mx'} = 0
   mw{'my'} = 0
   tp = gl.n - int(mh / 2)
   if tp < 1 then tp = 1
   mw{'top'} = tp
   ft.id = 'ed'                         ;* the widget that had the focus and the editor are drawn, the cursor in the editor
   gosub focus.to
   return

* F4: the row after the current one of the table, and what it names
next.error:
   n = dcount(err.rows, @fm)
   if err.rows = '' then n = 0
   if n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   screen get f, 'err' returning rn
   k2 = rn + 1
   if k2 > n then k2 = 1
   gosub select.row
   gosub act.error
   return

* The table's current row is k2, shown near the middle
select.row:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'err', tn)
   tt = f{'widgets', tn}
   tt{'cur'} = k2
   tt{'value'} = k2
   tp = k2 - int((eh - 2) / 2)
   if tp < 1 then tp = 1
   tt{'top'} = tp
   gosub redraw
   return

* The table shows err.rows
err.load:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'err', tn)
   tt = f{'widgets', tn}
   tt{'rows'} = err.rows
   tt{'top'} = 1
   tt{'cur'} = 1
   tt{'value'} = 1
   tt{'picked'} = ''
   call $sl_widget_init(tt)
   gosub redraw
   return

* ======================================================================
* Saving, closing, quitting

* Save the record in the editor
save.current:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      sv.ok = @false
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   sv.i = cb
   gosub save.one
   return

* edit.check  -  may this session save a change to the file in fv?  (17 Oct 26 dm, S.64)
*   IDE is $internal, so the kernel's own test of a WRITE to a VOC, a dictionary or a program file lets it through; it asks for itself,
*   by the pathname of the OPEN file (K$EDIT.OK).  The reason goes in msg, as every IDE complaint does (DISPLAY would write over the
*   screen).  Out: edit.ok.
edit.check:
   edit.ok = @true
   edit.p = fileinfo(fv, FL$PATH)
   if kernel(K$EDIT.OK, edit.p) then return
   edit.ok = @false
   edit.kind = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, edit.p)
   msg = edit_msg(edit.kind) ;* 12410 VOC, 12411 dictionary, 12412 program file, 12518 shared record, 12519 shared structure
   return

* The text of a refusal, whole, in a box that stays until a key is pressed (the status line is one row and cuts it).  In: rf.text
refuse.box:
   rf.w = lw - 16
   if rf.w > 100 then rf.w = 100
   call $tk_wrap(rf.text, rf.w, rf.pieces)
   rf.body = change(rf.pieces, @fm, @vm)
   screen msgbox rf.body buttons '&OK' default 1 returning rf.b else rf.b = 0
   last.status = char(1)
   return

* In: sv.i.  Out: sv.ok.  An untitled buffer first asks for its file and record (ide_name); then the record is locked, read again, compared with what the
* buffer was read as, and written ON ERROR.  The lock is given back on every path.  28 Oct 26 (S.88 Phase 4, I6, D1): the write used to abort the IDE when the kernel refused it.
save.one:
   sv.ok = @false
   ro.i = sv.i
   gosub ro.check                    ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a read-only tab (another session holds the record's lock) is not saved
   if not(ro.ok) then
      rf.text = msg
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   if bi<sv.i> = '' then
      sv.osdone = @false
      gosub name.untitled
      if not(sv.named) then return
      if sv.osdone then
         sv.ok = @true                       ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): an operating system file, made with the text; os.convert said "Saved"
         return
      end
   end
   u = bu<sv.i>
   k = 't' : u
   sv.text = bx{k}
   k = 's' : u
   sv.saved = bx{k}
   k = 'n' : u
   sv.new = bx{k}
   k = 'd' : u
   sv.dict = bx{k}
   k = 'k' : u
   sv.kind = bx{k}
   k = 'l' : u
   sv.slot = bx{k} + 0
   i = sv.i
   gosub buf.label
   sv.name = bf.name
   sv.id = bi<sv.i>                     ;* 29 Oct 26: set here, before coll.value, which says it in its box (12748); it was set after the record was opened and the box stopped the IDE: "Unassigned variable SV.ID"
   if not(sv.new) and (sv.text : '|') = (sv.saved : '|') then
      sv.ok = @true
      msg = sysmsg(12976)
      return
   end
   if sv.kind = 'path' then
      gosub save.path                  ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file is read again and written in place, not written as a record
      return
   end
   sv.out = sv.text                     ;* what is written: the text, or (stage F) the collection the text of a collection file's record parses to
   if sv.kind = 'coll' then
      gosub coll.value
      if not(cv.ok) then return
      sv.out = cv.y
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a tab that holds its record's lock saves through the file variable the lock was taken through: a READU through another one would move the
   *   lock to it (it is this session's), and the close of that one would then give it away.  The tab keeps the lock after the save (WRITEU).
   if sv.slot then
      fv = tfv(sv.slot)
   end else
      open sv.dict, bn<sv.i> to fv else
         msg = sysmsg(12205, sv.name)
         return
      end
   end
   gosub edit.check                  ;* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64; the dictionary's own path for a dictionary item (dict.edit), the VOC's for a VOC record (voc.edit)
   if not(edit.ok) then
      rf.text = msg
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   readu disk from fv, sv.id locked
      msg = sysmsg(12978, sv.name, sv.id)
      return
   end then
      if sv.kind = 'dict' then
         dt.rec = disk
         gosub dict.trim                     ;* the tab holds the item without its compiled code: so is the disk record compared
         disk = dt.rec
      end
      if sv.kind = 'coll' then
         * 29 Oct 26 (stage F): the record as the tab read it, in the text area's form (see coll.text).  JBUILD of anything that is not a collection or an array STOPS the program (message 12126); a
         *   READ of a collection file gives one of those or fails, but a read trigger could hand back a string: that is a change, so the question is asked (review R8).
         if jtype(disk) = 'collection' or jtype(disk) = 'array' then
            disk = convert(char(10), @fm, jbuild(disk, JB$PRETTY))
         end else
            disk = ''
         end
      end
      if (disk : '|') # (sv.saved : '|') then      ;* as text: "1" and "1.0" are one number, two texts (review R8)
         screen msgbox sysmsg(12208, sv.name, sv.id) buttons 'Overwrite,Cancel' default 2 returning b else b = 0
         if b # 1 then
            if not(sv.slot) then release fv, sv.id
            return
         end
      end
   end else
      null
   end
   gosub source.control.hook         ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 4): before the write, as SED and ED do: it may refuse the write or change the text
   if not(srcc.ok) then return
   if sv.slot then
      writeu sv.out on fv, sv.id on error
         sv.err = status()
         msg = sysmsg(12977, sv.name, sv.id, sv.err)
         return
      end
   end else
      write sv.out on fv, sv.id on error
         sv.err = status()
         release fv, sv.id
         msg = sysmsg(12977, sv.name, sv.id, sv.err)
         return
      end
      release fv, sv.id
   end
   sv.reload = srcc.update              ;* source.control changed the text: the editor shows what was written
   if sv.kind = 'coll' then
      * 29 Oct 26 (stage F): the tab keeps the record in the one layout JBUILD gives it, so that the next save compares like with like and the tab is not "changed" because it was typed compact
      sv.shown = sv.text
      sv.text = convert(char(10), @fm, jbuild(sv.out, JB$PRETTY))
      k = 't' : u
      bx{k} = sv.text
      if (sv.text : '|') # (sv.shown : '|') then sv.reload = @true        ;* review R8: the editor is loaded again only when the layout changed under the person's hands (its undo steps go with that)
   end
   k = 's' : u
   bx{k} = sv.text
   k = 'n' : u
   bx{k} = 0
   sv.ok = @true
   ri = sv.i
   gosub rc.drop.tab                            ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): the text is in the record now; its recovery copy goes
   msg = sysmsg(12207, sv.name, sv.id)
   if sv.new then
      rt.file = bn<sv.i>
      rt.dict = sv.dict
      gosub refresh.tree.for                   ;* the record is in the file now: the tree lists it
   end
   if sv.reload and sv.i = cb then gosub load.current              ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): the editor shows the layout that was kept
   return

* In: sv.i, u, sv.dict, sv.id, sv.text, sv.kind, sv.slot, fv (the record's lock is held).  Out: srcc.ok, srcc.update; sv.text, sv.out and the tab's text when the subroutine changed the text.
*   9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 4).  Nothing at all happens when no source.control is catalogued.  It may write to the terminal, as under SED, so the screen is drawn again after it.  A collection
*   file's record is given to it as its JSON text, and text it changes must still parse.
source.control.hook:
   srcc.ok = @true
   srcc.update = @false
   if srcc.active < 0 then srcc.active = catalogued(srcc.name)
   if not(srcc.active) then return
   srcc.allowed = @true
   call @srcc.name((sv.dict), (bn<sv.i>), (sv.id), sv.text, 1, srcc.allowed, srcc.update)
   prompt ''
   screen refresh
   last.status = char(1)
   if not(srcc.allowed) then
      if not(sv.slot) then release fv, sv.id
      srcc.ok = @false
      rf.text = sysmsg(6653)
      msg = rf.text
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   if srcc.update then
      k = 't' : u
      bx{k} = sv.text
      if sv.kind = 'coll' then
         gosub coll.value
         if not(cv.ok) then
            if not(sv.slot) then release fv, sv.id
            srcc.ok = @false
            return
         end
         sv.out = cv.y
      end else
         sv.out = sv.text
      end
   end
   return

* In: sv.text, sv.name, sv.id.  Out: cv.ok, cv.y (the collection or array the text is).  JPARSE answers a null string and STATUS() 1020 (INMAT() the character it gave up at) for text that is
*   not a JSON object or array; that is said in a box (12748) and nothing is written.  (29 Oct 26, stage F.)
coll.value:
   * The tab's text has its lines joined by field marks (coll.text); JPARSE reads a field mark between tokens as white space but BYTE 254 INSIDE a string or a name as data, so a Return typed in the
   *   middle of a string put a mark byte in the value.  The line feeds go back first: between tokens they are white space, inside a string the parse stops (12748) and says where.  (Review of the fixes, CT1.)
   cv.y = jparse(convert(@fm, char(10), sv.text))
   cv.st = status()
   cv.at = inmat()
   cv.ok = (cv.st = 0)
   if cv.ok then cv.ok = (jtype(cv.y) = 'collection' or jtype(cv.y) = 'array')
   if not(cv.ok) then
      rf.text = sysmsg(12748, sv.name, sv.id, cv.at)
      msg = rf.text
      gosub refuse.box
   end
   return

* In: sv.i, u, sv.text, sv.saved, sv.slot, sv.name (a tab of kind 'path' whose text has changed).  Out: sv.ok.  The kernel's gates were asked when the file was opened, through the
*   file variable the tab keeps, and are asked again by the write itself (a file opened read-only is not written: ro.check).  The file is read once more through that variable and
*   compared with what the tab read - the text, and whether its lines end with CR LF and whether the last one had its end - and a difference (or a file that is not text now) is the
*   overwrite question, as for a record.  Then !ide_osfile writes it IN PLACE: the same inode, owner, mode and links, no truncation until the new text is written.  If the write fails
*   the tab keeps its text and its old baseline, so the next save asks again, and the message says the file may be partly written.
save.path:
   sv.id = bi<sv.i>
   k = 'p' : u
   sv.pinfo = bx{k}
   if sv.slot = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12739, sv.id, 0)
      return
   end
   call !ide_osfile('reread', sv.id, tfv(sv.slot), sp.text, sp.info, sp.why)
   if sp.why = 'gone' then
      * 28 Oct 26 (review, finding 2a): another program removed the file or renamed another over it; the tab holds the old inode, and a write would "succeed" into a file nobody can reach
      rf.text = sysmsg(12747, sv.name, sv.id)
      msg = rf.text
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   * the texts are compared as STRINGS ((x : '|') = (y : '|')): = and # compare two numbers as numbers, so "1.0" and "1" are the same to them (review, finding 6)
   sp.same = (sp.why = '')
   if sp.same then sp.same = ((sp.text : '|') = (sv.saved : '|') and sp.info<1> + 0 = sv.pinfo<1> + 0 and sp.info<2> + 0 = sv.pinfo<2> + 0)
   if not(sp.same) then
      screen msgbox sysmsg(12208, sv.name, sv.id) buttons 'Overwrite,Cancel' default 2 returning b else b = 0
      if b # 1 then return
   end
   sp.winfo = sv.pinfo
   sp.sent = sv.text
   call !ide_osfile('save', sv.id, tfv(sv.slot), sv.text, sp.winfo, sp.why)
   begin case
      case sp.why = ''
         * the helper gave SV.TEXT and SP.WINFO back as the next reread gives them (a text ending in an empty line is written with that line end, and reads back without the line)
         k = 's' : u
         bx{k} = sv.text
         k = 'p' : u
         bx{k} = (sp.winfo<1> + 0) : @fm : (sp.winfo<2> + 0)
         if (sv.text : '|') # (sp.sent : '|') then
            k = 't' : u
            bx{k} = sv.text
            if sv.i = cb then gosub load.current
         end
         sv.ok = @true
         msg = sysmsg(12207, sv.name, sv.id)
      case sp.why = 'ro'
         rf.text = sysmsg(12742, sv.name, sv.id)                ;* 30 Oct 26 (S.88 9g): in a box
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
      case sp.why = 'bytes' or sp.why = 'big'
         rf.text = sysmsg(12746, sv.name, sv.id)
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
      case 1
         rf.text = sysmsg(12743, sv.name, sv.id, sp.winfo<6>)
         msg = rf.text
         gosub refuse.box
   end case
   return

* In: sv.i, an untitled buffer.  Out: sv.named.  The form asks for the file and the record; the buffer becomes that record (not yet on the disk: the save that follows writes it)
name.untitled:
   sv.named = @false
   nm.mode = 'save'
   nm.file = ''
   gosub default.file
   nm.file = df
   nm.id = ''
   call $ide_name(nm.mode, nm.file, nm.id, nm.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if nm.res # 'ok' then return
   if nm.file = 'path:' then
      * 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): the first save of an untitled buffer to an operating system file: the file is made with the text and the buffer becomes its tab; save.one has nothing more to do
      oc.i = sv.i
      oc.path = nm.id
      gosub os.convert
      if oc.ok then
         sv.named = @true
         sv.osdone = @true
      end
      return
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a record another tab holds (a new one, not yet on the disk, which the form cannot see) is not named twice
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = nm.file
   tf.id = nm.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, nm.file, nm.id)            ;* %1 %2 is open in another tab of the IDE: save it there
      return
   end
   bn<sv.i> = nm.file
   bi<sv.i> = nm.id
   tu.i = sv.i
   gosub tab.lock                                   ;* the record is locked for its creation and the tab keeps the lock; its kind is the file's
   tm.u = bu<sv.i>
   k = 't' : tm.u
   tm.raw = bx{k}
   gosub set.token.mode
   gosub tab.named
   sv.named = @true
   return

* The tab sv.i has just been given a file and a record: its tab label, its syntax and the tree follow
tab.named:
   if sv.i = cb then
      gosub load.current
   end
   return

* In: rt.file.  If that file's records are listed in the tree they are read again (a record has just been made in it) and the tree is drawn
refresh.tree.for:
   rt.keep = i                                  ;* the callers may be in a loop on i (do.quit); tree.nodes uses it
   rt.d = rt.dict                               ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): 'DICT': the item was made in the file's dictionary (taken here, and put back to '')
   rt.dict = ''
   lp = 0
   if rt.d # '' then
      nl = dcount(dfiles, @fm)
      if dfiles = '' then nl = 0
      for q = 1 to nl
         if dfiles<q> = rt.file then
            lp = q
            exit
         end
      next q
   end else
      nl = dcount(lfiles, @fm)
      if lfiles = '' then nl = 0
      for q = 1 to nl
         if lfiles<q> = rt.file then
            lp = q
            exit
         end
      next q
   end
   if lp then
      if rt.d # '' then
         ld.name = rt.file
         gosub load.dict
      end else
         lf.name = rt.file
         gosub load.file
      end
      gosub tree.apply
      gosub redraw.tree
   end
   i = rt.keep
   return

* A new untitled buffer, in the editor.  The number is the buffer's own: "Untitled 1", "Untitled 2", and it is not used again
add.untitled:
   gosub stash.current
   untitled += 1
   nbuf += 1
   uid += 1
   bn<nbuf> = ''
   bi<nbuf> = ''
   bu<nbuf> = uid
   k = 's' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 't' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'c' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'm' : uid
   bx{k} = 0
   k = 'n' : uid
   bx{k} = 1                         ;* not on the disk
   k = 'u' : uid
   bx{k} = untitled
   gosub rc.init.tab
   ts.kind = 'source'                ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a buffer with no record is text, and holds no lock
   ts.dict = ''
   ts.slot = 0
   ts.owner = ''
   gosub tab.state
   cb = nbuf
   return

* In: nm.file, nm.id.  A record that is not on the disk yet, in a tab of its own: nothing is written until it is saved.  Out: an.made.
* 28 Oct 26 (D2): a recovery copy kept for that record is offered first; Cancel makes no tab and keeps the copy (an.made is false).
add.new.record:
   an.made = @false
   rc.file = nm.file
   rc.id = nm.id
   rc.has.disk = @false
   rc.disk = ''
   gosub rc.offer
   if rc.choice = 'cancel' then return
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the new record is locked for its creation, as SED locks it, and the tab keeps the lock
   lk.dict = ''
   lk.file = nm.file
   lk.id = nm.id
   gosub lock.take
   if lk.err # '' then
      msg = lk.err
      return
   end
   if lk.exists then
      gosub lock.free
      msg = sysmsg(12222, nm.id, nm.file)        ;* made by another session since the name form looked
      return
   end
   if lk.owner # '' then
      msg = sysmsg(12691, nm.file, nm.id, lk.owner)
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   gosub drop.clean.untitled
   nbuf += 1
   uid += 1
   bn<nbuf> = nm.file
   bi<nbuf> = nm.id
   bu<nbuf> = uid
   k = 's' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 't' : uid
   bx{k} = rc.text                   ;* '' unless the person took the copy
   if lk.kind = 'coll' and rc.choice # 'use' then bx{k} = '{}'      ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a new record of a collection file starts as an empty collection
   k = 'c' : uid
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'm' : uid
   bx{k} = 0
   k = 'n' : uid
   bx{k} = 1
   gosub rc.init.tab
   ts.kind = lk.kind
   ts.dict = ''
   ts.slot = lk.slot
   ts.owner = ''
   gosub tab.state
   tm.u = uid
   tm.raw = rc.text
   if rc.choice # 'use' then gosub set.token.mode     ;* the kind decides the token form (a copy taken sets it below)
   if rc.choice = 'use' then
      tm.u = uid
      tm.raw = rc.text
      gosub set.token.mode
      gosub rc.took
   end
   cb = nbuf
   an.made = @true
   return

* The tab in the editor, when it is an untitled buffer nobody has typed in, is taken away (a record that is opened or made takes its place).
* 28 Oct 26 (TUI phase 2 review): a recovery copy the tab owns goes with it (the person emptied the buffer; with the switch off, or within the quiet second,
*   no tick had dropped it), so the next bare IDE does not offer text the person deleted
drop.clean.untitled:
   if nbuf > 0 and bi<cb> = '' then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 't' : u
      if bx{k} = '' then
         ri = cb
         gosub rc.drop.tab
         del bn<cb>
         del bi<cb>
         del bu<cb>
         nbuf -= 1
         cb = nbuf
      end
   end
   return

* Out: df, the file a new record is offered in: the file of the record in the editor, else the file the tree is on, else bp ($acc and the other $ files are not record files)
default.file:
   df = ''
   if nbuf > 0 then df = bn<cb>
   if df = 'path:' then df = ''                         ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): the forms start from a file of records
   if df = '' then
      gosub get.context
      df = ctx.file
   end
   if df = '' or df[1, 1] = '$' then df = 'bp'
   return

close.current:
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   gosub stash.current
   i = cb
   gosub is.dirty
   if dirty then
      gosub buf.label
      screen msgbox sysmsg(12209, bf.name, bf.id) buttons 'Save,Discard,Cancel' default 1 returning b else b = 3
      begin case
         case b = 1
            sv.i = cb
            gosub save.one
            if not(sv.ok) then return
         case b = 2
            null
         case 1
            return
      end case
   end
   ri = cb
   gosub rc.drop.tab                             ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): the person closed it (saved, or Discard): its recovery copy goes
   tu.i = cb
   gosub tab.unlock                              ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): and its record's lock
   k = 'v' : bu<cb>
   bx{k} = 0                                     ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): and its fields view
   fvs{'e' : bu<cb>} = ''
   del bn<cb>
   del bi<cb>
   del bu<cb>
   nbuf -= 1
   if cb > nbuf then cb = nbuf
   if cb < 1 then cb = 1
   if nbuf = 0 then
      if alone then                              ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED, closing the last record ends it
         done = @true
         return
      end
      gosub add.untitled                         ;* the editor is never left without a buffer to type in
   end
   gosub load.current
   return

* In: i.  Out: bf.name, bf.id: what to call buffer i to the person (file and record, or "Untitled n")
buf.label:
   bf.name = bn<i>
   bf.id = bi<i>
   k = 'd' : bu<i>
   if bx{k} # '' then bf.name = 'dict ' : bn<i>          ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B)
   if bf.id = '' then
      k = 'u' : bu<i>
      bf.name = sysmsg(12968, bx{k})
   end
   return

do.quit:
   gosub stash.current
   abort = @false
   * qi is this loop's own: the routines a save reaches (tab.labels, tree.nodes, refresh.tree.for) all loop on i (review, 28 Oct 26: saving an untitled tab ended this loop early)
   for qi = 1 to nbuf
      i = qi
      gosub is.dirty
      if dirty then
         gosub buf.label
         screen msgbox sysmsg(12209, bf.name, bf.id) buttons 'Save,Discard,Cancel' default 1 returning b else b = 3
         begin case
            case b = 1
               sv.i = qi
               gosub save.one
               if not(sv.ok) then abort = @true
            case b = 2
               null
            case 1
               abort = @true
         end case
      end
   until abort
   next qi
   if not(abort) then
      for ri = 1 to nbuf
         gosub rc.drop.tab                       ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): every buffer was saved or discarded by the person: no recovery copy is left
      next ri
      done = @true
   end else
      gosub load.current              ;* a buffer that was named by the loop has its new tab label
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* Building

* In: c.opt ('' or ' debugging').  Out: c.ok, c.file, c.id; the table holds the compiler's lines
do.compile:
   c.ok = @false
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub path.not.for                   ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file is not a program
   if pa.no then return
   * 28 Oct 26 (review of L2): the record is saved first only when there is something to save - a changed text, an untitled buffer or a record not on the disk yet.  A tab that
   *   is read-only (another session holds the record's lock, or it holds bytes 128 to 247) and unchanged is compiled, run, debugged and catalogued as it is on the disk;
   *   save.one refuses a read-only tab even when nothing needs saving.
   gosub stash.current
   i = cb
   gosub is.dirty
   k = 'n' : bu<cb>
   if dirty or bi<cb> = '' or bx{k} then
      gosub save.current
      if not(sv.ok) then return
      gosub path.not.for                ;* 30 Oct 26 (E2): the save of an untitled buffer may have made it an operating system file's tab (path:/name); that is not a program
      if pa.no then return
   end
   c.file = bn<cb>
   c.id = bi<cb>
   k = 'k' : bu<cb>
   if bx{k} = 'dict' then
      msg = sysmsg(12702, c.file, c.id)          ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B), as SED: a dictionary item is not a program (its I-type is compiled when it is used)
      return
   end
   qp.file = c.file ; qp.id = c.id ; gosub quote.pair
   if in.ptr # '' and c.file = in.ptr then
      * 2 Nov 26 (S.96 M4): a program of another account, opened from the master files page, is compiled IN that account by run.in, judged on the compiler's own success line
      execute 'run.in ' : in.acct : ' basic ' : in.file : ' ' : c.id : c.opt capturing c.out
      c.ok = (index(c.out, 'Compiled 1 program(s) with no errors', 1) > 0)
   end else
      execute 'basic ' : qp.f : ' ' : qp.i : c.opt capturing c.out
      c.ok = (@system.return.code # 0)
   end
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   nerr = 0
   loop
      z = remove(c.out, delim)
      begin case
         case z matches "1N...:..."
            ln = matchfield(z, '0N0X', 1) + 0
            tx = trimf(field(z, ':', 2, 999))
            nerr += 1
            err.rows<-1> = ln : @vm : tx
            err.lines<-1> = ln
         case z matches "'WARNING:'0X"
            nerr += 1
            err.rows<-1> = 0 : @vm : trimf(z)
            err.lines<-1> = 0
      end case
   while delim
   repeat
   out.kind = 'errors'
   gosub zoom.leave                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1): a compile with errors or warnings shows them; a clean one stays zoomed
   out.file = c.file
   gosub err.load
   if c.ok then
      msg = sysmsg(12210, c.file, c.id)
   end else
      msg = sysmsg(12211, nerr, c.file, c.id)
      n = dcount(err.lines, @fm)
      for q = 1 to n
         if err.lines<q> + 0 > 0 then
            k2 = q
            gosub select.row
            gosub act.error
            exit
         end
      next q
   end
   return

* 2 Nov 26 (S.96 M4, M5): Catalog, Run and Debug of a tab the master files page opened (in.ptr: a program of another account).  Catalog compiles (through run.in, in do.compile) and
* then catalogues through run.in in that account; Debug (F7, and from the first line) compiles for the debugger and debugs through run.in in that account; Run is refused,
* because run.in runs no program.
master.build:
   if cmd = 'catalog' then
      c.opt = ''
      gosub do.compile
      if c.ok then
         execute 'run.in ' : in.acct : ' catalog ' : in.file : ' ' : c.id capturing co
         gosub catalog.verified
      end
      return
   end
   if cmd = 'debug' or cmd = 'debug1' then
      * 2 Nov 26 (S.96 M5): compiled for the debugger and debugged IN the account, both through run.in; the editor's marks are armed as for any program (bk.arm)
      bk.first = (cmd = 'debug1')
      c.opt = ' debugging'
      gosub do.compile
      if c.ok then
         gosub bk.arm
         bk.first = @false
         gosub leave.screen
         execute 'run.in ' : in.acct : ' debug ' : in.file : ' ' : c.id trapping aborts
         gosub bk.take
         gosub retake.screen
      end
      bk.first = @false
      return
   end
   msg = 'a program of the account ' : in.acct : ' is not run from here: compile it (it is compiled in ' : in.acct : ') and run it in that account'
   return

* In: co, the words CATALOG printed for c.file c.id.  The IDE says "Catalogued" only if CATALOG said the program was added to a catalogue (local, global or private: messages
* 3029-3031); anything else (the account may not catalogue, a conflicting VOC entry, an object that is missing) is shown, whole, in a box.  28 Oct 26 (S.88 Phase 4, 4.9): it used to say
* "Catalogued" whatever the verb answered.
catalog.verified:
   cv.ok = @false
   for cv.m = 3029 to 3031
      if index(upcase(co), upcase(sysmsg(cv.m, c.id)), 1) then cv.ok = @true      ;* CATALOG prints the call name in lower case; the record id may not be
   next cv.m
   if cv.ok then
      msg = sysmsg(12212, c.file, c.id)
      return
   end
   cv.why = ''
   cv.n = dcount(co, @fm)
   for cv.j = 1 to cv.n
      cv.line = trim(convert(char(13) : char(10), '  ', co<cv.j>))
      if cv.line # '' then
         cv.why = cv.line
         exit
      end
   next cv.j
   if cv.why = '' then cv.why = '(no answer)'
   rf.text = sysmsg(12979, c.file, c.id, cv.why)
   msg = rf.text
   gosub refuse.box
   return

* The terminal goes back to the program being run, and comes back to the IDE after it
* ======================================================================
* BREAKPOINTS (S.98 H2, idebrk.h).  A program tab's marks are the memo's w{'bp'} while it is shown and bx b<uid> while it is not, with the text they were kept for in bx j<uid>.

* The editor's mark column and the marks of the tab shown, as they were kept (new.form; the text is the one they were kept for)
bk.form:
   mw{'bpon'} = 0
   mw{'bp'} = ''
   mw{'bpmax'} = MAXBRK
   if nbuf > 0 then
      k = 'k' : bu<cb>
      if bx{k} = 'source' then
         mw{'bpon'} = 1
         k = 'b' : bu<cb>
         mw{'bp'} = bx{k}
      end
   end
   return

* The tab is shown (load.current, after the memo's init): its marks are moved from the text they were kept for to the text it holds now
bk.load:
   gosub bk.form
   if nbuf > 0 then
      if mw{'bpon'} then
         u = bu<cb>
         k = 'b' : u
         if bx{k} # '' then
            k = 'j' : u
            call $sl_memo('bpfollow', mw, '', bx{k}, bk.r)
            k = 'b' : u
            bx{k} = mw{'bp'}
         end
         k = 'j' : u
         bx{k} = mw{'value'}
      end
   end
   return

* Shift-F7 / Build: the mark of the cursor's line
bk.toggle:
   msg = sysmsg(12793)
   if nbuf > 0 then
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      call $sl_memo('bptoggle', mw, '', 0, bk.r)
      begin case
         case bk.r = 'set'
            msg = sysmsg(12724, mw{'cy'})
         case bk.r = 'cleared'
            msg = sysmsg(12791, mw{'cy'})
         case bk.r = 'full'
            msg = sysmsg(12792, MAXBRK)
      end case
      if bk.r # 'none' then gosub redraw.editor
   end
   return

* Build / Clear breakpoints: every mark of the tab shown goes
bk.clear:
   msg = sysmsg(12793)
   if nbuf > 0 then
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      mw = f{'widgets', en}
      call $sl_memo('bpclear', mw, '', 0, bk.r)
      if bk.r # 'none' then
         msg = ''
         gosub redraw.editor
      end
   end
   return

* F7 / Debug from the first line: the debugger is handed the source path of the record just compiled, the tab's marks and whether to run on to the first (idebrk.h).  Only a program
* whose text on the disk is the text in the tab: the marks are line numbers of that text.  Arming with no marks too, so that the debugger starts from empty lists.
bk.arm:
   ibk.mode = IBK.K.IDLE
   bk.u = ''
   if nbuf > 0 then
      u = bu<cb>
      k = 'k' : u
      if bx{k} = 'source' then
         k = 'b' : u
         bk.marks = bx{k}
         open c.file to bk.fv then
            read bk.rec from bk.fv, c.id then
               k = 't' : u
               if bk.rec = bx{k} then
                  ibk.path = fileinfo(bk.fv, FL$PATH) : @ds : c.id
                  ibk.in = bk.marks
                  ibk.go = (if bk.first or bk.marks = '' then 0 else 1)
                  ibk.out = bk.marks
                  ibk.bad = ''
                  ibk.alt = 0
                  ibk.mode = IBK.K.ARMED
                  bk.u = u
               end else
                  if bk.marks # '' then msg = sysmsg(12723)
               end
            end
            close bk.fv
         end
      end
   end
   return

* The debugger has ended: the lines that still have a breakpoint are the tab's marks (taken only from a run that took the hand-over), and the main screen is given back if the debugger
* died holding the alternate one
bk.take:
   if ibk.mode + 0 = IBK.K.LIVE and bk.u # '' then
      bk.new = ''
      bk.last = 0
      for bk.i = 1 to dcount(ibk.out, @fm)
         bk.n = ibk.out<bk.i>
         if bk.n matches '1N0N' then
            bk.n += 0
            if bk.n > bk.last and dcount(bk.new, @fm) < MAXBRK then
               bk.new<-1> = bk.n
               bk.last = bk.n
            end
         end
      next bk.i
      k = 'b' : bk.u
      bx{k} = bk.new
      * 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the debugger says which marks it could not set only when it stops at the first line; a run that went straight on to a mark said nothing, so it is said here
      if ibk.bad # '' then msg = sysmsg(12721, change(ibk.bad, @fm, ' '))
   end
   if ibk.alt + 0 then
      display terminfo('rmcup') :
      ibk.alt = 0
   end
   ibk.mode = IBK.K.IDLE
   bk.u = ''
   return

leave.screen:
   gosub rc.tick                        ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): the program run may abort the session
   screen close
   call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)     ;* 28 Oct 26: the program run has the person's break key, and nothing is held while it runs (CED and SED keep their own)
   display @(-1) :
   return

retake.screen:
   display sysmsg(12228)
   input junk
* Without the pause (SED handed the record back and has nothing left to be read): the same, straight on.
* 28 Oct 26: the setting is read again, as the person has it now ('close' above may have put back the one an earlier aborted app had held, which is the
*   person's own and not the one this program read at its start), and kept again by $tk_brk before the break key is turned off
retake.quiet:
   saved.trap = pterm(PT$BREAK, -1)
   call $tk_brk('save', saved.trap)
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, @false)
   break off
   gosub open.screen
   gosub apply.theme
   gosub relock.all                      ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the locks a program run here let go are taken again (before the form: a tab may now be read-only)
   gosub new.form
   return

* ======================================================================
* Ctrl-E, File / Edit in SED: the record goes to SED and comes back (Part B of the 140x42 plan).  SED edits the record ON DISK, so a changed
* buffer is saved first (asked; Cancel leaves everything as it is).  SED ends its edit at its first successful save (it is told so by the
* named common /sedide/, see gpl.bp/sed), or the user leaves it without saving; the IDE then reads the record again (the disk is the truth),
* forgets the compile errors (they named lines of the old text), takes the screen back WITHOUT the "press Return" pause of Run, and says
* which it was.  SED makes ide.call 2 once the record is open for editing: if it is still 1 on the way back SED stopped on a message of its
* own (a record locked by another session, a refusal), and the IDE waits for a key so that the message can be read.
* ======================================================================
* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1) - the zoom.  The owner, 9 Nov: "if the IDE editor can be expanded on Ctrl+e to give the user the same full screen view that SED provides".
*   zoom.toggle  Ctrl-E (and Go > Zoom the editor): zm flips, the focus leaves a pane that is about to be hidden, and the form is laid out again (size.layout gives the
*                editor the whole inside while zm is 1; zoom.show hides or shows the rest).  Nothing is handed over and nothing is saved or read again.
*   zoom.show    the files, the separator, the messages and their pane marks hidden while zoomed and shown when not; called by new.form and move.form, so a resize and a
*                form made again keep the zoom.
*   zoom.leave   what fills the messages (a search, a view, a command's output, a compile with errors or warnings) ends the zoom, so that it can be read.

zoom.toggle:
   if alone then                        ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED there is nothing to zoom to: this entry takes the results table away
      if al.err then
         al.err = @false
         if focus.id = 'err' then
            ft.id = 'ed'
            gosub focus.to
         end
         gosub relayout
      end
      return
   end
   zm = not(zm)
   if zm and (focus.id = 'tree' or focus.id = 'err') then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   gosub relayout
   if zm then msg = sysmsg(15021) else msg = sysmsg(15022)
   return

zoom.show:
   zv = not(zm)
   zve = (zv or al.err)                 ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): the results table, shown under the editor when started as SED
   screen set f, 'tree' visible zv
   for zr = 1 to ih
      screen set f, 'sep' : zr visible zv
   next zr
   screen set f, 'errbar' visible zve
   screen set f, 'err' visible zve
   screen set f, 'ixtree' visible zv
   screen set f, 'ixerr' visible zve
   return

* The words of sent.in in st.toks, as fields; a text in quotes is one word and keeps its quotes.
sent.tokens:
   st.toks = ''
   st.s = trimf(trimb(sent.in))
   st.n = len(st.s)
   st.i = 1
   loop
   while st.i <= st.n
      st.c = st.s[st.i, 1]
      begin case
         case st.c = ' '
            st.i += 1
         case st.c = '"' or st.c = "'"
            st.e = index(st.s[st.i + 1, st.n], st.c, 1)
            if st.e = 0 then st.e = st.n - st.i + 1      ;* no closing quote: the rest of the sentence
            st.toks<-1> = st.s[st.i, st.e + 1]
            st.i += st.e + 1
         case 1
            st.e = index(st.s[st.i, st.n] : ' ', ' ', 1)
            st.toks<-1> = st.s[st.i, st.e - 1]
            st.i += st.e
      end case
   repeat
   return

* Started as SED with no record named: SED's questions, File name? (when none was named) and Record name?, ? or ? and a filter at the record listing them to pick from.  An empty answer ends the editor, as in SED.
alone.ask:
   as.file = fn0
   loop
      if as.file = '' then
         screen ask sysmsg(15066) history ih.files returning as.file else as.file = ''
         as.file = downcase(trimf(trimb(as.file)))
         if as.file = '' then
            done = @true
            return
         end
      end
      open dflag0, as.file to as.fv then
         close as.fv
      end else
         msg = sysmsg(12205, as.file)
         gosub alone.say
         as.file = ''
         continue
      end
      screen ask sysmsg(15067, as.file) history ih.recs returning as.id else as.id = ''
      as.id = trimf(trimb(as.id))
      if as.id = '' then
         done = @true
         return
      end
      if as.id[1, 1] = '?' then
         ls.file = as.file
         ls.dict = dflag0
         fx.t = trimf(trimb(as.id[2, len(as.id)]))
         gosub filter.clause
         if not(fx.ok) then
            gosub alone.say
            continue
         end
         ls.tmpl = fx.t
         gosub do.listrecs
         if ls.id = '' then continue
         as.id = ls.id
      end
      if upcase(as.id) = 'CREATING.SEQKEY' then       ;* as on the command line: the file's next sequential id
         sq.dict = dflag0
         sq.file = as.file
         gosub seq.take
         if sq.id = '' then
            gosub alone.say                           ;* seq.take says why in msg: a file that cannot give an id is said so and the question comes again
            continue
         end
         as.id = sq.id
      end
      or.dict = dflag0
      or.file = as.file
      or.id = as.id
      or.mknew = 1
      gosub open.record
      if nbuf > 0 then
         screen focus f, 'ed'
         focus.id = 'ed'
         return
      end
   repeat

* SED's questions come before the page's loop, the only thing that writes the status line, so a refusal is put there here or the question comes back with no reason
alone.say:
   gosub redraw
   gosub status.line
   return

* File, List records (started as SED): the records of the file the editor is on, to pick one and open it.
do.listrecs.menu:
   gosub get.context
   if ctx.file = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(15059)
      return
   end
   ls.file = ctx.file
   ls.dict = ''
   ls.tmpl = ''
   gosub do.listrecs
   if ls.id = '' then return
   or.file = ls.file
   or.id = ls.id
   gosub open.record
   if nbuf > 0 then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   return

* In: ls.file, ls.dict, ls.tmpl (a filter clause, or null).  Out: ls.id, the record picked, or null.  With no filter the window reads the file itself and has no limit; a filter selects first.
do.listrecs:
   ls.id = ''
   open ls.dict, ls.file to ls.fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, ls.file)
      return
   end
   ls.name = (if ls.dict # '' then 'dict ' else '') : ls.file
   if ls.tmpl = '' then
      call $sl_pickwin('', sysmsg(15068, ls.name), 0, 0, ls.ret, ls.st, ls.fv, '0' : @fm : 200 : @fm : 100000 : @fm : 20000)
   end else
      fv = ls.fv
      lt.k = 'L:' : ls.name
      gosub filter.slot
      lt.text<lt.p> = ls.tmpl
      lt.page<lt.p> = 0
      lr.key = lt.k
      lr.sent = ls.name
      gosub list.ids
      lr.key = ''
      ls.st = 1
      if lr.ok then
         ls.items = convert(@vm, @fm, lr.ids)
         call $sl_pickwin(ls.items, sysmsg(15068, ls.name), 0, 1, ls.n, ls.st, '', '')
         if ls.st = 0 then ls.ret = ls.items<ls.n + 0>
      end
   end
   last.status = char(1)
   if ls.st = 0 then ls.id = ls.ret
   return

* Started as SED, the File menu has List records after Open record.
alone.file.menu:
   ag.new = ''
   ag.n = dcount(m, @vm)
   for ag.i = 1 to ag.n
      ag.new<1, -1> = m<1, ag.i>
      if m<1, ag.i, 3> = 'open' then ag.new<1, -1> = change(sysmsg(15069), '~', @sm)
   next ag.i
   m = ag.new
   return

* Started as SED, the Go menu has no files pane to go to and nothing to hand a record over to; Zoom the editor becomes the way to take the results table away.
alone.go.menu:
   ag.new = ''
   ag.n = dcount(m, @vm)
   for ag.i = 1 to ag.n
      ag.c = m<1, ag.i, 3>
      if ag.c = 'tree' or ag.c = 'filter' then continue
      if ag.c = 'zoom' then
         ag.new<1, -1> = change(sysmsg(15065), '~', @sm)
      end else
         ag.new<1, -1> = m<1, ag.i>
      end
   next ag.i
   m = ag.new
   return

zoom.leave:
   if not(zm) then return
   if out.kind = 'errors' and err.rows = '' then return      ;* a clean compile: nothing to read
   if alone then
      if not(al.err) then
         al.err = @true                 ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 7): started as SED, the table comes up under the editor until it is taken away
         gosub relayout
      end
      return
   end
   zm = 0
   gosub relayout
   return

* F12, Go / Edit as fields: the record in the tab goes to CED's data mode as an array of fields (each a string or a list of values, each of those a string or a
* list of subvalues: $recarr), so that nobody types a value or a subvalue mark.  CED is given no file and no record id, so it writes nothing and takes no lock; its
* Ctrl-S keeps the changes in the array.  What comes back replaces the text of the tab (the tab is then changed, as after any edit) and the IDE's own save
* writes it, with the S.64 gate and the overwrite question as for any other change.  Nothing is saved here, and if CED did not keep anything the tab is untouched.
do.fields:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      gosub fields.leave                ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): F12 again shows the text
      return
   end
   pa.allow = 'coll'                    ;* 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a collection record IS handed to CED (below); an operating system file is not fields and values
   gosub path.not.for
   pa.allow = ''
   if pa.no then return
   ro.i = cb
   gosub ro.check                       ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the text of a read-only tab is not changed
   if not(ro.ok) then
      gosub ro.box                      ;* 28 Oct 26: bytes outside ASCII are refused in a box
      return
   end
   k = 'n' : bu<cb>
   if bi<cb> = '' or bx{k} then
      gosub save.current                ;* an untitled buffer, or a new record not yet on the disk, is named and written first
      if not(sv.ok) then return
      pa.allow = 'coll'
      gosub path.not.for                ;* 30 Oct 26 (E2): the save may have made the tab an operating system file's (path:/name), which has no fields
      pa.allow = ''
      if pa.no then return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   fd.u = bu<cb>
   fd.file = bn<cb>
   fd.id = bi<cb>
   k = 't' : fd.u
   fd.was = bx{k}
   k = 'k' : fd.u
   fd.coll = (bx{k} = 'coll')
   * S.88 g-P2 (decision 16, gap G1): the record of an ORDINARY file whose text is a JSON object or array, with no value or subvalue mark in it, is edited as the tree it is, as a collection
   *   file's record is; anything else (marks, text that does not parse, a first character that is not { or [) keeps the multivalue view, with no word said about JSON.
   fd.json = 0
   if not(fd.coll) then
      if index(fd.was, @vm, 1) = 0 and index(fd.was, @sm, 1) = 0 then
         fd.jq = trim(convert(@fm, char(10), fd.was))[1, 1]
         if fd.jq = '{' or fd.jq = '[' then
            fd.c = jparse(convert(@fm, char(10), fd.was))
            fd.jst = status()
            if fd.jst = 0 then fd.json = (jtype(fd.c) = 'collection' or jtype(fd.c) = 'array')
         end
      end
   end
   if fd.coll then
      * 29 Oct 26 (stage F): a record of a collection file goes to CED as the collection its JSON text parses to (data type 0, no file and no id: CED writes nothing and keeps the
      *   changes in the variable); what comes back is the JSON text of it, in the layout the tab uses.  Text that does not parse is said, not edited.
      sv.text = fd.was
      sv.name = fd.file
      sv.id = fd.id
      gosub coll.value
      if not(cv.ok) then return
      fd.c = cv.y
   end else
      if not(fd.json) then call $recarr('to', fd.was, fd.arr, fd.ok)
   end
   * 30 Oct 26 (CED in the IDE, stage 2b): the fields view is a PANE of the tab, not a mode of the IDE.  The editor's engine ($ced_core) runs in the IDE's own form for as long as the tab
   *   shows its fields, and the tabs, the menu, the other panes and every IDE key stay alive.  The engine's data is the tab's text in another shape: each change the engine makes is
   *   written back into the tab's text at once (fields.after), so that Save, Compile and the recovery copy never see anything but the text.  F12 again shows the text.
   if fd.coll or fd.json then
      fz.type = 0
      fz.c = fd.c
   end else
      fz.type = 2
      fz.c = fd.arr
   end
   fz.title = fd.file : ' ' : fd.id
   gosub fields.enter
   return

* ======================================================================
* The form is the DEFAULT face of a dictionary item (31 Oct 26, S.90; the owner's word).  Called for the tab a person has just opened on an item - `ide dict file item` and Return on an item in the
*   tree -, not for a record found by a search (it is opened at a line), opened with others from a list or already in a tab.  Nothing is said and the text stays when the current tab is not a dictionary
*   item (nothing could be opened) or is read-only (open.record has said why: another session holds the record, or it holds bytes outside ASCII); do.dform says why when the form cannot show the item.
dform.default:
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   k = 'k' : bu<cb>
   if bx{k} # 'dict' then return
   ro.i = cb
   gosub ro.check
   if not(ro.ok) then return
   gosub redraw
   gosub do.dform
   return

* ======================================================================
* A dictionary item as a full-screen form (31 Oct 26, S.90 stage 2).  The form is gpl.bp/dict_form, over !dict_item: it opens nothing and writes nothing, and gives back the record it built.  MODAL, over the IDE's
*   own screen.  Accept: the record becomes the tab's text (the token form, as a Reload would show it) and the IDE's own save writes it, with its edit gates and its overwrite question.
*   Esc or Cancel: nothing changes, and the tab shows the item as text.  (The form's third result, text, went with its Edit as text button on 31 Oct 26: the owner's layout has Accept and Cancel.)  An item the form cannot show (a type it does not know, a format that is not a width, L R T or C and decimals) stays text, with the reason said.
do.dform:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   k = 'k' : bu<cb>
   if bx{k} # 'dict' then
      msg = sysmsg(12954)
      return
   end
   ro.i = cb
   gosub ro.check                       ;* a read-only tab (another session holds the record, bytes outside ASCII) is not changed
   if not(ro.ok) then
      gosub ro.box
      return
   end
   gosub stash.current
   df.u = bu<cb>
   k = 't' : df.u
   df.rec = bx{k}
   i = cb
   gosub buf.label
   gosub ai.origin                                         ;* 3 Nov 26 (S.97): the form's AI line sends the item's values; the wall asks where the item is
   call $dict_form(df.rec, bf.name : ' ' : bf.id, df.new, df.res, ai.org)
   last.status = char(1)
   gosub redraw
   gosub draw.bar                                          ;* 1 Nov 26: the form drew its own bar on row 0 and the IDE's menu bar is no widget of the form, so redraw leaves the form's bar there
   begin case
      case df.res = 'cannot'
         msg = sysmsg(12955, bf.name : ' ' : bf.id, df.new)
      case df.res = 'save' or df.res = 'text'
         if (df.new : '|') # (df.rec : '|') then
            k = 't' : df.u
            bx{k} = df.new
            k = 'c' : df.u
            bx{k} = ''
            tm.u = df.u
            tm.raw = df.new
            gosub set.token.mode
            gosub load.current
         end
         if df.res = 'save' then
            sv.i = cb
            gosub save.one
         end
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* Where the text of the current tab comes from, for the AI's wall ($tk_aiwall, S.97 stage 0, 1 Nov 26): out ai.org, a buffer that is not yet a record (untitled), an operating system file
*   (path, its pathname) or a record (its file, its id, 1 for an item of the file's dictionary).  The wall asks where the FILE is and refuses a text of SD's own files.
ai.origin:
   if bi<cb> = '' then
      ai.org = 'untitled'
   end else
      if bn<cb> = 'path:' then
         ai.org = 'path' : @vm : '' : @vm : bi<cb>
      end else
         k = 'd' : bu<cb>
         ai.org = 'record' : @vm : bn<cb> : @vm : bi<cb> : @vm : (if bx{k} # '' then 1 else 0)
      end
   end
   return

* The fields view (30 Oct 26, CED in the IDE, stage 2a).  The engine is gpl.bp/ced_core, the same that CED's own program uses; this is the second host.  In: fz.c (the collection or array),
* fz.type (0 a collection or a JSON record, 2 a record as $recarr makes it), fz.title.  Out: fz.c (the engine may replace it: a revert, an accepted AI edit), fz.kept (Ctrl-S kept
* something).  It writes NOTHING: the IDE's own save is the only writer, with its edit gates and its overwrite question.  The keys are the ones CED has always had (Ctrl-S keeps, Ctrl-Q or F12
* leaves, with the question when something is changed and not kept), and the table is the form's, so the Up and Down keys, the pages, the mouse and the scroll bar are the widget's.
* MODAL: the loop below is the only one that reads events until the view ends, so the tabs, the menu bar and the other panes wait; the memo stays under the view, hidden.
fields.enter:
   fz.kind = jtype(fz.c)
   if fz.kind # 'collection' and fz.kind # 'array' then return
   if fz.type = 2 and fz.kind # 'array' then return          ;* a record is an array of fields
   fz.u = bu<cb>
   fz.st = collection()
   fz.st{'c'} = fz.c
   fz.st{'ftype'} = fz.type
   fz.st{'title'} = fz.title
   gosub ai.origin
   fz.st{'origin'} = ai.org                                   ;* 1 Nov 26: where the record in the view comes from, for the AI's wall
   fz.st{'w'} = ew
   fz.st{'h'} = mh + 3
   fz.st{'ai.ok'} = 0                                         ;* the engine's own F2 menu is not used (F2 is the toolkit's): the AI entry and the Tools menu call 'ai.request' and 'ai.explain' (fields.ai)
   call $ced_core('init', fz.st, '', fz.out)
   fz.st{'path.row'} = ystat                                  ;* what the engine paints over a dialogue (a refused value) goes on the status row, which the IDE writes again after
   fz.st{'msg.row'} = ystat
   fz.k1 = 't' : fz.u
   fz.st{'h.text'} = bx{fz.k1}                                ;* the host's keys: the text the engine's data stands for,
   fz.st{'h.from'} = bx{fz.k1}                                ;* the text when the view was entered (fields.leave: one undo step if it changed),
   fz.st{'h.sig'} = jbuild(fz.c)                              ;* and the data as the engine last agreed it with the text
   fvs{'e' : fz.u} = fz.st
   fz.k1 = 'v' : fz.u
   bx{fz.k1} = 1
   fv.on = @true
   gosub fields.view.on
   ft.id = 'fld'
   gosub focus.to
   msg = sysmsg(15995)
   return

* F12 in the view: the tab shows its text again.  The tab's text is already the engine's (fields.after wrote it back at each change); the text area is given it as ONE undo step when it
*   differs from what the area still shows, so Ctrl-Z in the area brings back the text as it was before the fields were shown.  Entering and leaving without a change touches nothing.
fields.leave:
   gosub fields.state
   fz.k1 = 'v' : fz.u
   bx{fz.k1} = 0
   fvs{'e' : fz.u} = ''
   fv.on = @false
   fz.k1 = 't' : fz.u
   tb.u = fz.u
   tb.raw = bx{fz.k1}
   gosub tab.display
   gosub fields.view.off
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ed.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   if (tb.disp : '|') # (ed.mw{'value'} : '|') then
      ed.new = tb.disp
      ed.cy = 0
      gosub ed.set
   end
   ft.id = 'ed'
   gosub focus.to
   i = cb
   gosub buf.label
   gosub is.dirty
   begin case
      case dirty
         msg = sysmsg(12994, bf.name, bf.id)                 ;* changed since the record was read or saved: it is not on the disk yet
      case (bx{fz.k1} : '|') # (fz.st{'h.from'} : '|')
         msg = ''                                            ;* changed while the fields were shown, and saved since
      case 1
         msg = sysmsg(12995, bf.name, bf.id)
   end case
   return

* In: the current tab shows its fields.  Out: fz.u (the tab's uid) and fz.st (its engine state).
fields.state:
   fz.u = bu<cb>
   fz.st = fvs{'e' : fz.u}
   return

* Out: fv.on, whether the current tab shows its fields
fields.is.on:
   fv.on = @false
   if nbuf > 0 then
      fv.k = 'v' : bu<cb>
      if bx{fv.k} then fv.on = @true
   end
   return

* The view on: the text area hidden, the table and the path line shown with the engine's state.  The engine is laid out again when the form's size is not the one it was made for.  Draws.
fields.view.on:
   gosub fields.state
   if fz.st{'w'} + 0 # ew or fz.st{'h'} + 0 # (mh + 3) then
      call $ced_core('layout', fz.st, ew : @fm : (mh + 3), fz.out)
      fz.st{'path.row'} = ystat
      fz.st{'msg.row'} = ystat
   end
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   screen set f, 'ed' visible 0
   screen set f, 'fld' visible 1
   screen set f, 'fpath' visible 1
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   fv.shown = @true
   gosub fields.fill
   gosub redraw
   return

* The view off: the text area showing, the table and the path line hidden.  Draws.
fields.view.off:
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   screen set f, 'fld' visible 0
   screen set f, 'fpath' visible 0
   screen set f, 'ed' visible 1
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   fv.shown = @false
   gosub redraw
   return

* A form just made (new.form): its widgets start with the text area showing.  A tab that shows its fields has the view put in; the focus follows the pane.
fields.form:
   fv.shown = @false
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      gosub fields.state
      if fz.st{'w'} + 0 # ew or fz.st{'h'} + 0 # (mh + 3) then
         call $ced_core('layout', fz.st, ew : @fm : (mh + 3), fz.out)
         fz.st{'path.row'} = ystat
         fz.st{'msg.row'} = ystat
      end
      f{'quiet'} = 1
      screen set f, 'ed' visible 0
      screen set f, 'fld' visible 1
      screen set f, 'fpath' visible 1
      f{'quiet'} = 0
      fv.shown = @true
      gosub fields.fill
      gosub redraw
      if focus.id = 'ed' then focus.id = 'fld'
   end else
      if focus.id = 'fld' then focus.id = 'ed'
   end
   return

* A tab was put on the form (load.current): the view is made to match it.  An engine that is not in step with the tab's text is brought in step first, and a text it cannot show ends the view.
fields.apply:
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      gosub fields.state
      gosub fields.sync.tab
   end
   begin case
      case fv.on
         gosub fields.view.on
         ft.id = focus.id
         gosub focus.to
      case fv.shown
         gosub fields.view.off
         ft.id = focus.id
         gosub focus.to
   end case
   return

* The main loop, once a time round: the engine of the tab on show follows its text (it can be changed by the overwrite question's Reload, an AI edit, SED, anything that sets the text) and
*   the path line follows the tab's marker.  A text the engine cannot show ends the view, and the text area takes over.
fields.sync:
   gosub fields.is.on
   if not(fv.on) then return
   gosub fields.state
   gosub fields.sync.tab
   if fv.on then
      gosub fields.label
   end else
      gosub load.current
   end
   return

* In: fz.u, fz.st.  The engine's data against the tab's text: equal text, nothing to do; the same data in another layout (a save keeps the JSON layout JBUILD makes), the text is taken as the new
*   stamp; other data, the engine is made again from the text, in the container it was in when that still exists.  Out: fv.on (false when the text is not a record the fields can show).
fields.sync.tab:
   fz.k1 = 't' : fz.u
   fz.txt = bx{fz.k1}
   if (fz.txt : '|') = (fz.st{'h.text'} : '|') then return
   gosub fields.parse
   if not(fz.ok) then
      gosub fields.drop
      return
   end
   fz.sig = jbuild(fz.nc)
   if (fz.sig : '|') = (fz.st{'h.sig'} : '|') then
      fz.st{'h.text'} = fz.txt
      return
   end
   gosub fields.rebuild
   return

* In: fz.st, fz.txt.  Out: fz.nc (the data the text stands for) and fz.ok (false when it is not a record the fields can show)
fields.parse:
   fz.nc = ''
   fz.ok = @false
   if fz.st{'ftype'} + 0 = 2 then
      call $recarr('to', fz.txt, fz.nc, fz.ok)
   end else
      fz.nc = jparse(convert(@fm, char(10), fz.txt))
      fz.ok = (status() = 0)
   end
   if fz.ok then
      fz.kind = jtype(fz.nc)
      if fz.kind # 'collection' and fz.kind # 'array' then fz.ok = @false
      if fz.st{'ftype'} + 0 = 2 and fz.kind # 'array' then fz.ok = @false
   end
   return

* In: fz.u, fz.st, fz.nc (the new data), fz.txt (its text), fz.sig.  The engine is made again for the new data, in the container it was in (and on the row it was on) when that is still there; the
*   clip stays.  The tab's text is not touched.
fields.rebuild:
   fz.old = fz.st
   fz.st = collection()
   fz.st{'c'} = fz.nc
   fz.st{'ftype'} = fz.old{'ftype'}
   fz.st{'title'} = fz.old{'title'}
   fz.st{'w'} = ew
   fz.st{'h'} = mh + 3
   fz.st{'ai.ok'} = 0
   call $ced_core('init', fz.st, '', fz.out)
   fz.st{'path.row'} = ystat
   fz.st{'msg.row'} = ystat
   fz.pth = fz.old{'path'}
   fz.np = dcount(fz.pth, @fm)
   if fz.pth = '' then fz.np = 0
   fz.good = @true
   fz.x = fz.nc
   for fz.j = 1 to fz.np
      if fz.good then
         fz.x = fz.x{fz.pth<fz.j>}
         fz.t = jtype(fz.x)
         if fz.t # 'collection' and fz.t # 'array' then fz.good = @false
      end
   next fz.j
   if fz.good and fz.np > 0 then
      fz.st{'path'} = fz.pth
      fz.st{'hist.sel'} = fz.old{'hist.sel'}
      fz.st{'hist.top'} = fz.old{'hist.top'}
      fz.st{'sel'} = fz.old{'sel'}
      fz.st{'top'} = fz.old{'top'}
      call $ced_core('layout', fz.st, ew : @fm : (mh + 3), fz.out)          ;* resolves the path again and builds the list; the selection is kept inside it
   end
   fz.st{'clip.has'} = fz.old{'clip.has'}
   fz.st{'clip'} = fz.old{'clip'}
   fz.st{'clip.type'} = fz.old{'clip.type'}
   fz.st{'clip.name'} = fz.old{'clip.name'}
   fz.st{'h.text'} = fz.txt
   fz.st{'h.from'} = fz.old{'h.from'}
   fz.st{'h.sig'} = fz.sig
   fvs{'e' : fz.u} = fz.st
   return

* The view ends because the text is not a record the fields can show (a Reload brought text that does not parse, say).  The text is the tab's and is not touched.
fields.drop:
   fz.k1 = 'v' : fz.u
   bx{fz.k1} = 0
   fvs{'e' : fz.u} = ''
   fv.on = @false
   i = cb
   gosub buf.label
   msg = sysmsg(15999, bf.name, bf.id)
   return

* Esc (the key comes back as no event): up a level in the fields view when it is on the table and not at the top, else the IDE's own Esc, the menu bar.
fields.esc:
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on and focus.id = 'fld' then
      gosub fields.state
      if fz.st{'path'} # '' then
         msg = ''
         fz.key = 'esc'
         gosub fields.key
         return
      end
   end
   cmd = 'menu'
   gosub run.cmd
   return

* One key for the engine, from do.key (the keys the IDE has no use of) or the table's Return.  The IDE's keys never get here, so Ctrl-S is the IDE's save of the tab's text and Ctrl-Q the IDE's quit.
fields.key:
   fz.k = fz.key
   if fz.k = 'mouse' or fz.k = 'tab' or fz.k = 'btab' or fz.k = 'f2' or fz.k = 'resize' or fz.k = 'guardquit' then return
   if upcase(fz.k) = 'Q' then return                       ;* the engine's Q leaves a CED: this view is left with F12
   if fz.k = 'alt-t' then fz.k = 'f4'                       ;* the engine's change of type (F4 is the IDE's next problem); 8 Nov 26 (S.179): Alt-T, Ctrl-T being the theme everywhere and F2 reserved for the bar's finder
   if fz.k = 'left' or fz.k = 'bs' then fz.k = 'esc'              ;* Backspace is 'bs' to the key reader ($sl_key)
   gosub fields.state
   gosub fields.pull
   call $ced_core('key', fz.st, fz.k, fz.out)
   gosub fields.after
   return

* The AI line for the engine (31 Oct 26, stage 4).  In: fz.op ('ai.request' with the request in fz.arg, or 'ai.explain').  The engine asks the person what it needs (Send or Cancel, then
*   Accept or Reject: $tk_ai) and puts what is accepted in its data; that is written back into the tab's text by fields.after like the result of any key, as one change of the fields
*   (F12 and Ctrl-Z in the text area undo all of a session's changes at once).  There is no undo of its own for the AI's last change here.
fields.ai:
   gosub fields.state
   gosub fields.pull
   if fz.op = 'ai.explain' then
      call $ced_core('ai.explain', fz.st, '', fz.out)
   end else
      call $ced_core('ai.request', fz.st, fz.arg, fz.out)
   end
   last.status = char(1)                                    ;* the wait wrote on the status row
   gosub fields.after
   return

* After the engine took a key: it has no end of its own here, the data is written back to the tab's text when it changed, the engine's message is the status line's, and the view is drawn again.
fields.after:
   fz.st{'done'} = @false
   fz.st{'quit.all'} = @false
   fz.st{'req'} = ''
   fz.nc = fz.st{'c'}
   fz.sig = jbuild(fz.nc)
   if (fz.sig : '|') # (fz.st{'h.sig'} : '|') then gosub fields.writeback
   gosub fields.is.on
   if not(fv.on) then
      gosub load.current                                    ;* the view ended (fields.drop): the text area takes over, with the text the tab holds
      return
   end
   msg = fz.st{'msg'}
   last.status = char(1)                                    ;* a dialogue or the engine's message line may have written over the status row
   gosub fields.show
   ft.id = 'fld'
   gosub focus.to
   return

* In: fz.u, fz.st, fz.nc (the data after the key), fz.sig.  The text of the data goes into the tab (as the record, token mode and cursor worked out again) and the engine is stamped with it.  When the data
*   cannot be a record (the engine allows a shape $recarr refuses) the engine goes back to the tab's text, which was the last that was good, and says so.
fields.writeback:
   if fz.st{'ftype'} + 0 = 2 then
      call $recarr('from', fz.nc, fz.txt, fz.ok)
   end else
      fz.txt = convert(char(10), @fm, jbuild(fz.nc, JB$PRETTY))
      fz.ok = @true
   end
   if not(fz.ok) then
      fz.k1 = 't' : fz.u
      fz.txt = bx{fz.k1}
      gosub fields.parse
      if fz.ok then
         fz.sig = jbuild(fz.nc)
         gosub fields.rebuild
         fz.st{'msg'} = sysmsg(12993)
      end else
         gosub fields.drop
      end
      return
   end
   fz.k1 = 't' : fz.u
   bx{fz.k1} = fz.txt
   fz.k1 = 'c' : fz.u
   bx{fz.k1} = ''
   tm.u = fz.u
   tm.raw = fz.txt
   gosub set.token.mode
   fz.st{'h.text'} = fz.txt
   fz.st{'h.sig'} = fz.sig
   return

* The engine's selection and window from the table: the Up, Down and page keys and the mouse move the widget and not the engine
fields.pull:
   screen get f, 'fld' returning fz.rn
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'fld', fz.tn)
   fz.tt = f{'widgets', fz.tn}
   fz.st{'sel'} = fz.rn + 0
   if fz.tt{'top'} + 0 >= 1 then fz.st{'top'} = fz.tt{'top'} + 0
   return

* The table and the path line from the engine's state, put on the form and not drawn
fields.fill:
   fz.tb = fz.st{'tbl'}
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'fld', fz.tn)
   fz.tt = f{'widgets', fz.tn}
   fz.tt{'cols'} = fz.tb{'cols'}
   fz.tt{'rows'} = fz.tb{'rows'}
   fz.tt{'top'} = fz.st{'top'}
   fz.tt{'cur'} = fz.st{'sel'}
   fz.tt{'value'} = fz.st{'sel'}
   fz.tt{'picked'} = ''
   fz.tt{'stay'} = 1                                         ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): Up on the first row and Down on the last stay in the table; the form would move the focus to another pane
   call $sl_widget_init(fz.tt)
   gosub fields.pathtext
   fz.q = f{'quiet'} + 0
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   screen set f, 'fpath' text fz.pt
   f{'quiet'} = fz.q
   fv.pt = fz.pt
   return

* Out: fz.pt, the path line: the container the view is in, and the tab's marker (the tab changed since it was read or saved, as the status line says it)
fields.pathtext:
   fz.pt = sysmsg(12143, '/' : change(fz.st{'path'}, @fm, '/'))
   fz.k2 = 't' : fz.u
   fz.k3 = 's' : fz.u
   fz.dd = ((bx{fz.k2} : '|') # (bx{fz.k3} : '|'))
   fz.k3 = 'n' : fz.u
   if bx{fz.k3} and bx{fz.k2} # '' then fz.dd = @true
   if fz.dd then fz.pt := ' *'
   return

* The path line when the tab's marker changed under it (a save): the label alone is set and drawn again
fields.label:
   gosub fields.pathtext
   if fz.pt = fv.pt then return
   fv.pt = fz.pt
   f{'quiet'} = 1
   screen set f, 'fpath' text fz.pt
   f{'quiet'} = 0
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   call $sl_form('drawwidget', f, scr, 'fpath', rr)
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* The table and the path line from the engine's state, drawn
fields.show:
   gosub fields.fill
   gosub redraw
   return

* The window changed size.  2 Nov 26 (S.88 p3.3a): the form's widgets are MOVED and SIZED to the new layout (move.form), not made again, so that the editor keeps its undo and
* redo steps, its selection mark, its find text and its view, the messages table its row, the tree its open nodes and the AI entry what was typed in it.  Before a form exists
* (and when the screen is taken back from a program, retake.quiet) the form is made from the state, as it always was.
relayout:
   gosub stash.current
   if have.form then
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
      ow = f{'widgets', en}
      ed.macro = ow{'macro'}
      ed.over = ow{'over'} + 0                      ;* Ins in the area toggles it there: a form made again later (retake.quiet) is given what this one has
   end
   screen clear
   mv.bad = 0                            ;* set here too: BASIC does not short-circuit, and new.form's path never sets it
   if have.form then
      gosub move.form
   end else
      gosub new.form
   end
   if have.form and mv.bad > 0 then      ;* 2 Nov 26 (review): move.form was refused for a widget - the form is made again rather than left half moved
      mv.bad = 0
      screen clear
      gosub new.form
   end
   return

* The form that is there, moved and sized to the layout for the window as it is now: every widget is where new.form would put it (test-widgetmove-units.py holds the two
* in step).  The screen was cleared, so what the form saved from under it is stale: it is taken off (f{'shown'} 0, nothing put back) and the next SCREEN WAIT draws it whole.
move.form:
   last.status = char(1)                ;* the status line and the foot are written again (status.line forgets last.foot then)
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   w = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   h = scr<SL.S.H> + 0
   call $sl_window(ww0, wh0)
   if ww0 < w then w = ww0
   if wh0 < h then h = wh0
   gosub size.layout
   f{'shown'} = 0
   f{'under'} = ''
   f{'w'} = fw
   f{'h'} = fh
   mv.bad = 0                           ;* 2 Nov 26 (review): a move refused leaves that widget where it was; any refusal sends relayout back to new.form

   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'tree', 1, 1, (tw - 1) : @vm : ih, r) ; mv.bad += r
   * the separator, one label to a row of the form's inside: rows the form gained get a label, rows it lost lose theirs
   call $sl_line('v', '', vch)
   for r = 1 to ih
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'sep' : r, sn)
      if sn = 0 then
         screen label f, 'sep' : r, vch at tw,r width 1 attr 'window'
      end else
         call $sl_widget_move(f, 'sep' : r, tw, r, '', mr) ; mv.bad += mr
      end
   next r
   r = ih + 1
   loop
      call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'sep' : r, sn)
   while sn > 0
      call $sl_form('delete', f, scr, 'sep' : r, dn)
      r += 1
   repeat

   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'tabbar', ex, 1, ew : @vm : 1, r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'tabs', ex, 1, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ed', ex, 2, ew : @vm : mh, r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'errbar', ex, err.y, ew : @vm : 1, r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'err', tn)
   tbw = f{'widgets', tn}
   tbw{'cols'} = sysmsg(12226) : @sm : '6R' : @vm : sysmsg(12227) : @sm : (ew - 11) : 'L'
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'err', ex, err.y + 1, ew : @vm : (eh - 1 - ai.rows), r) ; mv.bad += r
   if ai.on then
      call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ailab', ex, ai.y, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
      call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ai', ex + 4, ai.y, (ew - 4) : @vm : 1, r) ; mv.bad += r
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'fld', tn)
   tbw = f{'widgets', tn}
   tbw{'cols'} = sysmsg(12138) : @sm : '10L' : @vm : sysmsg(12139) : @sm : '10L' : @vm : sysmsg(12140) : @sm : (ew - 26) : 'L'
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'fld', ex, 2, ew : @vm : (mh - 1), r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'fpath', ex, mh + 1, ew : @vm : 1, r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ixtree', tw - 4, 0, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ixtabs', ex + ew - 3, 1, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ixed', ex + ew - 4, 0, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
   call $sl_widget_move(f, 'ixerr', ex + ew - 3, err.y, '', r) ; mv.bad += r
   if mv.bad > 0 then return            ;* a widget the move refused: relayout builds the screen again (the text is kept: it stashed it first)
   gosub zoom.show                      ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1)
   call $sl_form('layout', f, scr, '', r)
   gosub draw.bar
   return

* ======================================================================
* Records: a new one, and a search

* ======================================================================
* The AI (23 Oct 26, AI line stage 2).  $tk_ai does the asking: the preview of everything that will be sent (Send or Cancel, Cancel the default), the wait, the answer and, if the
* answer proposes edits, the diff in full with Accept and Reject (Reject the default).  This program supplies what it may be told and applies what is accepted to ITS OWN BUFFER, as
* one undo step; it saves nothing (Save is the person's act, through save.current and the kernel's edit gates as ever).

* Explain the compile message the table has selected (the table must hold a compile, not a search)
ai.error:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12667)
      return
   end
   if err.rows = '' or out.kind # 'errors' then
      msg = sysmsg(12668)
      return
   end
   screen get f, 'err' returning ai.rn
   ai.k = ai.rn + 0
   if ai.k < 1 or ai.k > dcount(err.rows, @fm) then
      msg = sysmsg(12668)
      return
   end
   ai.req = sysmsg(12661, err.rows<ai.k, 2>)
   ai.kind = 'error'
   ai.note = 'The compile message: ' : err.rows<ai.k, 2> : (if err.lines<ai.k> + 0 > 0 then ' (line ' : err.lines<ai.k> : ')' else '')
   gosub ai.go
   return

* Explain the text from the editor's mark to its cursor
ai.selection:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12667)
      return
   end
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      fz.op = 'ai.explain'              ;* 31 Oct 26 (stage 4): in the fields view the "selection" is the element the table has selected
      fz.arg = ''
      gosub fields.ai
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.my = mw{'my'} + 0
   ai.mx = mw{'mx'} + 0
   if ai.my < 1 or ai.mx < 1 then
      msg = sysmsg(12669)
      return
   end
   ai.cy = mw{'cy'} + 0
   ai.cx = mw{'cx'} + 0
   ai.lines = mw{'value'}
   call $tk_ai('select', ai.lines, ai.my : @vm : ai.mx : @vm : ai.cy : @vm : ai.cx, ai.selr)     ;* the text between the mark and the cursor (the library's, shared with SED)
   ai.sel = ai.selr<1>
   if ai.sel = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(12669)
      return
   end
   ai.req = sysmsg(12662)
   ai.kind = 'selection'
   ai.note = 'The selection runs from line ' : ai.selr<2> : ' to line ' : ai.selr<3>
   gosub ai.go
   return

* In: ai.req (the request), ai.kind, ai.note (a sentence about what is selected, or null), ai.sel (the selected text, or null).  The record in the editor, as far as it fits, is the text
ai.go:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12667)
      return
   end
   if trim(ai.req) = '' then return
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      * 31 Oct 26 (stage 4): in the fields view a request typed in the AI entry goes to the engine, which has the record as indented JSON and takes what is accepted; the other
      *   kinds (a compile message, a selection of text) are about the text area and stay with it
      if ai.kind = 'ask' then
         screen set f, 'ai' value ''
         fz.op = 'ai.request'
         fz.arg = ai.req
         gosub fields.ai
      end else
         msg = sysmsg(15998)
      end
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.lines = mw{'value'}
   ai.cy = mw{'cy'} + 0
   ai.total = dcount(ai.lines, @fm)
   * the window: the whole record when it fits in 24000 bytes, else lines round the cursor (the library's, shared with the other editors)
   call $tk_ai('window', ai.lines, ai.cy, ai.wspec)
   ai.first = ai.wspec<1>
   ai.last = ai.wspec<2>
   ai.win = ai.wspec<4>
   page ='This is the IDE of SD Core, an editor for SD BASIC programs and other records.'
   if bi<cb> = '' then
      i = cb
      gosub buf.label
      page<1, -1> = 'An untitled buffer, ' : bf.name : ', not yet saved to a record.'
   end else
      if bn<cb> = 'path:' then
         page<1, -1> = 'It is the operating system file ' : bi<cb> : ', not a record.'      ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E)
      end else
         page<1, -1> = 'File ' : bn<cb> : ', record ' : bi<cb> : '.'
      end
      k = 'd' : bu<cb>
      if bx{k} # '' then page<1, -1> = "It is an item of the file's dictionary."
   end
   page<1, -1> = 'The cursor is on line ' : ai.cy : ' of ' : ai.total : '.'
   if ai.note # '' then page<1, -1> = ai.note
   if ai.first > 1 or ai.last < ai.total then msg = sysmsg(12672, ai.first, ai.last - ai.first + 1)
   gosub ai.origin
   actx = 'IDE' : @fm : 'ide' : @fm : page : @fm : '' : @fm : 0 : @fm : ai.req : @fm : ystat : @fm : ai.win : @fm : ai.first : @fm : ai.sel : @fm : 'nocmd' : @fm : '' : @fm : ai.org
   ai.ctx = ''
   ai.ctx<TK.C.W> = lw
   res = ''
   call $tk_ai('ask', ai.ctx, actx, res)
   last.status = char(1)                                   ;* the wait wrote on the status row, and the preview over the form: both are drawn again
   if res<5> # '' then msg = res<5>
   if res<1> = 'edit' then gosub ai.apply
   if focus.id = 'ai' then
      screen set f, 'ai' value ''
   end
   ai.sel = ''
   ai.note = ''
   gosub redraw
   return

* The accepted edits (res<2>: position SM length SM new text, as values, positions in the window) go into the editor's buffer, as ONE undo step.  The buffer must still hold, at each
* place, exactly what the model was shown; if the person changed it while the AI was answering, nothing is applied.  Nothing is written to the disk.
ai.apply:
   ro.i = cb
   gosub ro.check                       ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the text of a read-only tab is not changed
   if not(ro.ok) then
      gosub ro.box                      ;* 28 Oct 26: bytes outside ASCII are refused in a box
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ai.now = change(mw{'value'}, @fm, char(10))
   ai.wspec<5> = res<2>
   call $tk_ai('splice', ai.now, ai.wspec, ai.sp)          ;* the library checks that the buffer still holds, at each place, what the model was shown
   if ai.sp = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(12671)
      return
   end
   ai.new = ai.sp<1>
   ai.line1 = ai.sp<2>
   ai.n = ai.sp<3>
   * the old text and cursor go on the memo's own undo steps as one step, and redo is forgotten: the memo's 'snap' does it, in the memo's own form.  28 Oct 26 (TUI
   *   phase 2 review): this pushed the step in the memo's OLD form (the lines joined by value marks), which the memo now reads as legacy and turns every value
   *   mark back into a line end, so Ctrl-Z after an AI edit gave back a record whose values had become lines
   call $sl_memo('snap', mw, '', '', ai.sr)
   ai.old = mw{'value'}
   mw{'value'} = change(ai.new, char(10), @fm)
   call $sl_memo('bpfollow', mw, '', ai.old, ai.sr)        ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the breakpoint marks move with the text
   mw{'lastop'} = ''
   mw{'cy'} = ai.line1
   mw{'cx'} = 1
   mw{'want'} = 1
   mw{'left'} = 1
   mw{'mx'} = 0
   mw{'my'} = 0
   ai.tp = ai.line1 - int(mh / 2)
   if ai.tp < 1 then ai.tp = 1
   mw{'top'} = ai.tp
   msg = sysmsg(12666, ai.n)
   return

* Out: ctx.file, the file the tree or the editor is on
get.context:
   ctx.file = ''
   if focus.id = 'tree' or nbuf = 0 then
      screen get f, 'tree' returning code
      if code[1, 2] = 'F:' or code[1, 2] = 'D:' then ctx.file = code[3, 99]
      if code[1, 2] = 'R:' or code[1, 2] = 'Q:' then ctx.file = field(code, ':', 2)
   end
   if ctx.file = '' and nbuf > 0 then ctx.file = bn<cb>
   if ctx.file = 'path:' then ctx.file = ''             ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file is in no file of records
   return

* Ctrl-W, as SED's: the text in the editor is written to a record of the file under another name and the editor goes on
* with the record it had
* Go / Filter the records (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 5), SED's "? template" at the record name: a filter for the list of the file the tree or the editor is on.  A word is an @ID pattern (abc...), a text
*   in quotes the same, and LIKE or WITH a query clause, as check.template in SED; empty, every record again.  The filter stays until it is changed and the list goes back to its first page.
do.filter:
   gosub get.context
   if ctx.file = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(15059)
      return
   end
   fx.dict = @false
   if focus.id = 'tree' then
      screen get f, 'tree' returning code
      fx.dict = (code[1, 2] = 'D:' or code[1, 2] = 'Q:')
   end
   lt.k = (if fx.dict then 'D:' else 'F:') : ctx.file
   gosub filter.slot
   fx.at = lt.p
   screen ask sysmsg(15060, ctx.file) width 60 value lt.text<fx.at> returning fx.t else return
   fx.t = trimf(trimb(fx.t))
   gosub filter.clause
   if not(fx.ok) then return
   lt.text<fx.at> = fx.t
   lt.page<fx.at> = 0
   if fx.dict then
      ld.name = ctx.file
      gosub load.dict
   end else
      lf.name = ctx.file
      gosub load.file
   end
   cur.code = (if fx.dict then 'D:' else 'F:') : ctx.file
   gosub tree.apply
   gosub redraw.tree
   return

* In: fx.t, what the person typed.  Out: fx.t a query clause, or null for none; fx.ok false (and msg) for a text that cannot be one.  SED's check.template: a word is an @ID pattern, LIKE or WITH is a clause as it stands.
filter.clause:
   fx.ok = @true
   if fx.t # '' and upcase(fx.t[1, 5]) # 'LIKE ' and upcase(fx.t[1, 5]) # 'WITH ' then
      fx.c = fx.t[1, 1]
      if index(\'"\, fx.c, 1) then
         if fx.t[1] # fx.c or len(fx.t) < 2 or index(fx.t, fx.c, 2) # len(fx.t) then
            msg = sysmsg(6674)
            fx.ok = @false
            return
         end
         fx.t = 'with @id like ' : fx.t
      end else
         if index(fx.t, '"', 1) then
            if index(fx.t, "'", 1) then
               msg = sysmsg(6675)
               fx.ok = @false
               return
            end
            fx.t = "with @id like '" : fx.t : "'"
         end else
            fx.t = 'with @id like "' : fx.t : '"'
         end
      end
   end
   return

* In: lt.k.  Out: lt.p, its place in lt.keys, 0 when it has none.
filter.find:
   locate lt.k in lt.keys<1> setting lt.p else lt.p = 0
   return

* In: lt.k.  Out: lt.p, its place in lt.keys, made when it has none.
filter.slot:
   gosub filter.find
   if lt.p = 0 then
      lt.keys<-1> = lt.k
      lt.p = dcount(lt.keys, @fm)
      lt.text<lt.p> = ''
      lt.page<lt.p> = 0
   end
   return

* File / Spool the text (9 Nov 26, S.184 stage 5): SED's SPOOL.  The tab's text as it is now goes to print unit 0, saved or not, the record is not touched and nothing is compiled.  The words
*   are SED's: AT printer (for this job only), LNUM (line numbers), REGION (the lines of the selection).  A long line runs on to the next line of paper.
do.spool:
   gosub ed.read
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   screen ask sysmsg(15056) width 50 returning sp.w else return
   sp.w = trim(sp.w)
   sp.at = ''
   sp.lnum = @false
   sp.region = @false
   sp.n = if sp.w = '' then 0 else dcount(sp.w, ' ')
   sp.i = 0
   loop
      sp.i += 1
   while sp.i <= sp.n
      sp.c = downcase(field(sp.w, ' ', sp.i))
      begin case
         case sp.c = 'at'
            sp.i += 1
            sp.at = field(sp.w, ' ', sp.i)
            if sp.at = '' then
               msg = sysmsg(6696, sp.c)
               return
            end
         case sp.c = 'lnum'
            sp.lnum = @true
         case sp.c = 'region'
            sp.region = @true
         case 1
            msg = sysmsg(15057, sp.c)
            return
      end case
   repeat
   sp.from = 1
   sp.to = dcount(ed.t, @fm)
   if sp.region then
      gosub sel.range
      if not(sr.ok) then
         msg = sysmsg(12556)
         return
      end
      sp.from = sr.y1
      sp.to = if sr.x2 = 1 then sr.y2 - 1 else sr.y2
   end
   gosub stash.current
   i = cb
   gosub is.dirty
   gosub buf.label
   sp.head = bf.name : ' ' : bi<cb>
   if dirty then sp.head := ' ' : sysmsg(6910)
   printer on
   call !getpu(PU$MODE, 0, sp.mode, sp.code)
   if sp.mode # 3 then
      call !getpu(PU$BANNER, 0, sp.banner, sp.code)
      call !setpu(PU$BANNER, 0, bi<cb>[1, 32], sp.code)
   end
   if sp.at # '' then
      call !getpu(PU$LOCATION, 0, sp.oldat, sp.code)
      call !setpu(PU$LOCATION, 0, sp.at, sp.code)
      if sp.code then
         msg = sysmsg(6699, sp.code)
         sp.at = ''                           ;* nothing to put back: the location was not changed
         gosub spool.close
         return
      end
   end
   heading sp.head : "   'TGSL'"
   sp.width = @lptrwide
   sp.nw = 0
   if sp.lnum then
      sp.nw = len(sp.to)
      if sp.nw < 4 then sp.nw = 4
      sp.width -= sp.nw + 2
   end
   if sp.width < 1 then sp.width = 1
   for sp.ln = sp.from to sp.to
      sp.l = ed.t<sp.ln>
      if sp.lnum then print fmt(sp.ln, sp.nw : "'0'R") : ': ' :
      loop
         print sp.l[1, sp.width]
      while len(sp.l) > sp.width
         sp.l = sp.l[sp.width + 1, len(sp.l)]
         if sp.lnum then print space(sp.nw + 2) :
      repeat
   next sp.ln
   sp.done = sp.to - sp.from + 1
   gosub spool.close
   msg = sysmsg(15058, sp.done)
   return

* The job is ended and the print unit's banner and location are put back (SED left the printer on when a location was refused).
spool.close:
   printer off
   hush on
   printer close
   hush off
   printer on
   if sp.mode # 3 then call !setpu(PU$BANNER, 0, sp.banner, sp.code)
   if sp.at # '' then call !setpu(PU$LOCATION, 0, sp.oldat, sp.code)
   printer off
   printer close
   screen refresh
   last.status = char(1)
   return

do.writeas:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage E, review finding 4): Write a copy WORKS on an operating system file's tab - it puts the text in a RECORD and touches neither the file nor the tab - because it is the
   *   way out of a save that failed, was refused or found the file replaced, when the editor holds the only copy of the text (a record's tab keeps one in the record; this tab keeps none
   *   in a recovery copy)
   gosub stash.current
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 't' : u
   wa.text = bx{k}
   gosub default.file
   wa.file = df
   wa.id = ''
   call $ide_name('copy', wa.file, wa.id, wa.res)       ;* the form has asked the gate and whether the record exists
   last.status = char(1)
   if wa.res # 'ok' then return
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a record open in a tab (a new one, not yet on the disk, which the form cannot see) is not written from another: its tab holds its lock
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = wa.file
   tf.id = wa.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, wa.file, wa.id)
      return
   end
   gosub write.copy
   return

* In: wa.text, wa.file, wa.id (the form has named a record that was not there, and no tab holds it).  29 Oct 26 (stage D): the write of Write a copy, taken out so that Export the selection
*   writes the same way: the file is opened, the account's edit gate asked again, the record locked and looked at again, and written ON ERROR.
write.copy:
   if wa.file = 'path:' then
      gosub copy.to.os                  ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): a name of the operating system: a file is made for the text
      return
   end
   open wa.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, wa.file)
      return
   end
   gosub edit.check                  ;* asked again at the write, as save.one does: the form's answer is not the gate
   if not(edit.ok) then
      rf.text = msg
      gosub refuse.box
      return
   end
   * under the record's lock, and looked at again: a record another session made since the form asked is not overwritten (Opus's review, 28 Oct 26)
   readu x from fv, wa.id locked
      msg = sysmsg(12978, wa.file, wa.id)
      return
   end then
      release fv, wa.id
      msg = sysmsg(12222, wa.id, wa.file)
      return
   end else
      null
   end
   write wa.text on fv, wa.id on error
      wa.err = status()
      release fv, wa.id
      msg = sysmsg(12977, wa.file, wa.id, wa.err)
      return
   end
   release fv, wa.id
   rt.file = wa.file
   gosub refresh.tree.for
   msg = sysmsg(12207, wa.file, wa.id)
   return

* Ctrl-O, File / Open record: the form asks for a file and a record that exists (the picker of the plan's 5.11 C will take its place), and the record opens in a tab
do.open:
   gosub default.file
   op.file = df
   op.id = ''
   call $ide_name('open', op.file, op.id, op.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if op.res # 'ok' then return
   if op.file = 'path:' then
      op.path = op.id                           ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): an operating system file: the form returns the name as it was typed
      gosub open.path
      if nbuf > 0 then
         ft.id = 'ed'
         gosub focus.to
      end
      return
   end
   or.file = op.file
   or.id = op.id
   cur.code = 'R:' : or.file : ':' : or.id
   gosub open.record
   if nbuf > 0 then
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   return

* Save as: the form names the file and the record, the tab becomes that record, and it is saved.  The record the tab had is as it was on the disk.
do.saveas:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub path.not.for                   ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage E): Save as would make the tab another record; Write a copy keeps the text
   if pa.no then return
   gosub stash.current
   gosub default.file
   sa.file = df
   sa.id = ''
   call $ide_name('saveas', sa.file, sa.id, sa.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if sa.res # 'ok' then return
   if sa.file = 'path:' then
      * 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): the tab becomes the operating system file, made with its text (see os.convert); the record it had is as it was on the disk
      oc.i = cb
      oc.path = sa.id
      gosub os.convert
      return
   end
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = sa.file
   tf.id = sa.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, sa.file, sa.id)                 ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): another tab holds that record
      return
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the tab lets go of the record it had (that record is as it was on the disk) and takes the lock of the one it becomes; a read-only tab
   *   becomes an ordinary one (the lock it could not have was the old record's).  The form names a file of records: the new record is in its data part.
   tu.i = cb
   gosub tab.unlock
   u = bu<cb>
   k = 'o' : u
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'd' : u
   bx{k} = ''
   bn<cb> = sa.file
   bi<cb> = sa.id
   k = 's' : u
   bx{k} = ''
   k = 'n' : u
   bx{k} = 1                                            ;* the record is not on the disk until the save below has written it
   gosub tab.lock
   tm.u = u
   k = 't' : u
   tm.raw = bx{k}
   gosub set.token.mode
   sv.i = cb
   gosub tab.named
   gosub save.one
   return

* (Ctrl-R, Replace, is do.replace at the end of this program with the other editing commands of stage D.)

* New record (File menu, Ctrl-N): the form names the file and the record (it has asked the account's gate and whether the record exists), and a buffer for it
* opens in a tab of its own.  Nothing is written until it is saved (28 Oct 26, S.88 Phase 4, I1, N3).
do.new:
   gosub default.file
   nm.file = df
   nm.id = ''
   call $ide_name('new', nm.file, nm.id, nm.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if nm.res # 'ok' then return
   if nm.file = 'path:' then
      * 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): a name of the operating system: an empty file is made and opened in a tab of its own
      gosub os.new
      if nbuf > 0 and cr.why = '' then
         ft.id = 'ed'
         gosub focus.to
         gosub redraw.tabs
      end
      return
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): CREATING.SEQKEY as the name, as SED takes it: the file's next sequential id; in quotes it is a record of that name
   if upcase(nm.id) = 'CREATING.SEQKEY' then
      sq.dict = ''
      sq.file = nm.file
      gosub seq.take
      if sq.id = '' then return
      nm.id = sq.id
   end else
      if upcase(nm.id) = '"CREATING.SEQKEY"' or upcase(nm.id) = "'CREATING.SEQKEY'" then nm.id = nm.id[2, len(nm.id) - 2]
   end
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = nm.file
   tf.id = nm.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, nm.file, nm.id)                 ;* a new record of that name is already open in a tab
      return
   end
   gosub add.new.record
   if not(an.made) then return               ;* 28 Oct 26 (D2): Cancel at the recovery copy's question
   gosub load.current
   ft.id = 'ed'
   gosub focus.to
   gosub redraw.tabs
   return

do.find:
   gosub get.context
   if ctx.file = '' then
      msg = sysmsg(12217)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(12215, ctx.file) returning pat else return
   if pat = '' then return
   if len(pat) > FINDMAX then
      msg = sysmsg(12557, FINDMAX)               ;* INDEX cuts a text at 256 characters and would list records that do not hold it (review of the fixes, CB1)
      return
   end
   open ctx.file to fv else
      msg = sysmsg(12205, ctx.file)
      return
   end
   if fileinfo(fv, FL$COLLECTION) then
      msg = sysmsg(12703, ctx.file)              ;* 28 Oct 26 (stage B): a collection's records are not text; a plain READ of one would stop the IDE
      return
   end
   * 28 Oct 26 (stage B): the records are read as the tree lists them, at most MAXREC, without sorting the whole file first
   gosub list.ids
   ids = convert(@vm, @fm, lr.ids)
   n = lr.n
   if ids = '' then n = 0
   upat = upcase(pat)
   hits = 0
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   sid = ''
   sln = ''
   for i = 1 to n
      read r from fv, ids<i> then
         nl = dcount(r, @fm)
         for j = 1 to nl
            if index(upcase(r<j>), upat, 1) then
               hits += 1
               err.rows<-1> = j : @vm : ids<i> : ': ' : trim(r<j>)
               err.lines<-1> = j
               sid<-1> = ids<i>
               sln<-1> = j
            end
         until hits >= MAXHIT
         next j
      end
   until hits >= MAXHIT
   next i
   out.kind = 'search'
   gosub zoom.leave                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1)
   out.file = ctx.file
   out.dict = ''
   gosub err.load
   msg = sysmsg(12216, hits, ctx.file)
   if lr.more then msg := '  ' : sysmsg(12224, MAXREC, ctx.file)
   return

* ======================================================================
* Editing commands (the editor merge's stage D, 29 Oct 26).  They work on the TEXT AREA'S live text - mw{'value'}, the lines joined by field marks, as the person sees it, the
* token form too - and each is ONE undo step ($sl_memo 'snap' before the text is set, as an AI edit does).  None of them writes a record: Save is the person's act.

* Out: ed.ok, ed.mw (the text area's widget) and ed.t (its text).  False, and msg says why, when no tab is open.  ed.get also refuses a tab that cannot be changed (ro.check: another
*   session's lock, bytes 128-247, an operating system file this session may not write); ed.read is for the commands that only look.
ed.read:
   ed.ok = @false
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      msg = sysmsg(15998)               ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): the commands that work on the text (Replace, case, Import, Export, Format, ...) wait for F12: the text area behind the table is not the record's text
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ed.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ed.t = ed.mw{'value'}
   ed.ok = @true
   return

ed.get:
   ed.ok = @false
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   ro.i = cb
   gosub ro.check
   if not(ro.ok) then
      gosub ro.box
      return
   end
   gosub ed.read
   return

* In: ed.mw, ed.new (the whole new text, lines joined by field marks), ed.cy (the line the cursor goes to, at its start; 0 keeps the cursor where it was, kept inside the text).  The old text
*   and cursor go on the undo steps as ONE step and redo is forgotten ('snap'); the mark is taken away; the editor is drawn.
ed.set:
   call $sl_memo('snap', ed.mw, '', '', ed.sr)
   ed.old = ed.mw{'value'}
   ed.mw{'value'} = ed.new
   call $sl_memo('bpfollow', ed.mw, '', ed.old, ed.sr)     ;* 2 Nov 26 (S.98 H2): the breakpoint marks move with the text
   ed.mw{'lastop'} = ''
   ed.mw{'mx'} = 0
   ed.mw{'my'} = 0
   ed.nl = dcount(ed.new, @fm)
   if ed.cy > 0 then
      if ed.cy > ed.nl then ed.cy = ed.nl
      ed.mw{'cy'} = ed.cy
      ed.mw{'cx'} = 1
      ed.mw{'want'} = 1
      ed.mw{'left'} = 1
      ed.tp = ed.cy - int(mh / 2)
      if ed.tp < 1 then ed.tp = 1
      ed.mw{'top'} = ed.tp
   end else
      if ed.mw{'cy'} + 0 > ed.nl then ed.mw{'cy'} = ed.nl
      if ed.mw{'cy'} + 0 < 1 then ed.mw{'cy'} = 1
      ed.mw{'cx'} = 1
      ed.mw{'want'} = 1
      ed.mw{'left'} = 1
   end
   gosub redraw.editor
   return

* In: fp.line, fp.pat (not null), fp.from (the column to start at).  Out: fp.at, the column of the first match from there (0: none).  Honours fo.case (match case: on as delivered, which is how
*   Replace has always compared) and fo.word (a match must not touch a word character: a letter, a digit, . _ $ or %).  The text area's own Find (Ctrl-F) ignores case and is not changed.
find.in.line:
   fp.at = 0
   fp.hay = fp.line
   fp.nee = fp.pat
   if not(fo.case) then
      fp.hay = upcase(fp.hay)
      fp.nee = upcase(fp.nee)
   end
   fp.ln = len(fp.nee)
   if fp.ln > FINDMAX then return               ;* INDEX would cut the text at 256 and "find" what is not there; the commands refuse such a text at the prompt, this keeps any other caller safe
   fp.p = fp.from
   fp.hl = len(fp.hay)
   loop
      fp.i = index(fp.hay[fp.p, fp.hl], fp.nee, 1)
      if fp.i = 0 then exit
      fp.s = fp.p + fp.i - 1
      fp.good = @true
      if fo.word then
         if fp.s > 1 then
            if convert(WORDCH, '', fp.hay[fp.s - 1, 1]) = '' then fp.good = @false
         end
         fp.c = fp.hay[fp.s + fp.ln, 1]
         if fp.c # '' then
            if convert(WORDCH, '', fp.c) = '' then fp.good = @false
         end
      end
      if fp.good then
         fp.at = fp.s
         exit
      end
      fp.p = fp.s + 1
   repeat
   return

* In: rl.line, rl.old, rl.new.  Out: rl.out (the line with every match replaced) and rl.n (how many)
replace.line:
   rl.out = ''
   rl.from = 1
   rl.n = 0
   loop
      fp.line = rl.line
      fp.pat = rl.old
      fp.from = rl.from
      gosub find.in.line
      if not(fp.at) then exit
      rl.out := rl.line[rl.from, fp.at - rl.from] : rl.new
      rl.from = fp.at + len(rl.old)
      rl.n += 1
   repeat
   rl.out := rl.line[rl.from, len(rl.line)]         ;* the rest of the line, whatever its length (29 Oct 26: it was cut at 99,999 characters)
   return

* Ctrl-R, as SED's: a text is replaced by another all through the record in the editor, as ONE undo step (29 Oct 26: it was set through load.current, which forgot the undo steps).  The
* find options (Find options) apply; with the delivered ones (match case, any word) the whole text is changed at once.
do.replace:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   screen ask sysmsg(12237) returning rp.old else return
   if rp.old = '' then return
   if len(rp.old) > FINDMAX then
      msg = sysmsg(12557, FINDMAX)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(12238) returning rp.new else return
   gosub ed.source
   if fo.case and not(fo.word) then
      rp.n = count(ed.src, rp.old)
      ed.new = change(ed.src, rp.old, rp.new)
   end else
      rp.n = 0
      ed.new = ''
      rp.nl = dcount(ed.src, @fm)
      for rp.j = 1 to rp.nl
         rl.line = ed.src<rp.j>
         rl.old = rp.old
         rl.new = rp.new
         gosub replace.line
         ed.new<rp.j> = rl.out
         rp.n += rl.n
      next rp.j
   end
   if rp.n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12239, 0)
      return
   end
   gosub ed.shown
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   ed.cy = 0
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(12239, rp.n)
   return

* 29 Oct 26 (review R4).  In: ed.t (the live text).  Out: ed.src, the text Replace and Query replace work on, and ed.tok.  A tab that shows the token form (a data record, a dictionary item, a
*   VOC record) is changed as the RECORD it stands for, so that a colon in a value is a colon and a mark's token (:@vm:) is never text to be found or changed; the record is shown again by
*   ed.shown.  Any other tab is changed as the text it is.  The lines are the record's fields one to one either way, so a line number is the same in both.
ed.source:
   ed.src = ed.t
   ed.kk = 'm' : bu<cb>
   ed.tok = bx{ed.kk}
   if ed.tok then call $recarr('fromtext', ed.t, ed.src, ed.ok2)
   return

* In: ed.new, the changed record (ed.source's); ed.tok.  Out: ed.new as the editor shows it, with ed.ok true.  The token form is refused for a record in which a field would be just the text of a
*   mark token (it would be read back as a mark): ed.ok is false, msg says so (12997) and the text is left as it was.
ed.shown:
   ed.ok = @true
   if ed.tok then
      call $recarr('totext', ed.new, ed.disp, ed.ok2)
      if ed.ok2 then
         ed.new = ed.disp
      end else
         msg = sysmsg(12239, 0) : '. ' : sysmsg(12997, ed.disp)        ;* "0 replacement(s)": 12997 alone says why the token form cannot show the text, not that nothing was replaced (review of the fixes, TF3)
         ed.ok = @false
      end
   end
   return

* Search / Query replace: each match is asked about - Yes, No, All (the rest without asking) or Quit - with the line it is in; the answers are collected and the text is set ONCE at the end
*   (one undo step); a Quit keeps what was answered Yes before it.
do.qreplace:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   screen ask sysmsg(12237) returning qr.old else return
   if qr.old = '' then return
   if len(qr.old) > FINDMAX then
      msg = sysmsg(12557, FINDMAX)
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(12238) returning qr.new else return
   gosub ed.source
   ed.new = ed.src
   qr.nl = dcount(ed.src, @fm)
   qr.yes = 0
   qr.no = 0
   qr.all = @false
   qr.quit = @false
   qr.last = 0
   for qr.j = 1 to qr.nl
      qr.line = ed.src<qr.j>
      qr.shown = ed.t<qr.j>                          ;* the line as the person sees it (the token form shows a mark as :@vm:); the matches are found in qr.line
      qr.out = ''
      qr.from = 1
      loop
         fp.line = qr.line
         fp.pat = qr.old
         fp.from = qr.from
         gosub find.in.line
      until not(fp.at) or qr.quit
         qr.take = qr.all
         if not(qr.all) then
            qr.body = sysmsg(12551, qr.old, qr.new, qr.j) : @vm : qr.shown
            screen msgbox qr.body buttons 'Yes,No,All,Quit' default 1 returning qr.b else qr.b = 4
            last.status = char(1)
            begin case
               case qr.b = 1
                  qr.take = @true
               case qr.b = 3
                  qr.take = @true
                  qr.all = @true
               case qr.b = 4
                  qr.quit = @true
            end case
         end
         if not(qr.quit) then
            qr.out := qr.line[qr.from, fp.at - qr.from]
            if qr.take then
               qr.out := qr.new
               qr.yes += 1
               qr.last = qr.j
            end else
               qr.out := qr.line[fp.at, len(qr.old)]
               qr.no += 1
            end
            qr.from = fp.at + len(qr.old)
         end
      repeat
      qr.out := qr.line[qr.from, len(qr.line)]
      ed.new<qr.j> = qr.out
   until qr.quit
   next qr.j
   if qr.yes = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12552, 0, qr.no)
      return
   end
   gosub ed.shown
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   ed.cy = qr.last
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(12552, qr.yes, qr.no)
   return

* Search / List matching lines (SED's VIEW): the lines of this record that hold a text go in the table at the bottom, each with its number; Return on one goes to it.  The find options apply.
do.view:
   gosub ed.read
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   screen ask sysmsg(12548) returning vw.pat else return
   if vw.pat = '' then return
   if len(vw.pat) > FINDMAX then
      msg = sysmsg(12557, FINDMAX)
      return
   end
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   vw.n = 0
   vw.nl = dcount(ed.t, @fm)
   for vw.j = 1 to vw.nl
      fp.line = ed.t<vw.j>
      fp.pat = vw.pat
      fp.from = 1
      gosub find.in.line
      if fp.at then
         vw.n += 1
         err.rows<-1> = vw.j : @vm : trimf(trimb(fp.line))
         err.lines<-1> = vw.j
      end
   until vw.n >= MAXHIT
   next vw.j
   if vw.n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12550, vw.pat)
      return
   end
   out.kind = 'view'
   gosub zoom.leave                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1)
   out.file = ''
   out.dict = ''
   gosub err.load
   msg = sysmsg(12549, vw.n, vw.pat)
   return

* Search / Find options: C = match case, W = whole words; the answer sets both (a blank answer is neither)
do.findopts:
   screen ask sysmsg(12553) returning fo.text else return
   fo.text = upcase(fo.text)
   fo.case = (index(fo.text, 'C', 1) > 0)
   fo.word = (index(fo.text, 'W', 1) > 0)
   msg = sysmsg(12554, (if fo.case then 'on' else 'off'), (if fo.word then 'on' else 'off'))
   return

* In: ed.mw.  Out: sr.ok and the selection's range sr.y1 sr.x1 sr.y2 sr.x2 (the earlier end first; the character under the later end is not in it), false when there is no mark or nothing between
sel.range:
   sr.ok = @false
   sr.my = ed.mw{'my'} + 0
   sr.mx = ed.mw{'mx'} + 0
   sr.cy = ed.mw{'cy'} + 0
   sr.cx = ed.mw{'cx'} + 0
   if sr.my < 1 or sr.mx < 1 then return
   if sr.my < sr.cy or (sr.my = sr.cy and sr.mx <= sr.cx) then
      sr.y1 = sr.my ; sr.x1 = sr.mx ; sr.y2 = sr.cy ; sr.x2 = sr.cx
   end else
      sr.y1 = sr.cy ; sr.x1 = sr.cx ; sr.y2 = sr.my ; sr.x2 = sr.mx
   end
   if sr.y1 = sr.y2 and sr.x1 = sr.x2 then return
   sr.ok = @true
   return

* Search / Upcase the selection and Downcase the selection (dc.up: 1 or 0): the characters between the mark (Ctrl-G) and the cursor change case; one undo step
do.case:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   gosub sel.range
   if not(sr.ok) then
      msg = sysmsg(12556)
      return
   end
   ed.new = ed.t
   for dc.j = sr.y1 to sr.y2
      dc.ln = ed.t<dc.j>
      dc.a = 1
      dc.z = len(dc.ln)
      if dc.j = sr.y1 then dc.a = sr.x1
      if dc.j = sr.y2 then dc.z = sr.x2 - 1
      dc.part = dc.ln[dc.a, dc.z - dc.a + 1]
      if dc.up then dc.part = upcase(dc.part) else dc.part = downcase(dc.part)
      ed.new<dc.j> = dc.ln[1, dc.a - 1] : dc.part : dc.ln[dc.z + 1, len(dc.ln)]
   next dc.j
   ed.cy = 0
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(12555, sr.y1, sr.y2, (if dc.up then 'upper' else 'lower'))
   return

* Search / Export the selection: the form names a record that is not there yet (as Write a copy does), and the selection is written to it
do.export:
   gosub ed.read
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   gosub sel.range
   if not(sr.ok) then
      msg = sysmsg(12556)
      return
   end
   xp.res = ''
   call $tk_ai('select', ed.t, sr.my : @vm : sr.mx : @vm : sr.cy : @vm : sr.cx, xp.res)
   wa.text = change(xp.res<1>, char(10), @fm)
   * 29 Oct 26 (review R7): a tab that shows the token form writes the RECORD, as Write a copy does: a whole token in the selection (:@vm:) becomes the mark; half of one stays the text it is
   ed.kk = 'm' : bu<cb>
   if bx{ed.kk} then
      * Only whole lines: a cut that starts or ends inside a line could take half of a token (or text that only looks like one, a literal @vm next to the cut) and make a mark of it; that is
      *   written as the text shown.  (Review of the fixes, TF2.)
      xp.last = ed.t<sr.y2>
      if sr.x1 = 1 and sr.x2 > len(xp.last) then
         call $recarr('fromtext', wa.text, ex.rec, ed.ok2)
         wa.text = ex.rec
      end
   end
   gosub default.file
   wa.file = df
   wa.id = ''
   call $ide_name('copy', wa.file, wa.id, wa.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if wa.res # 'ok' then return
   tf.dict = ''
   tf.file = wa.file
   tf.id = wa.id
   gosub tab.find
   if tf.p then
      msg = sysmsg(12696, wa.file, wa.id)
      return
   end
   gosub write.copy
   return

* Search / Import a record here: the form names a record that exists, and its lines go in at the cursor (one undo step).  A record that holds value, subvalue or text marks or bytes 128 to 247
*   is refused (it would put what the editor cannot keep into this text); so is a collection file's and an operating system file's.
do.import:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   gosub default.file
   im.file = df
   im.id = ''
   call $ide_name('open', im.file, im.id, im.res)
   last.status = char(1)
   if im.res # 'ok' then return
   if im.file = 'path:' then
      * 30 Oct 26 (stage E2): the text of an operating system file.  A file that a tab of this IDE holds is imported from the TAB's text (opening it again and closing that variable would give away
      *   the lock the tab keeps); any other is opened and read through !ide_osfile 'open', which asks the account's gates, and closed again at once.
      tf.dict = ''
      tf.file = 'path:'
      tf.id = im.id
      gosub tab.find
      if tf.p then
         im.k = 't' : bu<tf.p>
         im.x = bx{im.k}
         if tf.p = cb then im.x = ed.t                  ;* the tab in the editor: the live text (ed.get read it), not what stash.current has not been asked to store
      end else
         call !ide_osfile('open', im.id, im.f, im.x, im.info, im.why)
         if im.why # '' then
            op.why = im.why
            op.path = im.id
            op.info = im.info
            gosub path.refusal
            return
         end
         close im.f
      end
   end else
      open im.file to fv else
         msg = sysmsg(12205, im.file)
         return
      end
      if fileinfo(fv, FL$COLLECTION) then
         msg = sysmsg(12562, im.file)              ;* 29 Oct 26 (review R7): its own words (12703 is about Find in the records)
         return
      end
      read im.x from fv, im.id else
         msg = sysmsg(12206, im.id, im.file)
         return
      end
   end
   if index(im.x, @vm, 1) or index(im.x, @sm, 1) or index(im.x, @tm, 1) or convert(NA.CHARS, '', im.x) # im.x then
      msg = sysmsg(12564, im.file, im.id)
      return
   end
   im.cy = ed.mw{'cy'} + 0
   im.cx = ed.mw{'cx'} + 0
   if im.cy < 1 then im.cy = 1
   im.ln = ed.t<im.cy>
   im.n = dcount(im.x, @fm)
   if im.x = '' then im.n = 0
   if im.n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12558, 0, im.file, im.id)
      return
   end
   if im.cx < 1 then im.cx = 1
   im.block = im.ln[1, im.cx - 1] : im.x
   im.block := im.ln[im.cx, len(im.ln)]
   ed.new = ed.t
   ed.new<im.cy> = im.block
   ed.cy = im.cy
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(12558, im.n, im.file, im.id)
   return

* Build / Run a command (SED's XEQ): a command of the account's TCL is run as the prompt would run it - through the same command processor, so its gates are the prompt's - with its
*   output captured into the table at the bottom.  The changed texts' copies are kept first (a command may end the session), and the locks the tabs hold are taken again after it (a
*   bare RELEASE in it lets them go).  A command that asks for input from the keyboard is not for this (its prompt is captured, not shown), and neither is one that changes the account (LOGTO):
*   the IDE goes on in the account the command left (29 Oct 26, review R8: said, not guarded - a first-word test is not a wall, and a paragraph can do the same).
*   TRAPPING ABORTS (29 Oct 26, review R2): a command that aborts - a program with a run-time error, ABORT, QUIT - is stopped as Run and Debug's are; without it the abort passed up and
*   cast the IDE off with every unsaved tab.  What it printed before the abort is thrown away (kernel.c), so the table says it printed nothing.
do.xeq:
   screen ask sysmsg(12559) history ih.cmds returning xq.cmd else return
   xq.cmd = trimf(trimb(xq.cmd))
   if xq.cmd = '' then return
   hp.item = xq.cmd
   gosub hist.command
   gosub rc.tick
   xq.out = ''
   execute xq.cmd trapping aborts capturing xq.out
   gosub relock.all
   last.status = char(1)
   err.rows = ''
   err.lines = ''
   xq.n = 0
   xq.text = convert(char(13), '', xq.out)
   xq.nl = dcount(xq.text, @fm)
   for xq.j = 1 to xq.nl
      xq.ln = xq.text<xq.j>
      if trimb(xq.ln) # '' then
         xq.n += 1
         err.rows<-1> = xq.n : @vm : xq.ln
         err.lines<-1> = 0
      end
   until xq.n >= MAXHIT
   next xq.j
   if xq.n = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12561, xq.cmd)
      return
   end
   out.kind = 'xeq'
   gosub zoom.leave                     ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 1)
   out.file = ''
   out.dict = ''
   gosub err.load
   msg = sysmsg(12560, xq.n, xq.cmd)
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3) - Build > Run a command at the terminal: SED's command line gave the screen to the command and asked for Return after it (sed:8238-8250), so an
*   interactive or full-screen command can run.  The same hand-over as Run and the Tools pages: the text in the editor is kept with its tab (stash.current), the recovery
*   copies are kept and the screen and the break key are given back (leave.screen), the command runs with its aborts trapped, and "Press Return", the screen, the break key,
*   the form and the locks the tabs hold come back (retake.screen).  The command goes through EXECUTE as do.xeq's does, so it is the prompt's command processor that
*   runs it and every gate it asks applies exactly as when it is typed at the prompt; nothing here widens what the account may run.  An open tab's text is not read
*   again: a record the command changed is shown as the tab had it (the overwrite question asks at the next save).
do.xeqterm:
   screen ask sysmsg(15053) history ih.cmds returning xm.cmd else return
   xm.cmd = trimf(trimb(xm.cmd))
   if xm.cmd = '' then return
   hp.item = xm.cmd
   gosub hist.command
   gosub stash.current
   gosub leave.screen
   execute xm.cmd trapping aborts
   gosub retake.screen
   msg = sysmsg(15054, xm.cmd)                   ;* Back from "%1": the tabs are as they were ...
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3) - View > List of tabs: SED's list of buffers (sed:6809).  One row a tab in $sl_pickwin (typing narrows the list): its number, file, record, *
*   when it has changes, and its lines.  Enter goes to the tab; Esc leaves everything as it is.  Nothing is read from the disk: the rows are what the tabs hold.
do.tablist:
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   gosub stash.current                           ;* the tab in the editor: its text as it is now, for the changed mark and the count
   tq.items = ''
   for i = 1 to nbuf
      gosub buf.label
      gosub is.dirty
      k = 't' : bu<i>
      tq.n = dcount(bx{k}, @fm)
      tq.items<i, 1> = i
      tq.items<i, 2> = bf.name : @sm : bf.id : @sm : (if dirty then '*' else '') : @sm : tq.n
   next i
   tq.cols = change(sysmsg(15052), '|', @vm) : @fm : 4 : @vm : 24 : @vm : 44 : @vm : 7 : @vm : 6
   call $sl_pickwin(tq.items, sysmsg(15051), 0, cb, tq.ret, tq.st, '', '', '', tq.filt, tq.cols, tq.cur)
   last.status = char(1)
   if tq.st = 0 then
      new.cb = tq.ret + 0
      if new.cb >= 1 and new.cb <= nbuf and new.cb # cb then gosub switch.to
      ft.id = 'ed'
      gosub focus.to
   end
   return

* Tools / a development page (3 Nov 26, S.75 DV10; Opus decision 12c): the page is a full-screen program of its own, so it is given the TERMINAL as Run gives it a program -
*   the text in the editor is kept with its tab, the recovery copies are kept (the page may end the session), the screen and the break key are given back (leave.screen),
*   the verb runs with its aborts trapped, and the screen, the break key, the form and the locks the tabs hold are taken back (retake.screen, with Run's pause, so that what a
*   page printed when it refused to open can be read).  NOT do.xeq: that captures what is printed, and a full-screen page writes to the terminal.  Only these seven verbs;
*   each page asks its own gates, exactly as when its verb is typed at the prompt.  An open tab's text is not read again: a record the page changed is shown as the tab had it.
do.tool:
   tl.verb = cmd
   if index(' tool.files tool.dict tool.voc tool.query tool.bulk tool.catalog tool.test ', ' ' : tl.verb : ' ', 1) = 0 then return
   gosub stash.current
   gosub leave.screen
   execute tl.verb trapping aborts
   gosub retake.screen
   msg = sysmsg(15005, tl.verb)
   return

* View / Refresh: the screen is drawn again from what the IDE holds (a program or the terminal may have written over it)
do.refresh:
   screen refresh
   last.status = char(1)
   last.foot = ''
   return

* Go / Go to line, which takes more than a line number (29 Oct 26): N a line, N,C a line and a column, and F,V,S - in a record shown in the token form - a field (line), the V-th value in it
*   (between :@vm:) and the S-th subvalue of that (between :@sm:).  A number out of range goes to the nearest.
* ======================================================================
* S.88 D2 (29 Oct 26): the editor's modes and Format.  Overwrite and auto-indent are the text area's opt-ins (w{'over'}, w{'autoindent'}), the tab width is its tabindent; the IDE keeps
* the three in ed.over, ed.auto and ed.tab so that a form made again (a resize) is given them.  None of them changes a record: Save is the person's act.

* View > Overwrite mode (Ins in the area does the same): a typed character replaces the one under the cursor
* 8 Nov 26 (S.180): an Edit menu entry - ek.key, the key it names - given to the fields pane when it has the focus, else to the editor's text area
do.editkey:
   if focus.id = 'fld' then
      fz.key = ek.key
      gosub fields.key
      return
   end
   if nbuf = 0 then return
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ek.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   call $sl_scr('get', ek.scr)
   call $sl_memo('key', ek.mw, ek.scr, ek.key, ek.r)
   gosub redraw.editor
   return

do.overwrite:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ed.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ed.over = (if ed.mw{'over'} + 0 then 0 else 1)
   ed.mw{'over'} = ed.over
   msg = (if ed.over then sysmsg(12772) else sysmsg(12773))
   return

do.autoindent:
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ed.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ed.auto = (if ed.auto then 0 else 1)
   ed.mw{'autoindent'} = ed.auto
   msg = (if ed.auto then sysmsg(12788) else sysmsg(12789))
   return

* The width Tab and Shift-Tab indent by, and Format indents with: a whole number from 1 to 8; anything else leaves it as it was
do.tabwidth:
   screen ask sysmsg(12774) width 4 value ed.tab returning tw.text else return
   tw.text = trim(tw.text)
   if tw.text = '' or not(num(tw.text)) then return
   tw.n = int(tw.text + 0)
   if tw.n < 1 or tw.n > 99 then return            ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3): 1 to 99, as SED's TABS (it was 1 to 8)
   ed.tab = tw.n
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   ed.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   ed.mw{'tabindent'} = ed.tab
   msg = sysmsg(12790, ed.tab)
   return

* ======================================================================
* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3) - SED's small commands, for the IDE (the owner, 9 Nov: SED is retired once the IDE has all its features).  Each changes the text through ed.set as ONE
*   undo step and saves nothing, or only looks.
*   do.stamp       Search > Stamp a history line: '* Updated by <who> (<login name>) <time and date>' above the cursor's line, as SED's STAMP
*   do.expandtabs  Search > Expand tabs: every tab character becomes blanks to the next tab stop (the tab width), as SED's EXPAND.TABS
*   do.charcode    View > Character code (Alt-=): the code of the character at the cursor and where it is in the record's fields, as SED's expand char
*   do.viewswitch  View > Syntax colour, View > Line numbers: switched off or on for every tab (SED's SYNTAX OFF / ON and LNUM OFF / ON); View > Mouse (stage 3b)
*   read.options   the account's &ide.options& (stage 3b)

do.stamp:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   st.y = ed.mw{'cy'} + 0
   if st.y < 1 then st.y = 1
   ed.new = ed.t
   ins '* Updated by ' : @who : ' (' : @logname : ') ' : timedate() before ed.new<st.y>
   ed.cy = st.y + 1
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(15027, st.y)
   return

do.expandtabs:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   if index(ed.t, char(9), 1) = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(15029)
      return
   end
   ed.new = ed.t
   xt.n = 0
   xt.w = ed.tab + 0
   if xt.w < 1 then xt.w = 3
   xt.lines = dcount(ed.new, @fm)
   for xt.i = 1 to xt.lines
      xt.ln = ed.new<xt.i>
      xt.j = index(xt.ln, char(9), 1)
      if xt.j then
         xt.n += 1
         loop
            xt.k = xt.w - mod(xt.j - 1, xt.w)
            xt.ln = xt.ln[1, xt.j - 1] : space(xt.k) : xt.ln[xt.j + 1, len(xt.ln)]
            xt.j = index(xt.ln, char(9), 1)
         while xt.j
         repeat
         ed.new<xt.i> = xt.ln
      end
   next xt.i
   ed.cy = 0
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(15028, xt.n, xt.w)
   return

do.charcode:
   gosub ed.read
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   cc.y = ed.mw{'cy'} + 0
   cc.x = ed.mw{'cx'} + 0
   cc.ln = ed.t<cc.y>
   if cc.x > len(cc.ln) then
      msg = sysmsg(15031, cc.y, cc.x)
      return
   end
   cc.n = seq(cc.ln[cc.x, 1])
   cc.name = ''
   cc.names = 'NULSOHSTXETXEOTENQACKBELBS HT LF VT FF CR SO SI DLEDC1DC2DC3DC4NAKSYNETBCANEM SUBESCFS GS RS US '      ;* SED's ctrl.char.names
   if cc.n < 32 then cc.name = trimb(cc.names[(cc.n * 3) + 1, 3])
   if cc.n = 127 then cc.name = 'DEL'
   if cc.n >= 32 and cc.n < 127 then cc.name = "'" : char(cc.n) : "'"
   cc.pre = cc.ln[1, cc.x - 1]
   cc.v = count(cc.pre, @vm) + 1
   cc.s = count(field(cc.pre, @vm, cc.v), @sm) + 1
   msg = sysmsg(15030, cc.y, cc.x, cc.n, cc.name) : sysmsg(15036, cc.y, cc.v, cc.s)
   return

do.viewswitch:
   if cmd = 'mouseswitch' then
      * View > Mouse (S.184 stage 3b): the terminal stops or starts reporting the mouse.  S.186: the switch is the session's, kept by $tk_std and shared with every
      *   full-screen page, so a mouse switched off in the IDE stays off on the pages and the reverse; the keyboard does everything a click does
      call $tk_std('mouse', '', '', '', ms.r)
      msg = ms.r<2>
      return
   end
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   vs.mw = f{'widgets', en}
   if cmd = 'synswitch' then
      syn.off = not(syn.off)
      vs.mw{'synlang'} = (if syn.off then 0 else vs.mw{'syn.want'} + 0)
      msg = (if syn.off then sysmsg(15033) else sysmsg(15032))
   end else
      lnum.off = not(lnum.off)
      vs.mw{'lnum'} = (if lnum.off then 0 else 1)
      msg = (if lnum.off then sysmsg(15035) else sysmsg(15034))
   end
   gosub redraw.editor
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.184 stage 3b): the account's defaults for the IDE, as SED took its own from &sed.options& - an X record &ide.options& in the VOC (&sed.options& when there is
*   none, so an account's existing record still counts).  Field 2 holds words: CASE_ON and CASE_OFF (Find options' match case), WORD and BWORD (whole words, a word being
*   letters, digits and . _ $ %), INDENT, OVERLAY, LNUM, NO.LNUM, SYNTAX, SYNTAX.LIGHT, NO.SYNTAX, MOUSE, NO.MOUSE, NO.CASE.EXPLORE (the tree's records in case-blind order),
*   TABS n (1 to 99); SCROLL1 is SED's paging and is passed over.  Field 5 is a tab width.  The key bindings of fields 3 and 4 are not read: the IDE has none (the owner,
*   9 Nov 26).  A word the IDE does not know is named on the status line; nothing in the record is ever written.
read.options:
   read ro.rec from @voc, '&ide.options&' else
      read ro.rec from @voc, '&sed.options&' else ro.rec = ''
   end
   if upcase(ro.rec[1, 1]) # 'X' then return
   ro.bad = ''
   ro.w = trim(upcase(ro.rec<2>))
   ro.n = dcount(ro.w, ' ')
   ro.i = 0
   loop
      ro.i += 1
   while ro.i <= ro.n
      ro.t = field(ro.w, ' ', ro.i)
      begin case
         case ro.t = 'CASE_ON'
            fo.case = @true
         case ro.t = 'CASE_OFF'
            fo.case = @false
         case ro.t = 'WORD' or ro.t = 'BWORD'
            fo.word = @true
         case ro.t = 'INDENT'
            ed.auto = 1
         case ro.t = 'OVERLAY'
            ed.over = 1
         case ro.t = 'LNUM'
            lnum.off = 0
         case ro.t = 'NO.LNUM'
            lnum.off = 1
         case ro.t = 'SYNTAX' or ro.t = 'SYNTAX.LIGHT'
            syn.off = 0
         case ro.t = 'NO.SYNTAX'
            syn.off = 1
         case ro.t = 'MOUSE'
            mouse.off = @false
         case ro.t = 'NO.MOUSE'
            mouse.off = @true
         case ro.t = 'NO.CASE.EXPLORE'
            ex.nocase = @true
         case ro.t = 'SCROLL1'
            null
         case ro.t = 'TABS'
            ro.i += 1
            ro.v = field(ro.w, ' ', ro.i)
            if ro.v matches '1-2N' and ro.v > 0 then ed.tab = ro.v + 0 else ro.bad<1, -1> = 'TABS ' : ro.v
         case 1
            ro.bad<1, -1> = ro.t
      end case
   repeat
   ro.v = trim(ro.rec<5>)
   if ro.v matches '1-2N' and ro.v > 0 then ed.tab = ro.v + 0
   if ro.bad # '' then msg = sysmsg(15037, convert(@vm, ' ', ro.bad))
   return

* Build > Format the program: !format() (the FORMAT verb's routine) lays the program in the tab out again with the tab width as its indent, in QUIET mode (the named common /fmtq/: no dots,
* no error display, no key asked for) and the IDE says what it found.  Only a program (kind source); the text is changed through ed.set as ONE undo step and nothing is saved.
* A program with a problem (fq.log is not empty after a format with errors) is left as it is.
common /fmtq/ fq.quiet, fq.log
do.format:
   gosub ed.get
   if not(ed.ok) then return
   k = 'k' : bu<cb>
   if bx{k} # 'source' then
      msg = sysmsg(12784)
      return
   end
   fm.res = ''
   fm.err = 0
   fq.quiet = 1
   call !format(ed.t, fm.res, bn<cb>, ed.tab, @false, fm.err)
   fq.quiet = 0
   if fm.err > 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12785, fm.err, field(fq.log, @fm, 1))
      return
   end
   if (fm.res : '|') = (ed.t : '|') then
      msg = sysmsg(12786)
      return
   end
   ed.new = fm.res
   ed.cy = 0
   gosub ed.set
   msg = sysmsg(12787, ed.tab)
   return

do.goto:
   if nbuf = 0 then
      msg = sysmsg(12223)
      return
   end
   gosub fields.is.on
   if fv.on then
      msg = sysmsg(15998)               ;* 30 Oct 26 (stage 2b): it moves the cursor of the text area, which is hidden while the tab shows its fields
      return
   end
   screen ask sysmsg(12214) returning gl.text else return
   gl.text = trim(gl.text)
   gl.k = dcount(gl.text, ',')
   if gl.text = '' or gl.k > 3 then return
   gl.p = ''                                    ;* 29 Oct 26 (review R1): an element of a variable that has no value stops the program; it was the first Go to line's end
   for gl.i = 1 to gl.k
      gl.v = trim(field(gl.text, ',', gl.i))
      if gl.v = '' then return
      if not(num(gl.v)) then return
      gl.v = int(gl.v + 0)                      ;* 1.5 is 1: a line and a column are whole numbers
      if gl.v < 1 then return
      if gl.v > 99999999 then gl.v = 99999999   ;* an INDEX occurrence is a 32-bit number: 3000000000 stopped the program with "Numeric overflow" (review of the fixes, RT1)
      gl.p<gl.i> = gl.v
   next gl.i
   gl.n = gl.p<1> + 0
   gosub goto.line
   if gl.k = 1 then return
   call $sl_form('find', f, '', 'ed', en)
   mw = f{'widgets', en}
   gl.ln = mw{'value'}<mw{'cy'} + 0>
   gl.lo = downcase(gl.ln)                      ;* the tokens are found in any case (reading accepts :@VM:); the length is the same, so a position in one is a position in the other
   gl.col = gl.p<2> + 0
   if gl.k = 3 then
      gl.from = 1
      if gl.p<2> + 0 > 1 then
         gl.q = index(gl.lo, ':@vm:', gl.p<2> + 0 - 1)
         if gl.q = 0 then gl.q = index(gl.lo, ':@vm:', dcount(gl.lo, ':@vm:') - 1)
         if gl.q > 0 then gl.from = gl.q + 5
      end
      gl.col = gl.from
      if gl.p<3> + 0 > 1 then
         gl.seg = gl.lo[gl.from, len(gl.lo)]
         gl.e = index(gl.seg, ':@vm:', 1)
         if gl.e > 0 then gl.seg = gl.seg[1, gl.e - 1]
         gl.q = index(gl.seg, ':@sm:', gl.p<3> + 0 - 1)
         if gl.q = 0 then gl.q = index(gl.seg, ':@sm:', dcount(gl.seg, ':@sm:') - 1)   ;* a subvalue out of range goes to the last one, as a value does
         if gl.q > 0 then gl.col = gl.from + gl.q + 4
      end
   end
   if gl.col > len(gl.ln) + 1 then gl.col = len(gl.ln) + 1
   mw{'cx'} = gl.col
   mw{'want'} = gl.col
   gl.w = mw{'w'} + 0
   mw{'left'} = 1
   if gl.col > gl.w - 6 then mw{'left'} = gl.col - int(gl.w / 2)
   gosub redraw.editor
   return

end

